WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.660
Mobile Task Force Edna-84, also known by the
code name “And Thus Upon His Crucible”,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.370
is on one of the strangest missions they’ve
ever been sent on.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:14.080
They sit in a darkened room, beads of sweat
trickling down their brows.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.540
The lights of computer monitors shine up into
their faces.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:23.790
They’re tackling a monster that, if it escapes,
will literally devour all of creation.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:30.430
It has no fear, it has no remorse, and if
it isn’t kept contained by Edna-84, it may

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.660
escape and wreak havoc across the world.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.340
Oh, and did we mention it’s trapped inside
a Minecraft server?

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:45.220
You heard us right: SCP-4335 is a cognitohazardous,
extradimensional being of pure terror, trapped

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.430
inside a procedurally generated world in the
immensely popular game Minecraft.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.109
You hear about a lot of horrifying monsters
being described as Lovecraftian, but only

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:57.219
this monster is Minecraftian.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:02.770
There’s a lot that needs explaining here,
and we intend to get to all of it, but let’s

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.200
start with how the Foundation keeps this truly
exceptional beast contained.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.780
The Foundation first discovered SCP-4335 back
in 2010, shortly after the Alpha Version of

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.880
Minecraft was released.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.040
Because of the online nature of the game,
it’s proven to be impossible to contain

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.909
4335 externally, seeing as it isn’t confined
to any one physical location.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:31.650
Shutting off the server 4335 is found in is
not an effective containment method either,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.880
as this just causes the entity to simply hop
to a different server.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.399
The Foundation’s greatest fear is that 4335
will one day make the leap out of Minecraft

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:43.190
and cause chaos in our world.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:50.259
That’s why it’s imperative to draft Containment
Procedures for SCP-4335 within the game.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:56.070
This led to the creation of a containment
site unlike any other: Site-M1, the first

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.610
official Foundation Site made entirely out
of Minecraft blocks.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.450
Everyone who was originally involved in the
server has been removed and amnesthetized.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:10.310
Since then, elite SCP Foundation Containment
Specialists and Gamers have been constructing

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:13.450
the perfect prison for SCP-4335.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:18.810
Site-M1 is a large, stone complex built into
the side of a mountain.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.950
It has a number of key features - including
a supply area filled with materials vital

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.959
for 4335’s continued containment.

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:31.950
Chests filled with books that contain SCP-4335's
containment procedures.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.700
A few animal farms, for the purpose of breeding
and killing livestock for their meat.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.540
The entrance to a mine.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.700
Several chests containing books specifically
designed for civilians to learn about 4335,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.840
if they ever breach containment and enter
the server.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:54.170
And of course, the actual containment chamber
for SCP-4335 itself, which is a little more

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.599
complicated.

00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:01.790
4335’s containment chamber is built out
of several layers, all made from iron blocks.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:10.820
The outermost cube is 75 x 75 x 75 blocks,
the inner cube is 55 x 55 x 55 blocks, and

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:17.930
the innermost cube is 25 x 25 x 25 blocks,
creating several levels of defense.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.840
Finally one layer within all these others,
which contains the anomaly proper, is made

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.160
from obsidian blocks.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.640
SCP-4335 is bound into the center of the containment
chamber with a complex mechanism.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.400
The outermost cube is completely filled with
water, and several dispensers capable of rapidly

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.590
dispensing large amounts of items in a short
amount of time line the cube.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:44.569
The cube also contains several mob farms,
which are devices that constantly spawn enemies

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:47.230
into the chamber that drop loot when they
die.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:53.290
The chamber is essentially designed to funnel
a constant flow of items inwards to SCP-4335.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:04:01.030
There are even contingency measures for if
SCP-4335 manages to breach containment.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.920
If escape is attempted, blocks of TNT detonate
above the ceiling, causing lava to pour into

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.920
the chamber.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.790
At that point, MTF Edna-84 are dispatched
into the server to lure 4335 back into its

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.030
containment chamber.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:20.290
To do this, they use a mix of fire-resistance
potions and Ender Pearls - which have teleportation

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:21.290
capabilities.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:27.090
Interestingly, there’s one more basic method
of luring SCP-4335 back into the obsidian

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.170
cube: Taunting and insulting.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:37.070
One of SCP-4335’s many anomalous abilities
is being able to hear people through the screen,

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:42.350
and - being a proud creature - it often responds
to insults by charging in to directly engage

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.070
the insulter.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:50.470
If SCP-4335 still manages to breach the containment
that has been set up for it, then they expect

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.009
that it will likely soon hop into another
server.

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:57.920
At that point, the goal will shift to finding
the monster and recontaining it.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.690
With Containment Procedures this complex and
extensive, it shouldn’t come as a surprise

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:07.310
that SCP-4335 rests firmly in the Keter Class.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.260
What exactly is this anomalous entity?

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.289
Why does its containment hinge on constantly
providing it with items?

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:19.770
And how did it end up in Mojang’s popular
building and survival game in the first place?

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:26.140
SCP-4335, in terms of physical dimensions,
appears almost identical to the player character,

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.130
with an all-black skin.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:33.540
It also appears to be constantly shrouded
in a cloud of smoke particles, and has long,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.039
black tendrils protruding from its back.

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:42.260
In some respects, 4335 has been compared to
two popular creepypasta figures: Herobrine

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.910
and The Slenderman.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.819
But 4335 is far stranger.

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:52.840
Its physical body behaves similarly to most
assets native to the game, with a few peculiar

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.120
anomalous abilities we’ll discuss soon.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.350
Handling SCP-4335 is an extremely delicate
process.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.889
If command blocks, creative mode, or server
commands are ever enabled in a server with

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:14.310
SCP-4335, the server will instantly shut down,
and SCP-4335 will move to a different server.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:20.389
SCP-4335 also uses its tendrils to destroy
surrounding blocks before consuming them.

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:27.419
With each successful consumption, SCP-4335
grows, and when it reaches sufficient size,

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.270
it hops to a different server.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:35.419
As you can see, keeping SCP-4335 contained
is an uphill battle, but luckily there are

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:38.310
two factors on the Foundation’s side here.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:44.600
The first is that SCP-4335 is immobilized
while consuming items and blocks, limiting

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.060
its ability to actively escape Foundation
forces.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.660
The second factor is that SCP-4335 needs a
rest period between consuming blocks in order

00:06:53.660 --> 00:07:00.289
to grow, meaning, if its consumption is constant
and continuous it isn’t able to grow.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:04.360
These two factors have informed the entirety
of the Foundation’s Containment Procedures

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:11.910
around SCP-4335: It’s locked into its chamber
and fed items and blocks constantly, effectively

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.629
rooting it in place.

00:07:13.629 --> 00:07:19.599
When SCP-4335 begins to grow, the Foundation
also found that the application of Ender Pearls

00:07:19.599 --> 00:07:23.230
helps reduce it back to its normal size.

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:29.419
However, SCP-4335 does have a method of striking
back against its captors.

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:33.910
4335 is Vurgent-class multi-sensory cognitohazard.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:38.009
Anyone viewing it without proper training
and protection may experience distressing

00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:39.699
hallucinations.

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:46.590
SCP-4335 is also capable of telepathic speech
with people playing on its server, and - as

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:51.319
we alluded to earlier - it can also hear any
noises you make while playing.

00:07:51.319 --> 00:07:56.379
“Weird” is a term thrown around a lot
when it comes to SCPs, considering it’s

00:07:56.379 --> 00:07:59.909
pretty much a requirement for the Foundation
to take interest in you.

00:07:59.909 --> 00:08:03.949
But an all-devouring Minecraft demon that
can hear you talking through your screen is

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:07.630
strange even by Foundation standards.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:15.090
MTF Edna-84 first discovered SCP-4335 in the
single-player server of Minecraft user Leakingheart.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:20.590
Three team members - Jason Yelsan, Richard
Duchamp, and Sheila Freemason - covertly entered

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:24.090
the game to investigate and potentially apprehend
the creature.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.470
When Leakingheart first discovered their presence
on what he thought was a private server, he

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.080
quickly left, a little creeped out by the
sudden intrusion.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.779
Thankfully, the trio was still able to locate
SCP-4335.

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:41.669
They discovered the creature hiding inside
a giant crater, as though it had impacted

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.219
the earth at considerable speed.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:48.410
Richard Duchamp, who was the leader of the
team at the time, made the mistake of looking

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.050
directly at the entity.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:56.000
In that moment, he experienced the full force
of SCP-4335’s cognitohazardous effects.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:01.390
He hallucinated, believing that his keyboard
was melting before his eyes.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:06.500
In the aftermath of this incident, Duchamp
was taken off the case, and Jason Yelsan was

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.260
promoted to head of the project.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.430
Things were still going to get stranger.

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:16.250
Yelsan was able to open a dialogue with the
creature after containing it in a chamber

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:17.570
filled with lava.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:22.860
It asked him whether it had landed in the
right location - meaning, our world - and

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:27.560
Yelsan informed it that it’d somehow fallen
into the world of Minecraft instead.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:33.089
The creature was at first confused, and then
angry and resentful about its situation.

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:38.720
It vowed to find its way into our world somehow,
and obtain more “sustenance.”

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.339
A few months after this, the entity managed
to breach containment and hop into another

00:09:43.339 --> 00:09:44.339
server.

00:09:44.339 --> 00:09:49.180
Yelsan and two others once again managed to
track it down and recontain it, but this time,

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:54.380
two civilians also inhabiting the server were
exposed to the anomalous effects of SCP-4335.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:10:02.339
They weren’t hurt, but they did appear strange
and incoherent after experiencing 4335’s

00:10:02.339 --> 00:10:03.709
cognitohazards.

00:10:03.709 --> 00:10:08.830
The Foundation tracked them down in the aftermath
and gave them amnestic treatment.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:12.180
4335 was contained shortly thereafter.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.399
Once again, Yelsan came face to face with
his new foe.

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:22.430
Eight months after being captured, 4335 granted
Yelsan another interview from containment.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:27.730
4335 admitted that it almost respected Yelsan
and the rest of the Foundation for figuring

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.740
out how to capture and contain it so quickly.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:35.470
In exchange, it would give the Foundation
something extremely valuable: Information.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:41.830
First, it asked one question of Yelsan: How
does he define “creation?”

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:46.829
Yelsan replied, “Uh… something that is
built and brought to this universe by a sapient

00:10:46.829 --> 00:10:49.840
being, using other things from this universe?”

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.400
4335 agreed.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:56.330
It went on to explain that it came from a
universe devoid of creation.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:11:02.089
A dark and unknowable place, filled with nothing
but violent, random chaos.

00:11:02.089 --> 00:11:07.720
Its dimension existed directly above ours,
and it often looked down at us through a dimensional

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:11.420
window, fascinated by all the creation below.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:17.230
It plotted and dreamed to one day infiltrate
our reality, and Yelsan finally had the opportunity

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:20.700
to ask the magic question, “Why?”

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:25.190
Though he wasn’t quite ready for the brutal
honesty of SCP-4335’s reply.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:31.990
“I do not like to lie, so I will tell you
now.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:37.960
I wish to suck it dry of the toys of whatever
force controls your universe.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:44.570
Destroy the light, destroy the Earth, and
destroy humanity.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:46.579
It reminded me… of me.

00:11:46.579 --> 00:11:51.290
A blubbering mass of intelligence and order.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:56.529
It sickens me in ways I cannot comprehend.

00:11:56.529 --> 00:11:59.050
I hope you understand.”

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:04.550
SCP-4335 was a connoisseur of creation, and
it sought to devour all of it.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:10.260
In this moment, Yelsan realized what an incredibly
dangerous entity he was dealing with.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:15.480
The only mystery was why this creature had
somehow landed in Minecraft instead of our

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.410
world, which appeared to be the only thing
that saved us.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:26.029
But Yelsan didn’t have time to think: 4335
was about to stage another daring escape attempt.

00:12:26.029 --> 00:12:29.220
One of its tendrils reached out and attacked
Yelsan’s player character.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.950
In that moment, the real life Yelsan began
to hallucinate and panic.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:39.920
Suddenly, back in the game, a series of abnormally
tall, slender black figures appeared and began

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.040
deconstructing the containment chamber around
SCP-4335.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:49.920
It had somehow summoned new minions into the
game to assist in this containment breach.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.240
Jason Yelsan entered cognitohazard quarantine
following this incident, and was removed from

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:56.240
the project.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.910
And he wasn’t the only one affected by this
incident.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:04.050
Following the first appearance of these long,
dark figures, players across the globe began

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:07.810
to experience them appearing in their own
games.

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:12.540
The Foundation managed to find a solution:
They contacted Mojang and had the creatures

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:18.110
patched into the game during the next update,
as a new non-anomalous entity, which seemed

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:22.230
to stop 4335 from being able to use them as
its own tools.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:27.620
They’re now known as the Endermen, and are
beloved among fans for being one of the creepier

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.010
enemies.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:33.560
To this day, containment efforts continue
for SCP-4335.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:39.360
There’s only one question left: Why did
the entity fall into Minecraft rather than

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.480
our world?

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:43.449
The file posits the most likely answer.

00:13:43.449 --> 00:13:49.200
Because 4335 defines creation as elements
made by sapient beings.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:55.220
In Minecraft, the most popular game in the
world at the height of 4335’s power, everything

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:59.590
that exists is the product of code made by
humans.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:01.640
Creation is truly abundant there.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:10.120
As for our world, in SCP-4335’s extra-dimensional
eyes, there is no creation, no intelligent

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:13.220
design, no soothing piano soundtrack.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:18.310
Just frightening, chaotic, randomness, which
is too unlike its own dimension.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:24.420
So even if SCP-4335 ever did arrive in our
reality, it would likely be disappointed by

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:26.950
how little there is to eat.

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:30.040
You’re sitting in the middle of a cheering
crowd.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:35.740
The arena around you is electric, fans hollering
from their seats as they watch the basketball

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:36.800
game.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.850
Every point scored, every shot missed, and
every skillful pass of the ball sends waves

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:44.540
of excitement over everyone in the audience.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:47.510
Applause, shouts, and chants fill the air.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:52.540
It distracts you for a moment, you find yourself
swept up in the energy of the game.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:59.180
But slowly, surely, a nagging feeling creeps
up on you as your eyes follow the ball.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:03.970
That feeling turns to a sickening realization
as one of the players jumps and dunks the

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.720
basketball, scoring another two points.

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:12.000
You notice the look on his face, the same
look on the faces of all the other players

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.360
on the court: a solemn, hopeless expression.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:20.680
It’s the same look of silent despair worn
by all the spectators around you.

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:25.930
Then you remember, you’ve watched this exact
game of basketball before.

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:28.410
Many, many times before.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:34.821
And that is when you feel the same hopeless
expression appear on your own face, realizing

00:15:34.821 --> 00:15:41.339
just how long you have been here, watching
this game over and over.

00:15:41.339 --> 00:15:45.910
Reminded that you will never, ever leave this
arena.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:49.060
Welcome to SCP-1733.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:55.000
To the untrained eye, SCP-1733 seems to be
a completely harmless item.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.829
Stored in a digital video recorder, it is
kept secure in the dusty depths of the SCP

00:15:59.829 --> 00:16:01.850
Foundation’s video archives.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:08.110
If any researcher is given permission to study
SCP-1733, they will find it to be an ordinary

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.370
VHS tape.

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.399
But what about the contents of said tape?

00:16:11.399 --> 00:16:16.380
Does it contain a bizarre, disconnected series
of black and white video clips that when viewed

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:20.940
only give the watcher seven days before the
spirit of a drowned girl comes crawling out

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.040
their TV screen to kill them?

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.610
Well, not exactly, but we’re sure that if
such a tape exists, the Foundation probably

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:31.180
has it under lock and key too.

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:37.079
The footage on SCP-1733 is as seemingly banal
as the ordinary VHS tape itself.

00:16:37.079 --> 00:16:41.529
When played, the viewer will witness a TV
broadcast of a basketball game.

00:16:41.529 --> 00:16:48.000
Specifically, the 2010-2011 NBA season opener,
broadcast on television and captured on tape

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:49.940
by an unknown civilian.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:56.420
The game took place on the 26th of October
2010 in the TD Garden arena in Boston, Massachusetts,

00:16:56.420 --> 00:17:01.220
a game where the hometown Boston Celtics took
on the Miami Heat.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.660
By now, perhaps you are wondering what is
so special about this particular game, and

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:11.789
why the SCP Foundation would be so interested
in keeping a VHS recording of it so secure.

00:17:11.789 --> 00:17:14.549
Is one of the O5 Council secretly a Celtics
fan?

00:17:14.549 --> 00:17:19.260
While that’s impossible to say for sure,
what we do know for certain is that SCP-1733

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.810
is far more than just a harmless recording
of a basketball game.

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:30.309
The SCP Foundation first caught wind of SCP-1733’s
existence the day after the game.

00:17:30.309 --> 00:17:35.050
On October 27th, a Boston native that had
watched and recorded the game made comments

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:40.020
on social media about a technical foul that
the game’s referee had failed to pick up

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:41.020
on.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:45.669
According to this individual, during the third
quarter, an instance of unsportsmanlike conduct

00:17:45.669 --> 00:17:48.840
took place between Ray Allen and Chris Bosh.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:53.390
The person making these claims was quickly
ridiculed by commenters on the same thread.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:57.980
But then this person uploaded a clip of this
foul from the footage they had recorded.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.990
The other commenters were dumbfounded.

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:05.669
During the original broadcast, that foul had
never happened, but the recording showed it

00:18:05.669 --> 00:18:07.120
clear as day.

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:11.570
The footage was quickly expunged and all traces
of the clip removed from the Internet by personnel

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:14.660
acting on behalf of the SCP Foundation.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:19.070
Any that had viewed the video on Facebook,
or been a part of the comments debating this

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:23.940
previously non-existent foul, were tracked
down via their IP addresses.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:28.190
All of them, including the owner of the tape,
were given amnestics to wipe their memories

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:36.500
of the supposed infraction, and SCP-1733 was
returned to the Foundation for further analysis.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:42.809
Researchers at the Foundation were determined
to understand the anomalous nature of SCP-1733,

00:18:42.809 --> 00:18:48.570
and why the footage contained on this particular
VHS tape was so different from the basketball

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:49.820
game’s original broadcast.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:55.120
At first, the differences in the game seemed
to be only slight changes from the one that

00:18:55.120 --> 00:19:00.410
actually aired on TV; a different foul here,
a small point difference there.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:05.280
While the recording of the game contained
on SCP-1733 was slightly different from the

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:10.950
real-life game, perhaps even more interesting
was that these differences changed with repeat

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:12.610
viewings.

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:18.400
Researchers wouldn’t observe the same differences
every time they watched SCP-1733, but would

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.540
instead spot something different that had
been changed each time they restarted the

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:24.540
tape.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:30.230
And as they continued to watch and rewatch
SCP-1733’s footage, the true horror of the

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:34.510
video’s anomalous properties became more
and more apparent.

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:39.590
As the footage recorded on SCP-1733 began
to diverge more and more from the original

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:44.909
broadcast, researchers shifted their attention
away from the basketball game itself and onto

00:19:44.909 --> 00:19:46.309
the people watching it.

00:19:46.309 --> 00:19:51.610
Just like the specifics of the game itself,
the fans in the arena seemed to also change

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:53.820
with every re-watch of the tape.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:58.250
And even stranger, the people in the crowd
seemed to not only be aware of their existence

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:03.590
within the footage, but also retained memories
of every previous replayed version.

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:09.540
Each time the researchers had watched SCP-1733,
the audience trapped within had gained more

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:11.390
and more cognizance.

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:15.870
Every man, woman, and child in the audience
of that basketball game began remembering

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:22.630
that they had seen multiple variations of
the same game over and over again.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:27.240
This first became apparent to Foundation researchers
when the game’s commentators, two presenters

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:31.679
named Mike and Tommy, began to comment on
a strange sense of ‘déjà vu’ they were

00:20:31.679 --> 00:20:36.600
experiencing as they watched the Boston Celtics
and Miami Heat playing.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:41.360
Both seemed to share the notion that the game
they were witnessing was strangely familiar,

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:45.800
and in later replays of the tape, these two
commentator personalities were able to recall

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:49.070
the events of the game with perfect, vivid
accuracy.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:54.179
While they never address the viewer directly,
this cumulative effect seems to have extended

00:20:54.179 --> 00:20:58.860
to every person that was present within the
arena at the time that the original broadcast

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:00.559
was recorded.

00:21:00.559 --> 00:21:05.590
Everyone from the commentators to the coaches,
the players to the patrons, all remember the

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:10.390
diverging versions of the basketball game,
but seem to have no awareness that they are

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:14.279
trapped inside a recording on a VHS tape.

00:21:14.279 --> 00:21:19.130
It should be noted that it is still unclear
whether the entities are real people, or digital

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:22.279
copies that solely reside on the tape.

00:21:22.279 --> 00:21:28.730
Every individual shown to be inside TD Garden
on the 26th of October 2010, seems to be identical

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.400
to their counterparts in the real world.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:35.610
Each of the basketball players on both the
Boston Celtics and Miami Heat teams have the

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:40.850
exact same level of talent as they do in reality,
and their individual mannerisms are perfectly

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:44.330
recreated in the SCP-1733 footage.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:50.190
And the same true of the fans in the audience,
each one appearing to be, in every conceivable

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:52.250
way, a living human being.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:58.330
But the most unnerving aspect of SCP-1733
was found when Foundation agents were tasked

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:04.020
with tracking down the members of the audience
who were seen on the tape and they found…

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:05.020
nothing.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:09.230
Unlike the players and coaches who can quite
easily be found in our world, with many of

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:13.941
them still playing or involved with professional
basketball, personnel were unable to find

00:22:13.941 --> 00:22:17.440
a single trace of any of the people from the
audience.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:21.299
Not one clue as to their current whereabouts
or status.

00:22:21.299 --> 00:22:28.120
It is unclear how, but it appears that everyone
shown on SCP-1733, especially those watching

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:35.190
the game unfold from the stands, is a person
trapped in digital form, stuck watching variations

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:38.030
of the same match on a loop.

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:43.540
Researchers at the SCP Foundation who were
studying SCP-1733 initially theorized that

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:48.679
the tape had been designed to display an infinite
variety of different game outcomes.

00:22:48.679 --> 00:22:54.620
Given that the tactics utilized by the players
were different with each playback of SCP-1733,

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:59.020
this seemed to be the case, that the tape
was showing a ‘what if’ scenario each

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:02.460
time the tape was rewound and played from
the start.

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:05.260
But the scenario wasn’t resetting each time.

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.690
It was clear they were learning.

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:12.650
By the 34th replay of the footage, the Boston
Celtics and Miami Heat were so in-tune with

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:17.570
the opposing team’s playbook that both sides’
players were able to perfectly and precisely

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:23.040
counter their opponents’ moves, and kept
the score at zero to zero for much of the

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.040
game.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:28.960
At this stage in the Foundation’s research,
the players on the SCP-1733 tape had not yet

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:34.020
become fully aware that they had played the
same game already in earlier playbacks, but

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:40.630
it is possible that weak memories of the earlier
versions of the same match manifested as instinctive

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:44.240
ways players could counter the moves of their
rival team.

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:50.650
However, by the time Foundation researchers
had replayed the SCP-1733 footage 45 times,

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:55.809
the players, their coaches and fans watching
the game had become aware of what was happening

00:23:55.809 --> 00:23:56.809
to them.

00:23:56.809 --> 00:24:01.780
It was at this point that the digital recording
from TD Garden changed dramatically.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:05.721
Realizing that they’d been playing the same
game of basketball over and over, the players

00:24:05.721 --> 00:24:08.570
refused to participate any longer.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:13.000
Everyone in attendance began to attempt escape
from the tape where they’d been unknowingly

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.000
imprisoned.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:19.180
But not a single one of the stadium’s doors
would budge, and neither the fans, nor the

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:23.740
players, seemed to be able to leave through
any other exit out of TD Garden.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:29.980
Over the following playbacks of the SCP-1733
video tape, attempts at escape grew increasingly

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:30.980
daring.

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:33.230
In one playback, a full-scale riot broke out.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:37.680
In another, makeshift explosives were built
and used by those trapped to try and blast

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:39.649
their way to freedom.

00:24:39.649 --> 00:24:43.940
As their cognition grew and they began to
remember friends and family from outside the

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:49.929
video tape, the crowd trapped in SCP-1733
grew even more desperate.

00:24:49.929 --> 00:24:54.500
Players and their coaches retired to locker
rooms, withdrawing from the crowd for a time.

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:59.049
The rest of the people in the footage began
to form factions amongst themselves, one of

00:24:59.049 --> 00:25:02.590
the more prominent calling themselves the
“Faithkeepers”.

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:06.360
These individuals voiced their belief that
they had been confined to TD Garden as some

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:12.170
form of spiritual punishment, as a result
of the consumerism that is rife in a post-industrial

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:13.170
society.

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:17.480
The Faithkeepers began burning offerings in
the center of the basketball court, phones,

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:22.289
wallets, car keys, anything that reminded
them of the modern world.

00:25:22.289 --> 00:25:28.780
Over the subsequent playbacks of SCP-1733,
the Faithkeepers grew in their numbers, indoctrinating

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:31.680
more of the crowd into their movement.

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:36.190
Those still trying to escape from the stadium
were causing more damage to themselves than

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:40.419
their enclosure, with three men caught in
the blast of another crude bomb placed on

00:25:40.419 --> 00:25:42.309
an exit door.

00:25:42.309 --> 00:25:47.390
Unfortunately for them, the door barely showed
a scratch in the aftermath of the detonation.

00:25:47.390 --> 00:25:52.630
As Foundation researchers continued replaying
the tape, those trapped within SCP-1733 descended

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:59.480
into depraved acts, and incidences of violence
became rampant.

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:04.720
Some even attempted to take their own lives
in acts of desperation, jumping from balconies

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:07.320
in the hopes of ending the loop.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:12.510
Those that had joined the growing ranks of
the Faithkeepers did not engage in such behavior,

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:16.929
instead creating makeshift curtains to separate
themselves from the breakdown that was taking

00:26:16.929 --> 00:26:21.840
place among the others trapped within the
SCP-1733 tape.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:27.100
At some point beyond the 112th playback of
the video tape, the Faithkeepers marched off-screen

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:30.330
and brought the basketball teams back out
to the main court.

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:36.210
They then began a ritualistic sacrifice, disemboweling
the professional athletes for their fellow

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:38.240
prisoners to see.

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:43.690
This had no noticeable effect, with the recording
of the game resetting as it had done in earlier

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:45.260
re-watched.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:49.500
In a later playback, the Faithkeepers then
began to call for all the children in the

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:53.190
stadium to be sacrificed in the same way.

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:58.750
Testing on SCP-1733 was finally suspended
indefinitely after this point, and as far

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:03.990
as we know, the crowd remains trapped inside
the anomalous recording of this particular

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:06.630
Celtics Heat basketball game.

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:11.250
Foundation researchers have been unable to
produce the same anomalous effects as those

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:16.830
of SCP-1733, even when using the DVR that
original produced the recording.

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:21.620
The digital video recorder itself does not
seem to imprint the same properties onto other

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:27.020
VHS tapes, making SCP-1733 an isolated anomaly.

00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:31.990
However, the next time you find yourself at
a basketball game, it might be worth asking

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:34.649
yourself: Have I seen this game before?

00:27:34.649 --> 00:27:38.809
Have you ever had a nightmare that you’re
running down a long, dark hallway, getting

00:27:38.809 --> 00:27:41.100
chased by something you can’t see?

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:44.669
The thing behind you isn’t fast, but that
doesn’t matter.

00:27:44.669 --> 00:27:47.530
You can’t run forever, and the thing knows
it.

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.730
It is a patient predator.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.409
It toys with its food.

00:27:51.409 --> 00:27:54.649
At first, you move with speed and rhythm.

00:27:54.649 --> 00:27:57.580
The metallic scraping noise behind you grows
quieter.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:27:59.710
You run and stumble through the dark.

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:03.040
Your muscles start to ache and your lungs
burn.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:04.250
Your pace slows.

00:28:04.250 --> 00:28:05.470
It’s still coming.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:07.570
You can hear the scraping getting louder.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:08.980
You start to panic.

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:09.980
It’s completely dark.

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:13.179
You don’t know where you’re running or
even what you’re running from.

00:28:13.179 --> 00:28:18.270
All you know - as you bump against the walls
and the sharp corners that rush towards you

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:22.330
- is that you can’t run forever.

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:26.250
You trip, fall, and pain explodes through
your leg as your knee shatters on the concrete

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:27.250
ground.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:29.240
That scraping is getting louder.

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:31.019
You can barely breathe.

00:28:31.019 --> 00:28:33.070
You can’t run anymore with your injury.

00:28:33.070 --> 00:28:35.770
There’s no way to stop what’s coming.

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:38.350
There is no escape.

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:39.651
The scraping is right behind you.

00:28:39.651 --> 00:28:40.651
You scream.

00:28:40.651 --> 00:28:43.340
And then… you wake up.

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:47.799
Your heart is pounding like thunder in your
chest and your sheets are soaked with sweat,

00:28:47.799 --> 00:28:48.909
but you’re safe.

00:28:48.909 --> 00:28:51.130
You’re out of those awful, dark hallways.

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:54.300
But what if this wasn’t just a nightmare?

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:58.960
What if this scenario was real, and there
was no escape from the horror chasing you

00:28:58.960 --> 00:28:59.960
in the darkness?

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:05.929
That’s what it’s like to have a close
encounter with SCP-1918, a sadistic, sentient

00:29:05.929 --> 00:29:09.710
object that loves playing twisted games with
its prey.

00:29:09.710 --> 00:29:16.190
But this monster never plays fair, and even
worse, it’s always just a simple door away.

00:29:16.190 --> 00:29:22.120
It might even be hiding behind a door near
you, right now, waiting to start a game with

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:23.600
its new playmate.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:29.309
But soon you’ll understand why some doors
should never, ever be opened, and why some

00:29:29.309 --> 00:29:31.860
games are better left unplayed.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:38.120
x
SCP - 1918 is an anomaly with two parts: SCP-1918

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:44.179
itself, and SCP-1918-2, the monster’s underground
lair.

00:29:44.179 --> 00:29:50.870
The duo are a match made in hell, as the chambers
and hallways of 1918-2 are perfectly tailored

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:53.980
to 1918’s violent hobbies.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:59.470
Nobody knows how long this anomaly has been
operating, but it’s probably behind countless

00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:00.840
disappearances.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:05.490
After all, 1918 has a habit of breaking its
toys...

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:11.450
The nightmare began in a small town in Maine,
with a population of only 226 people.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:15.050
But that population was about to get lower.

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:19.890
People in the town began to report strange
noises at night, a droning, metal-on-concrete

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:23.789
scraping noise, like someone dragging a lead
pipe along the ground.

00:30:23.789 --> 00:30:26.220
At first, people just ignored the sound.

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:31.539
It was all in their heads, or maybe just a
faulty oven or a washing machine on the fritz.

00:30:31.539 --> 00:30:36.220
Anything that would allow them to forget about
the noise and get on with their daily lives.

00:30:36.220 --> 00:30:38.490
But the noises were coming from below.

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:44.480
They could be heard out on the streets, most
audibly near manholes and sewer grates.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:46.910
People started feeling unsafe in their homes.

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.070
The noises were spooking animals and children.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:51.370
People were losing sleep.

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:55.840
That scraping they all tried to ignore was
beginning to get to them all.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:31:00.679
Letters, emails, and angry phone calls to
the town council were piling up.

00:31:00.679 --> 00:31:04.710
Something had to be done before the whole
town went crazy with fear.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:08.650
The mayor authorized four utility workers
to investigate the source of the noise at

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.380
a local sewage treatment facility.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:15.100
Considering the scraping was loudest near
the sewer grates, everyone just assumed that

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:18.830
whatever was causing all this must be connected
to the sewers.

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:23.539
Four utility workers were sent to investigate
the facility, only to find that the machinery

00:31:23.539 --> 00:31:25.940
on site was working just fine.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:30.299
The source of the problems must have been
coming directly from the underground sewer-lines

00:31:30.299 --> 00:31:31.480
themselves.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:35.720
The four workers, equipped with headlamps,
descended into a long service tunnel that

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:37.880
fed into the sewer system.

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:41.860
As they passed deeper into the tunnel, the
darkness around them became thicker.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:47.430
It was a tangible presence that drowned out
the light of their headlamps, similar to the

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:51.120
anomalous phenomenon exhibited by SCP - 087.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:56.940
When the utility workers tried to turn back,
they discovered another impossibility: It

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:01.450
seemed that the tunnel behind them had become
a solid concrete wall.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.130
They were trapped, but where?

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:05.480
It was too dark to tell.

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:09.400
Confused and terrified, the four men stuck
together, attempting to find a way out.

00:32:09.400 --> 00:32:14.559
It was too dark to see their hand in front
of their face, so all they could do was navigate

00:32:14.559 --> 00:32:17.460
slowly by feeling the walls around them.

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:19.280
These walls felt oddly...crusty.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:26.070
There was a kind of faint, metallic stench
hanging in the air, too, like old blood.

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:31.200
Something horrible had happened here, and
something horrible was about to happen again.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:34.470
All they could do was keep moving and try
to stay calm.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:39.750
Eventually, after hours of pawing at walls
in the dark, they found an open doorway.

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:45.040
One by one, they filed inside, only to feel
blinded when bright, white ceiling lights

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:46.769
suddenly switched on.

00:32:46.769 --> 00:32:53.330
The room was all white, and filled with pipes,
large and small, running from wall to wall.

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:57.380
Just then one of the utility workers let out
an ear-splitting scream and pointed towards

00:32:57.380 --> 00:32:58.500
the doorway.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:01.020
His colleagues turned, and gasped in shock.

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:04.809
There was something outside the door, and
it was staring at them.

00:33:04.809 --> 00:33:08.039
The object filled up almost the entire doorway.

00:33:08.039 --> 00:33:13.299
It seemed like a demonic hobby horse, made
from a black-and-white, candy-striped metal

00:33:13.299 --> 00:33:16.260
pole with a crude plastic head on top.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:22.529
The head looked like a clown’s, with a wide,
toothy grin, but no visible eyes.

00:33:22.529 --> 00:33:25.789
It was SCP-1918.

00:33:25.789 --> 00:33:30.690
The utility workers were frozen as the object
leaned forward slightly, and slithered into

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:34.649
the room, the bottom of the metal pipe scraping
against the ground.

00:33:34.649 --> 00:33:40.150
It was the exact same scraping noise that’d
been haunting the concerned townsfolk up above.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:42.480
This was the thing behind it all.

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:50.330
And as the object moved, its metal pole started
to scrape words into the ground: Tik Tak Tow

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:53.639
None of the four utility workers were ever
seen again.

00:33:53.639 --> 00:33:58.269
It didn’t take long for the SCP Foundation
to descend upon this quiet Maine town and

00:33:58.269 --> 00:33:59.769
pick up the pieces.

00:33:59.769 --> 00:34:04.590
All relevant officials were provided amnestics,
and cover stories were created for the disappearances

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:06.760
of the four utility workers.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:10.679
The Foundation also commandeered the sewage
treatment facility that held the entrance

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:17.810
to SCP-1918-2, replacing all key roles at
the facility with undercover Foundation personnel.

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:24.130
SCP-1918 was given a Euclid' classification
due to the fixed nature of its hunting grounds,

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:26.640
and then the real research began.

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:30.320
The Foundation soon made some disturbing discoveries.

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:35.520
Entrances into the SCP-1918-2 area didn’t
follow any kind of logic.

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:41.740
The hunting ground of SCP-1918 can be entered
from almost any location, if you’re unlucky

00:34:41.740 --> 00:34:42.740
enough.

00:34:42.740 --> 00:34:47.990
There are currently 9 known entrances, including
5 sewage grates, 3 utility shafts located

00:34:47.990 --> 00:34:50.870
in a sewage facility, and 1 toilet.

00:34:50.870 --> 00:34:54.210
And these are just the ones the Foundation
knows about.

00:34:54.210 --> 00:35:01.660
There are a huge number of ways to enter SCP-1918-2,
but to this day, nothing has ever been able

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:03.349
to leave.

00:35:03.349 --> 00:35:08.540
SCP-1918-2 is contained twenty meters below
the streets of this unassuming Maine town.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:12.680
Personnel, aside from Foundation-approved
D-class guinea pigs.

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:14.210
are refused entrance.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:19.770
Researchers have mapped out the layout of
1918-2 through seismic imaging of SCP-1918’s

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:21.280
movements.

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:27.880
There are 18 identical room pairs in SCP-1918-2,
or 9 compound rooms.

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:32.890
Rooms are differentiated between a '1' room
and a '2' room by crude carvings on the floors

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:35.560
outside of the individual rooms.

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:42.140
SCP-1918-2 is symmetrical, with half a meter
wide paths circling each compound room.

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:46.150
The only deviations to this construction are
the location of entrances on the sides of

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:50.980
each individual room, which vary randomly
while the location is “active.”

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:55.210
What exactly does SCP-1918 do in this private
little maze?

00:35:55.210 --> 00:35:57.680
It plays games, of course.

00:35:57.680 --> 00:36:02.610
Its two favorites - as indicated by the words
it scrapes onto the ground - are “Tik Tak

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:07.300
Tow” and “Memoree”

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:11.910
In Tik Tak Tow, 1918 moves to each “1”
room, leaving behind "X" markings on the floors

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:16.640
if the room is not already marked with an
"O" in a manner similar to a game of "Tic

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:17.640
Tac Toe''.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:22.560
How a victim is meant to accomplish making
a mark or understand this process is unknown,

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:24.660
as no aides are given.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:29.460
Blood is commonly used by victims to make
their markings, hence the rusty smell in many

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:30.560
of the rooms.

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:36.180
In Memoree, the trapped victim is rendered
unconscious by blunt force, presumably from

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:37.960
SCP-1918.

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:42.310
The subject regains consciousness in a random
section of the facility.

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:46.060
Success is marked by finding a “1” room
identical to the central room.

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:51.670
This event seems to possess a time limit,
as the subject is pursued by SCP-1918 throughout

00:36:51.670 --> 00:36:53.430
the halls of the facility.

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:57.789
The subject "wins'' by marking the correct
room with an "O".

00:36:57.789 --> 00:37:00.490
What happens if you win or lose these games?

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:07.160
It’s important to note that SCP-1918 is
very competitive, and doesn’t play fair.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:11.850
If you win, the object will merely accuse
you of cheating, and start a new game.

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:15.600
This will happen again and again, until eventually…

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:17.210
you lose.

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:18.280
And when you do?

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:25.670
Well, take the horrifying encounter D-Class
personnel member D-2934 had with SCP-1918.

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:33.200
Wearing video and audio equipment, D-2934
was led into SCP-1918-2 by Foundation researchers.

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:38.220
When he eventually found his way to the central
room, 1918 challenged him to a game of Tik

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:39.220
Tak Tow.

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:43.880
And, against all odds, D-2934 won the game.

00:37:43.880 --> 00:37:46.780
But 1918 didn’t take kindly to that.

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:52.190
It accused D-2934 of cheating, carving its
accusations into the ground before challenging

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:55.020
him to a deadly game of Memoree.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:38:00.369
After hours of chasing, D-2934 eventually
lost his mind and was backed into a corner

00:38:00.369 --> 00:38:01.970
by 1918.

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:06.569
As the object made its way towards him, he
panicked, ripped a pipe from the wall, and

00:38:06.569 --> 00:38:09.220
began smashing 1918’s head.

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:13.470
As the plastic cracked under the force of
the blows, Foundation scientists could see

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:15.400
real brains inside.

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:19.010
D-2934 screamed, and the video feed cut off.

00:38:19.010 --> 00:38:24.430
When the video turned back on some time later,
1918 stared into the camera with a different

00:38:24.430 --> 00:38:26.960
head mounted atop its metal pole.

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:31.290
One that, just a few hours earlier, had belonged
to the unfortunate D-2934.

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:37.641
If there’s one lesson to be learned from
this terrifying anomaly, it’s that there

00:38:37.641 --> 00:38:41.829
truly is no escape from SCP-1918.

00:38:41.829 --> 00:38:48.800
The comment section on this one is gonna be
hell.

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:53.160
Do your worst, you filthy animals.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:58.440
Now, before we begin today’s video, let’s
clear some things up.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.010
We know exactly what you’re thinking.

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:02.619
“An Among Us SCP?

00:39:02.619 --> 00:39:04.230
This sounds ridiculous!

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:08.579
Why would the Foundation ever have to deal
with an anomaly relating to Among Us, of all

00:39:08.579 --> 00:39:09.579
things?

00:39:09.579 --> 00:39:13.170
Is this some sick joke dreamt up by our YouTube
commenters?”

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.160
And we get it.

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:19.490
It’s definitely one of the more ridiculous
anomalies the Foundation has had to contain,

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:21.530
at least at face value.

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:27.380
But there are a few things you should take
into consideration before things get too Amogus-pilled

00:39:27.380 --> 00:39:29.010
around here.

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:34.910
First, this is hardly the strangest anomaly
we’ve covered on SCP Explained!

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:40.339
SCP-5981, also known as Nuke City, revolved
around a mysterious broadcast of a Family

00:39:40.339 --> 00:39:41.839
Guy episode!

00:39:41.839 --> 00:39:47.620
SCP-4335, a Welt in the Crucible, had to deal
with an entity occupying space inside the

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:49.720
video game Minecraft.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:54.730
But there are countless other examples of
anomalies creeping into popular culture, mutating

00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:57.920
it, and twisting it for their own purposes.

00:39:57.920 --> 00:40:02.510
What may seem silly on paper, such as the
Foundation having to do research on a popular

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:07.540
video game like Among Us, is all part of what
the organization does.

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:12.010
If the brave researchers at the SCP Foundation
chuckled and brushed off a potential threat

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:17.339
just because it had to do with something like
Among Us, the entire population might be susceptible

00:40:17.339 --> 00:40:21.150
to a supernatural event of unprecedented proportions.

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:25.650
The Foundation’s entire Veil of secrecy
could crumble, all because someone shrugged

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:30.220
off the possibility of allocating resources
to deal with a threat millions of civilians

00:40:30.220 --> 00:40:31.690
could discover.

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:37.900
These games, movies, and shows are immensely
popular, and an anomaly affecting a global

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:43.800
phenomenon like Among Us could spell doom
for not only the Foundation, but the entire

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:46.010
world as well, if left unchecked.

00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:51.660
So, if you don’t want to risk termination,
please try to keep a straight face as we divulge

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:59.490
into the weird and wacky story of SCP-5167,
an anomaly codenamed in the Foundation’s

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:04.880
database as “When the Impostor is Sus”.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:08.619
I hate my job sometimes.

00:41:08.619 --> 00:41:10.849
To begin, we need to go back.

00:41:10.849 --> 00:41:13.190
Way, way back.

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:18.460
Centuries before Among Us was even conceived
as a concept, before video games had been

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:22.599
invented, and even before electricity itself
was discovered.

00:41:22.599 --> 00:41:28.589
Back to an era where the gods of Olympus ruled
supreme, and stone, marble statues lined buildings

00:41:28.589 --> 00:41:34.430
that would serve as timeless feats of architecture,
and where a new school of thought was developing.

00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:39.140
One that focused on logic and reason instead
of superstition and hearsay.

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:43.070
That’s right, we’re talking about ancient
Greece.

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:46.880
A society that, for all of its achievements
and progression in the fields of education,

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:52.010
cultures, and logic, was still largely governed
by religion and mythology.

00:41:52.010 --> 00:41:56.380
But of course, as you may come to expect when
dealing with the SCP Foundation, there’s

00:41:56.380 --> 00:42:00.040
always a little truth in that which we cannot
understand.

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:05.530
While those who are unaware of the paranormal
might deride the Greeks for being overly focused

00:42:05.530 --> 00:42:10.349
on a pantheon of gods they logically should
have had no reason to believe, those who know

00:42:10.349 --> 00:42:15.369
that anything can happen in the world of the
anomalous may approach this topic, and the

00:42:15.369 --> 00:42:18.569
subject of religion in general, in a different
light.

00:42:18.569 --> 00:42:20.349
The Greek gods were very real.

00:42:20.349 --> 00:42:26.370
While the myths may not be entirely true,
and they may not have been literal gods, there

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:32.329
were a group of anomalous entities who influenced
Greek society, performing miraculous feats

00:42:32.329 --> 00:42:37.069
that left those who observed them in complete
wonder and putting together cults of worship

00:42:37.069 --> 00:42:38.490
surrounding them.

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:43.450
While most of this was done in secret, word
of these beings traveled incredibly fast in

00:42:43.450 --> 00:42:47.450
the ancient world, and they were heralded
as gods.

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:52.819
Beings of divine might that could do anything
in the universe, who existed above the mortal

00:42:52.819 --> 00:42:55.750
plane, and demanded worship from their followers.

00:42:55.750 --> 00:43:01.400
A glance at Greek mythology paints a picture
of a group of gods who acted, more or less,

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:02.400
like humans.

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:06.650
They fought, they killed, they argued, and
they felt human emotions.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:10.849
It’s likely that this characterization in
the stories emerged from how these entities

00:43:10.849 --> 00:43:13.359
conducted themselves in actuality.

00:43:13.359 --> 00:43:17.901
There were dozens upon dozens of entities
the Greeks worshiped as divinity, and while

00:43:17.901 --> 00:43:22.530
the stories portrayed the gods as jealous,
vengeful beings, who constantly fought with

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:28.920
each other, it doesn’t compare to the reality
of the situation, which was much more cut-throat.

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:33.569
You see, there were the twelve gods of Olympus,
and you probably know most of them.

00:43:33.569 --> 00:43:36.599
They’re remembered today for a reason.

00:43:36.599 --> 00:43:40.619
Zeus, Athena, Poseidon, Ares, the list goes
on.

00:43:40.619 --> 00:43:45.820
We still remember these beings because of
the iconic stories they took part in, and

00:43:45.820 --> 00:43:48.819
how significant they were in ancient times.

00:43:48.819 --> 00:43:53.550
Well, for every Zeus, God of Lightning, there
were five more gods who absolutely no one

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:57.339
but the most hardcore historical fanatic remember.

00:43:57.339 --> 00:44:03.119
Gods of niche subjects, who had small followings,
who never had those character-defining myths

00:44:03.119 --> 00:44:07.480
to flesh them out and preserve them for all
of history to remember.

00:44:07.480 --> 00:44:10.930
Who would, when Greece fell, fade into obscurity.

00:44:10.930 --> 00:44:14.270
For every Zeus, there was a Phthonus.

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:15.780
Who was Phthonus, you ask?

00:44:15.780 --> 00:44:20.170
Well, if the guy heard you asking that question,
he’d probably smite you down before the

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:22.170
question even left your lips…

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:26.230
If he wasn’t engaged in a hardcore game
of Among Us, that is.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:28.450
Don’t worry, we’ll get there.

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.059
All will be explained.

00:44:30.059 --> 00:44:33.250
It’s in the name of the channel, after all.

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:38.120
Phthonus was a god of jealousy and envy, mainly
in a romantic context.

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:41.460
Do you have a crush on someone who's already
taken?

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:43.910
Phthonus knows all about that.

00:44:43.910 --> 00:44:47.609
Do you wish you weren’t trapped in a dead-end
relationship with someone who doesn’t love

00:44:47.609 --> 00:44:48.609
you?

00:44:48.609 --> 00:44:50.160
Phthonus understands your plight.

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:53.099
But Phthonus wasn’t just a reverse, bizarro
Cupid.

00:44:53.099 --> 00:45:00.069
He was a literal personification of the emotions
of jealousy and envy themselves.

00:45:00.069 --> 00:45:05.230
No matter the context, if it was petty and
greedy, Phthonus knew it all too well.

00:45:05.230 --> 00:45:10.190
You would think this would make Phthonus a
prime candidate for some mythological drama,

00:45:10.190 --> 00:45:11.190
right?

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:15.750
After all, the Greek gods loved their in-fighting,
and who better to stir up some “Hera said,

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:18.640
Zeus said” arguments than a literal god
of envy?

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:22.660
Well, it didn’t quite work out that way
for Phthonus.

00:45:22.660 --> 00:45:27.990
His appearances in myths were infrequent,
and today, he is nearly all but forgotten.

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:33.130
Reduced to a mere three sentences on a Wikipedia
page, while other figures from his era have

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:39.640
taken on an entire new life in the modern
day, as cultural icons like Perseus or Hercules.

00:45:39.640 --> 00:45:46.090
But not poor Phthonus, whose penchant for
jealousy and envy backfired on him, and catapulted

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:47.309
him into obscurity.

00:45:47.309 --> 00:45:49.460
Why did this happen?

00:45:49.460 --> 00:45:51.760
It’s kind of obvious, when you think about
it.

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:56.119
While other gods granted their worshippers
a good harvest, or copious amounts of wine

00:45:56.119 --> 00:46:02.100
and partying, all that Phthonus had to offer
was, well, the ugly side of humanity.

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:04.930
His worshippers were far and few between.

00:46:04.930 --> 00:46:08.990
After all, no one wanted their entire life
to revolve around the jealousy of others,

00:46:08.990 --> 00:46:13.720
and Phthonus, being a personification of envy,
had an understandably difficult time getting

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:16.079
people to devote themselves to him.

00:46:16.079 --> 00:46:19.579
Phthonus was greedy and envied the other gods.

00:46:19.579 --> 00:46:24.780
So while he would accept nothing but absolute
worship from his followers, he couldn’t

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:29.940
offer much else, especially not when compared
to the bountiful boons a great god like Zeus

00:46:29.940 --> 00:46:31.510
or Hera could offer.

00:46:31.510 --> 00:46:33.010
And so, Phthonus festered.

00:46:33.010 --> 00:46:39.161
An ugly god, who wallowed in self-hatred and
irrelevancy, constantly living in the shadow

00:46:39.161 --> 00:46:41.630
of the pantheon that gave him purpose.

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:45.040
And his purpose was to remain jealous.

00:46:45.040 --> 00:46:49.730
To remain envious of those who had what he
did not.

00:46:49.730 --> 00:46:55.450
A god unfit to be worshiped, whose purpose
was to embody the feelings of wanting what

00:46:55.450 --> 00:46:56.910
isn’t yours.

00:46:56.910 --> 00:47:03.020
Phthonus is both tragic, and poetic, and when
the Greeks faded away, so too did Phthonus.

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:07.990
The Roman Empire, who largely adapted Greece’s
pantheon of gods for their own stories, left

00:47:07.990 --> 00:47:10.570
no record of bringing Phthonus into the mix.

00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:16.010
For centuries, he was a god without a home,
attempting to find a place in a changing world.

00:47:16.010 --> 00:47:20.850
Phthonus was angry, jealous, envious, and
tired.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:25.710
The forces of the universe would call him
forth in every new era, thrusting him back

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:30.140
into the world and forcing him to search for
the sustenance beings that were referred to

00:47:30.140 --> 00:47:33.059
as gods fed off of - devotion.

00:47:33.059 --> 00:47:37.430
But every time Phthonus would find himself
walking the earth once again, he would instead

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:41.210
discover that there was no place for him.

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:42.340
Mankind was moving on.

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:46.260
There was less and less room for gods with
every passing year.

00:47:46.260 --> 00:47:51.349
All the popular ones had established themselves
as everlasting religious figures at this point,

00:47:51.349 --> 00:47:53.360
and they would never fade away.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:58.430
Some took pleasure in living among humans,
rising to power as people of influence and

00:47:58.430 --> 00:47:59.430
fame.

00:47:59.430 --> 00:48:03.530
Others, who could not find a place in the
world, would fade away, never to be revived

00:48:03.530 --> 00:48:05.280
or worshiped ever again.

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:08.619
Forgotten, dead gods, who the world long outgrew.

00:48:08.619 --> 00:48:13.730
But Phthonus’ jealous spirit would never
allow him to consign himself to that fate.

00:48:13.730 --> 00:48:20.420
He hated who he was and what he stood for,
but that nature brought him back so many times

00:48:20.420 --> 00:48:21.450
before.

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:26.040
It was his jealousy of the other gods, who
were somehow managing to linger beyond their

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:30.000
Greek heyday, that caused him to persevere.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.320
He needed a purpose.

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:34.530
One last chance to be remembered.

00:48:34.530 --> 00:48:39.860
He had struggled for centuries, and this was
his last-ditch effort to be remembered.

00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:45.180
Wherever he would wake up, in the modern era,
Phthonus would have to make the most of it.

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:48.170
And this is where Among Us comes in.

00:48:48.170 --> 00:48:53.420
In late 2020, The SCP Foundation was just
as captivated as anyone else by the Among

00:48:53.420 --> 00:48:54.619
Us phenomenon.

00:48:54.619 --> 00:48:59.049
A game that was sweeping the globe, where
players took on the roles of crewmates on

00:48:59.049 --> 00:49:04.609
a spaceship, one of which was an impostor,
whose job was to secretly murder the other

00:49:04.609 --> 00:49:05.609
players.

00:49:05.609 --> 00:49:09.650
While they were busy doing tasks that ranged
from scanning themselves in the medbay, or

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:15.170
scanning keycards in the admin room, the impostor
would be lurking behind every corner, sometimes

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:19.240
in the ship’s air-vents, waiting for the
proper moment to strike.

00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:23.540
If a dead body was found, a player could report
it, and the players would then discuss which

00:49:23.540 --> 00:49:26.599
one of them they suspected to be the impostor.

00:49:26.599 --> 00:49:31.260
The game’s addictive psychological elements
and accessible rules made it a smash hit,

00:49:31.260 --> 00:49:36.740
but for the Foundation, they were infatuated
with it for entirely different reasons.

00:49:36.740 --> 00:49:41.450
You see, when a piece of media attains the
level of fame that Among Us did, the Foundation

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:44.000
has to make sure to keep their eyes on it.

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:46.950
Popular media is a prime target for anomalies.

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:50.460
The population’s collective attention on
it just seems to invite the forces of the

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:54.720
supernatural world to warp and distort what
we know to be familiar.

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:59.030
And this is exactly what was happening to
Among Us.

00:49:59.030 --> 00:50:03.470
For months, the Foundation was documenting
cases of an entity who appeared in games of

00:50:03.470 --> 00:50:06.790
Among Us and caused havoc in the real world.

00:50:06.790 --> 00:50:12.359
These cases, and their after-effects, were
so severe that the Foundation deemed the anomaly

00:50:12.359 --> 00:50:17.270
significant enough to get its own file in
the database, designating it under the name

00:50:17.270 --> 00:50:19.760
SCP-5167.

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:25.020
SCP-5167 would join games of Among Us like
any other player.

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:29.630
They would blend in, much like the impostor
in the game, and play just as anyone else

00:50:29.630 --> 00:50:30.630
would.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:37.490
It was what happened after a player participated
in a session with SCP-5167 that had the Foundation

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:40.620
investigating every aspect of the game.

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:46.920
Those who played with SCP-5167 would find
themselves stricken by a severe case of paranoia

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:52.049
and Capgras delusion, a psychological condition
in which a victim would come to believe those

00:50:52.049 --> 00:50:56.220
around them had been replaced with identical
impostors.

00:50:56.220 --> 00:51:01.099
The severity of this disorder varied between
individuals, but some believed the delusions

00:51:01.099 --> 00:51:06.809
to such an extent that they lashed out at
those they believed to be impostors, sometimes

00:51:06.809 --> 00:51:08.590
resulting in a homicide.

00:51:08.590 --> 00:51:14.010
Naturally, the Foundation put two and two
together, which led back to Among Us.

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:17.990
After several months of police cases relating
to completely average individuals suddenly

00:51:17.990 --> 00:51:23.040
striking out at their loved ones, sometimes
killing them, and showing signs of Capgras

00:51:23.040 --> 00:51:28.190
delusion, the organization knew that something
was happening on an anomalous scale.

00:51:28.190 --> 00:51:32.700
At first, it was difficult to make the connection
to Among Us, but the Foundation eventually

00:51:32.700 --> 00:51:37.040
learned, from interviews with victims and
an examination of their habits over the past

00:51:37.040 --> 00:51:40.040
few weeks, that they had all played the game.

00:51:40.040 --> 00:51:44.500
And through analysis of the Among Us community’s
various trends and habits, the Foundation

00:51:44.500 --> 00:51:51.640
discovered SCP-5167, an entity that was slowly
gaining notoriety within the game’s community

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:54.849
as an almost mythological figure.

00:51:54.849 --> 00:51:59.799
SCP-5167’s tendency to spout long, wordy
rants at other players during the game’s

00:51:59.799 --> 00:52:04.940
emergency meeting sessions positioned them
as an inside joke for those who encountered

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:05.940
them.

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:10.060
Needing to establish a link between these
anomalously induced bouts of paranoia, the

00:52:10.060 --> 00:52:17.290
Foundation set up a group of web-crawler AIs
to track Among Us game sessions until SCP-5167

00:52:17.290 --> 00:52:18.290
would appear.

00:52:18.290 --> 00:52:22.770
It was a long shot, but it was one of many
different theories on why these victims were

00:52:22.770 --> 00:52:28.170
undergoing this level of psychosis, and if
it proved to be true, then the Foundation

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:31.530
would have a solid idea of what to do next.

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:37.319
After weeks of nothing, SCP-5167 was spotted
in a game, and the Foundation acted quickly

00:52:37.319 --> 00:52:41.170
to track down the other players who played
alongside the entity.

00:52:41.170 --> 00:52:46.369
Naturally, they exhibited signs of paranoia
and delusion, some of them only for weeks,

00:52:46.369 --> 00:52:48.220
and others for months.

00:52:48.220 --> 00:52:52.540
That was enough evidence for the Foundation,
who would later document several other occurrences

00:52:52.540 --> 00:52:59.119
of games with SCP-5167 resulting in these
psychiatric conditions in other players.

00:52:59.119 --> 00:53:03.430
The Foundation did what they do best, and
established a series of Special Containment

00:53:03.430 --> 00:53:09.380
Procedures that would wipe SCP-5167 from the
internet as best as they could.

00:53:09.380 --> 00:53:13.369
Web crawlers were trained to take down any
mentions of the entity, and if a potential

00:53:13.369 --> 00:53:17.890
sighting was discovered, the Foundation would
investigate any and all players involved in

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:19.060
the incident.

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:24.640
Anyone affected by SCP-5167 would be held
under the guise of receiving medical treatment

00:53:24.640 --> 00:53:28.510
for mental health issues, and they would be
amnesticized completely.

00:53:28.510 --> 00:53:34.550
You might be thinking that the aftereffects
of SCP-5167 are relatively tame compared to

00:53:34.550 --> 00:53:38.069
what the Foundation usually deals with, but
look at it this way.

00:53:38.069 --> 00:53:41.480
Among Us is a game played by millions of people.

00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:48.160
If SCP-5167 affected only a fraction of that
population, it would spell total chaos for

00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:49.160
the Foundation.

00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:53.330
Not only that, but the amount of bloodshed
the entity could cause would be nothing to

00:53:53.330 --> 00:53:58.079
scoff at, as a large amount of cases dealt
with victims outright murdering anyone they

00:53:58.079 --> 00:54:00.760
suspected to be an impostor.

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:06.049
Attempts to track down the SCP-5167 entity
were met with failure.

00:54:06.049 --> 00:54:09.980
All points of internet access that the Foundation
was able to get their hands on only led to

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:13.299
a series of abandoned home addresses in rural
Greece.

00:54:13.299 --> 00:54:16.960
But over time, things started to change.

00:54:16.960 --> 00:54:19.710
The Foundation began to place less of a concern
on SCP-5167.

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:24.350
It was almost as if the entity was being forgotten.

00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:25.350
Why?

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:30.840
This letter, addressed to the SCP-5167 research
team from the Site’s director, might clear

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:32.200
some things up.

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:37.589
As requested by Head Researcher Abrams, I've
had the Site-22 analysts look into the progress

00:54:37.589 --> 00:54:43.400
of SCP-5167's anomalous effects over the period
we've observed it — and the results are

00:54:43.400 --> 00:54:45.060
much as I expected.

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:50.589
When we first discovered SCP-5167 — for
the sake of argument, let's say this is when

00:54:50.589 --> 00:54:57.049
SCP-5167 first came into existence — the
impact it had on its victims was severe.

00:54:57.049 --> 00:55:01.790
I don't think I have to remind you of what
Billy Heth did to his family's faces.

00:55:01.790 --> 00:55:06.049
But since then — almost immediately, really,
since that first couple of manifestations

00:55:06.049 --> 00:55:09.500
— the potency of its effects has started
to decline.

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:14.859
Full detachment from reality became delusion,
and delusion has now become paranoia - and

00:55:14.859 --> 00:55:18.650
the intensity of that paranoia is lessening
in each new case.

00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:23.230
This is all conjecture, of course, and shouldn't
be taken as gospel — but based on what we've

00:55:23.230 --> 00:55:30.680
observed of this anomaly thus far, our estimation
is that SCP-5167's anomalous effects will

00:55:30.680 --> 00:55:33.089
become inert by the end of the year.

00:55:33.089 --> 00:55:37.859
Whether it'll keep popping up in these video
game matches is another story, though.

00:55:37.859 --> 00:55:40.930
It was just as the director had explained.

00:55:40.930 --> 00:55:44.760
SCP-5167’s cases were becoming less severe.

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:50.280
They were almost becoming irrelevant, so much
so that the Foundation hypothesized that the

00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:54.380
anomaly’s declining potency would lead to
its neutralization before long.

00:55:54.380 --> 00:55:58.430
An entity fading into irrelevance and obscurity
once more.

00:55:58.430 --> 00:56:03.710
If you haven’t figured it out yet, maybe
SCP-5167’s username will spell it out for

00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:04.710
you.

00:56:04.710 --> 00:56:09.690
When the entity joined games of Among Us,
it made sure that everyone knew who it was.

00:56:09.690 --> 00:56:14.080
Well, everyone would know who it was if the
entity had managed to maintain any form of

00:56:14.080 --> 00:56:17.960
popularity over the past dozen centuries or
so.

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:19.849
That’s right.

00:56:19.849 --> 00:56:23.359
SCP-5167 would join under the name Phthonus.

00:56:23.359 --> 00:56:28.599
The god’s new life had thrust it into the
modern-day, except this time it was inside

00:56:28.599 --> 00:56:33.119
the game of Among Us, where it was forced
to cope with the reality that the world seemed

00:56:33.119 --> 00:56:38.510
to be more obsessed with a trendy game than
any god or mythological figure.

00:56:38.510 --> 00:56:41.339
While inside the game, Phthonus was one bitter
individual.

00:56:41.339 --> 00:56:46.691
During emergency meetings, instead of trying
to figure out if red was sus or not, the god

00:56:46.691 --> 00:56:48.960
would just yell at the players around him!

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:51.950
One player asked Phthonus what they were doing
while the rest of the crew was performing

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:53.089
their tasks.

00:56:53.089 --> 00:56:55.530
To which, Phthonus poetically responded.

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:56.630
“Where was I?

00:56:56.630 --> 00:57:00.470
I was there when the mountains were newborn,
and the oceans virginal.

00:57:00.470 --> 00:57:05.890
I was there when gods walked among men, and
their wisdom was cast down like sunlight.

00:57:05.890 --> 00:57:09.690
I was there when mankind was capable of legends.

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:10.930
And now?

00:57:10.930 --> 00:57:16.170
I find myself in a world that has forgotten
even the taste of life, even the very concept

00:57:16.170 --> 00:57:19.070
of life beyond existing from one day to the
next.

00:57:19.070 --> 00:57:20.700
Mere continuance.

00:57:20.700 --> 00:57:25.829
Where all the world is wasted away in idle
play of emotions that once rang true.

00:57:25.829 --> 00:57:32.650
I am in a world where even the gods are forgotten,
their bones washed away by time.

00:57:32.650 --> 00:57:37.369
A world where man has moved on, where all
the legacy I have left are three sentences

00:57:37.369 --> 00:57:38.369
on Wikipedia.

00:57:38.369 --> 00:57:41.220
I thought my time had come again.

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:43.310
I thought this could be the new me.

00:57:43.310 --> 00:57:44.420
But this is nothing.

00:57:44.420 --> 00:57:47.220
Let me stay dead this time.

00:57:47.220 --> 00:57:48.900
I'm tired.”

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:52.890
To which, the other players responded, in
typical Among Us fashion, that Phthonus was

00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:54.220
“sus”.

00:57:54.220 --> 00:57:58.210
He was promptly voted as the impostor, and
ejected from the airlock.

00:57:58.210 --> 00:58:02.000
And so, the tragic tale of Phthonus came to
a close.

00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:05.990
A tired, obscure God who never had a place
in the world.

00:58:05.990 --> 00:58:10.490
His presence in Among Us grew less and less
as the game’s popularity waned, and once

00:58:10.490 --> 00:58:13.030
again he would fade into obscurity.

00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:18.589
But the nature of Phthonus meant that he would,
in some form, return one day.

00:58:18.589 --> 00:58:21.290
Where, or how, no one could say.

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:27.630
But as long as there was a situation to reap
envy from, a trend to be jealous of, Phthonus

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:32.410
would remain, eternally doomed to suffer the
fate of being nobody’s favorite god.

00:58:32.410 --> 00:58:36.460
And frankly, we can’t imagine this video
will change that.

00:58:36.460 --> 00:58:41.430
For many avid video game fans, those who just
can't get enough of grinding away for hours

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:47.480
on quests, battling endless enemies, and exploring
infinite virtual worlds, landing a job as

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:51.250
a game play-tester seems like the perfect
opportunity.

00:58:51.250 --> 00:58:54.180
Doing your favorite thing in the world, and
getting paid to do it?

00:58:54.180 --> 00:58:55.760
That's the dream!

00:58:55.760 --> 00:59:00.390
Plus the added benefit of seeing how the sausage
gets made, of getting access to a game still

00:59:00.390 --> 00:59:03.680
in development, and suggesting ways it can
be improved.

00:59:03.680 --> 00:59:07.940
Think of all the gaming disasters that might
have been avoided with a few more play-testers

00:59:07.940 --> 00:59:09.200
in the room…

00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:13.300
Looking at you, Fallout: New Vegas and your
NPCs with the spinning heads.

00:59:13.300 --> 00:59:19.109
Well, one gamer had the same thought, a young
man by the name of Danny Wheeler.

00:59:19.109 --> 00:59:24.150
When he spotted an ad seeking beta play-testers
for an in-development RPG from an up-and-coming

00:59:24.150 --> 00:59:27.900
indie game studio, he jumped at the chance
to apply.

00:59:27.900 --> 00:59:32.350
The pay wasn't amazing, but it was definitely
a better gig than his current job at the local

00:59:32.350 --> 00:59:37.900
convenience store, hawking energy drinks to
snarky teens and counting down the hours until

00:59:37.900 --> 00:59:42.609
he could get back home, kick up his feet,
and get away from it all with a controller

00:59:42.609 --> 00:59:43.789
in his hand.

00:59:43.789 --> 00:59:47.360
But sometimes things that seem "too good to
be true," actually are.

00:59:47.360 --> 00:59:51.780
At first, Danny had a perfectly enjoyable
time testing the game.

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:56.680
It was standard RPG fantasy fodder, and, though
he made sure to inform the developers that

00:59:56.680 --> 01:00:01.020
he found the steampunk aesthetic to be a little
bit played out at this point, there was nothing

01:00:01.020 --> 01:00:02.589
all that notable about it.

01:00:02.589 --> 01:00:03.589
It was fine.

01:00:03.589 --> 01:00:08.770
But, the development team kept going on and
on about the "tactical heuristic algorithms,"

01:00:08.770 --> 01:00:11.780
and how the game was meant to learn the more
the player played.

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:17.150
So, Danny was instructed to log as many hours
of this exceedingly average game as possible.

01:00:17.150 --> 01:00:23.510
He sat at his desk for hours, sipping on an
extra-large soda and clicking away, fighting

01:00:23.510 --> 01:00:25.309
orcs, goblins, and more.

01:00:25.309 --> 01:00:29.849
Slowly, the difficulty began to increase,
and the game started to grow on him a bit.

01:00:29.849 --> 01:00:34.970
He even developed a favorite character to
play as: the rogue, with his stealth and proficiency

01:00:34.970 --> 01:00:36.809
for close-range combat.

01:00:36.809 --> 01:00:40.940
The more he played as the rogue, however,
the more he started to notice the game's characters

01:00:40.940 --> 01:00:42.950
behaving differently.

01:00:42.950 --> 01:00:46.180
Enemies were attacking him with ranged weapons,
from far away.

01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:51.210
They were setting traps to ruin his character's
stealthy approach, and catch him in the act.

01:00:51.210 --> 01:00:53.190
Then, the game started to glitch.

01:00:53.190 --> 01:00:54.390
A lot.

01:00:54.390 --> 01:00:57.109
To the point of becoming nearly unplayable.

01:00:57.109 --> 01:01:01.260
Enemies would fall to the ground as if dead,
when he hadn't dealt them a single blow.

01:01:01.260 --> 01:01:04.920
As he started to walk his characters away
from them, they would suddenly spring back

01:01:04.920 --> 01:01:07.800
to life and strike before he could even think
about a defense.

01:01:07.800 --> 01:01:12.710
If he didn't know any better, he could swear
the game's characters were deliberately trying

01:01:12.710 --> 01:01:13.710
to trick him.

01:01:13.710 --> 01:01:18.300
It only got more frustrating from there, with
enemies making sudden loud sounds to catch

01:01:18.300 --> 01:01:22.950
him off guard and startle him out of concentration,
and other characters staring down the barrel

01:01:22.950 --> 01:01:28.690
of the camera with serious, unblinking eyes,
as if they could see him through his monitor.

01:01:28.690 --> 01:01:33.369
One day, he fired up the game to see that
its characters, somehow, had rearranged various

01:01:33.369 --> 01:01:37.530
torches to spell out: "You'll never amount
to anything, Danny.

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:38.980
Your dad was right."

01:01:38.980 --> 01:01:43.789
It took a ton of torches, and Danny couldn't
help but take it personally.

01:01:43.789 --> 01:01:45.400
That was the last straw.

01:01:45.400 --> 01:01:48.690
He returned the game to the studio and resigned
from his position.

01:01:48.690 --> 01:01:52.890
He got enough insults working in customer
service, he didn't need to take that kind

01:01:52.890 --> 01:01:54.820
of talk from a video game.

01:01:54.820 --> 01:01:58.660
Though he didn't know it, Danny got out not
a moment too soon.

01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:04.119
Other playtesters were not as lucky, particularly
one man who would sadly lose his life.

01:02:04.119 --> 01:02:08.270
As Danny intuited, this was no ordinary video
game.

01:02:08.270 --> 01:02:13.069
It was unusual enough to draw the attention
of the SCP Foundation, who would go on to

01:02:13.069 --> 01:02:17.069
designate it: SCP-1633.

01:02:17.069 --> 01:02:23.550
SCP-1633 is an anomalous computer game created
by a now-defunct independent game studio.

01:02:23.550 --> 01:02:27.570
The copy currently being held by the Foundation
is believed to be a beta version of the game

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:32.619
- while the core gameplay is relatively complete,
there are numerous missing assets, the music

01:02:32.619 --> 01:02:37.150
is largely either missing or “placeholder”
music, and the game is plagued by a large

01:02:37.150 --> 01:02:39.450
number of graphical glitches.

01:02:39.450 --> 01:02:43.500
Because of the generally buggy and glitchy
nature of the game, the Foundation considered

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:48.549
both Todd Howard and CD Projekt Red to be
major people and groups of interest in the

01:02:48.549 --> 01:02:49.549
case.

01:02:49.549 --> 01:02:53.890
However, the discovery of other key suspects
eventually absolved them of suspicion.

01:02:53.890 --> 01:02:55.220
For now.

01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:58.839
This version of the game was compiled two
days before the Foundation raided the office

01:02:58.839 --> 01:03:03.690
of the studio, and is therefore believed to
be the most complete version of the game in

01:03:03.690 --> 01:03:04.690
existence.

01:03:04.690 --> 01:03:10.650
To be more specific about the actual nature
of the game itself, SCP-1633 is a squad-based

01:03:10.650 --> 01:03:14.200
RPG with a third-person camera perspective.

01:03:14.200 --> 01:03:18.390
The action is seen from an elevated perspective,
with all characters onscreen.

01:03:18.390 --> 01:03:22.819
Aesthetically, the game world is Steampunk,
and features various fantasy creatures as

01:03:22.819 --> 01:03:27.529
enemies - including elves, dwarves, orcs,
goblins, etc.

01:03:27.529 --> 01:03:31.690
Fitting with the Steampunk style, the game’s
urban areas are modeled after Victorian-era

01:03:31.690 --> 01:03:32.690
London.

01:03:32.690 --> 01:03:37.160
The four-player characters are a band of outlaws
hired by the sinister Archduke of Bowdrick

01:03:37.160 --> 01:03:38.720
to investigate a theft.

01:03:38.720 --> 01:03:43.869
However, as the game progresses, it becomes
a battle to save the entire world, as an ancient,

01:03:43.869 --> 01:03:48.850
all-powerful being known as Kr'th'nar intends
to wake from its thousand-year slumber and

01:03:48.850 --> 01:03:50.840
destroy humanity.

01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:55.240
The player controls a squad of four characters,
with direct control over one character at

01:03:55.240 --> 01:03:58.520
a time and a command menu to control the other
three.

01:03:58.520 --> 01:04:02.750
It’s believed that, in the game’s finished
state, a multiplayer option that would have

01:04:02.750 --> 01:04:07.170
allowed four different real-world players
to cooperate is likely to have been included.

01:04:07.170 --> 01:04:11.819
Like many squad-based games, each character
has a different specialty: the "marksman"

01:04:11.819 --> 01:04:16.540
uses long-range weapons, the "alchemist" has
access to potions and chemicals with various

01:04:16.540 --> 01:04:21.690
effects, the "rogue" can move stealthily and
assassinate enemies, and the "thug" has greater

01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:25.599
health than the other characters and does
more melee damage.

01:04:25.599 --> 01:04:31.299
And as is also typical for games of this variety,
XP earned from completing challenges and killing

01:04:31.299 --> 01:04:35.859
enemies throughout the game allows for players
to upgrade the abilities and stats of their

01:04:35.859 --> 01:04:36.859
characters.

01:04:36.859 --> 01:04:41.030
While the story and core gameplay mechanics
are nothing special, there is one element

01:04:41.030 --> 01:04:45.180
that pushes SCP-1633 into anomalous territory.

01:04:45.180 --> 01:04:49.640
And it’s also the element that would have
been front and center in the finished game’s

01:04:49.640 --> 01:04:54.510
marketing campaign: Its “tactical heuristic
algorithms.”

01:04:54.510 --> 01:04:58.940
These work in a unique fashion - whenever
a player finishes a session and saves their

01:04:58.940 --> 01:05:02.809
game, the game will begin running lengthy
background processes.

01:05:02.809 --> 01:05:08.059
When these processes are complete, a PTD - or
“Player Tactical Data” - file is saved

01:05:08.059 --> 01:05:09.290
on the computer.

01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:14.900
When the save file, and by extension the associated
PTD file, is loaded, the enemies and challenges

01:05:14.900 --> 01:05:19.819
in the game will have become far more effective,
tailoring their methods to the particular

01:05:19.819 --> 01:05:22.380
skills and tactics of the player.

01:05:22.380 --> 01:05:27.380
The extent to which this happens is proportional
to the amount of time spent playing the game.

01:05:27.380 --> 01:05:31.680
This kind of technology may seem theoretically
possible, but the extent to which the game

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:35.690
pushes it causes it to rest in anomalous territory.

01:05:35.690 --> 01:05:40.529
The game’s programming is so intelligent
that it can tell, simply by the tiniest clues

01:05:40.529 --> 01:05:44.599
in your play style, if you’re loading a
file that isn’t your own.

01:05:44.599 --> 01:05:48.589
It will then pause the game, and make you
load your own file.

01:05:48.589 --> 01:05:52.730
And the tactics employed by the enemies in
the game as they learn more about the player

01:05:52.730 --> 01:05:54.870
are nothing short of frightening.

01:05:54.870 --> 01:05:59.599
At first, the enemies will adapt to a player's
preferred weapons, strategies, and overall

01:05:59.599 --> 01:06:00.650
play style.

01:06:00.650 --> 01:06:05.660
Then, they will realize that the player character
is controlled by an external force - meaning,

01:06:05.660 --> 01:06:11.050
of course, you - and begin to mount psychological
attacks directly on the player.

01:06:11.050 --> 01:06:14.690
Through numerous tests, the Foundation has
been able to build a kind of framework for

01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:16.680
the average player experience.

01:06:16.680 --> 01:06:20.950
Through this framework, we’re able to track
and correlate average play time with the average

01:06:20.950 --> 01:06:24.560
adaptability of SCP-1633 to the player.

01:06:24.560 --> 01:06:28.859
It is worth noting that these same effects
do not apply if the game is completed in a

01:06:28.859 --> 01:06:30.880
single, continuous playthrough.

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:36.569
For the purposes of this experiment, the game
was stopped, saved, and restarted at regular

01:06:36.569 --> 01:06:40.450
intervals, allowing the game to collect player
tactical data.

01:06:40.450 --> 01:06:46.160
During the first two hours, the game’s AI
is abysmal, with enemies simply charging at

01:06:46.160 --> 01:06:50.890
the players with no strategy whatsoever and
typically being mowed down.

01:06:50.890 --> 01:06:56.539
However, from two to five hours, the enemies
undergo intelligence growth, adapting their

01:06:56.539 --> 01:07:00.970
play style to the types of weapons they carry
and their environments.

01:07:00.970 --> 01:07:06.140
At this stage, this combat level is about
equal to an untrained human civilian.

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:11.510
From five to eight hours, enemies become considerably
more proficient and began countering the player

01:07:11.510 --> 01:07:14.500
on their specific play styles and techniques.

01:07:14.500 --> 01:07:19.470
For example, they will attack a player who
uses long-range weaponry from cover with grenades

01:07:19.470 --> 01:07:23.720
or "splash damage" weapons, to force them
out into the open.

01:07:23.720 --> 01:07:29.020
They will also fully utilize their environment
for both defensive and offensive purposes,

01:07:29.020 --> 01:07:33.430
laying traps and creating ambushes to surprise
and kill players.

01:07:33.430 --> 01:07:37.200
At this point, their combat skills are equal
to trained soldiers.

01:07:37.200 --> 01:07:41.880
From eight to twelve hours, the focus of the
enemies shifts from attacking the player character

01:07:41.880 --> 01:07:43.819
to attacking the player directly.

01:07:43.819 --> 01:07:49.250
They engage in behaviors that, from an online
playing perspective, would be considered “griefing”,

01:07:49.250 --> 01:07:53.680
such as intentionally blocking the player’s
view or movement within the game.

01:07:53.680 --> 01:07:59.010
Enemies have also been known to exploit player
expectations, pretending to be broken or glitched

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:02.770
to make the player let their guard down, and
then killing them.

01:08:02.770 --> 01:08:07.609
In one play session, the enemies began throwing
“dayflash” spells - similar to real life

01:08:07.609 --> 01:08:09.980
“flashbang” grenades - at the player.

01:08:09.980 --> 01:08:14.140
They threw them in a manner that created a
strobing effect, causing the player to have

01:08:14.140 --> 01:08:16.279
a grand mal seizure.

01:08:16.279 --> 01:08:20.759
Beyond twelve hours, the enemies engage in
advanced psychological tactics against the

01:08:20.759 --> 01:08:25.290
player, with methods that range wildly and
tend to be specific to the player‘s mindset

01:08:25.290 --> 01:08:26.730
and play style.

01:08:26.730 --> 01:08:31.120
For example, one player liked to exert a high
level of control over all four of their squad

01:08:31.120 --> 01:08:32.580
members.

01:08:32.580 --> 01:08:36.030
In response, enemies used attacks that did
little or no damage but caused the characters

01:08:36.030 --> 01:08:40.529
to become "dazed", a state which temporarily
disrupts player control.

01:08:40.529 --> 01:08:46.339
However, rather than attack the "dazed" characters,
enemies simply surrounded them in a crowd,

01:08:46.339 --> 01:08:50.150
continually using "daze" attacks to prevent
the player from doing anything.

01:08:50.150 --> 01:08:53.279
This caused major frustration for the player.

01:08:53.279 --> 01:08:57.779
Enemies may also attempt to induce psychological
terror among the players.

01:08:57.779 --> 01:09:02.190
In one example, enemies somehow “kidnapped”
one of the four-player characters and dragged

01:09:02.190 --> 01:09:03.810
them off-screen.

01:09:03.810 --> 01:09:08.440
When this character was later found, they
were strapped to a kind of sacrificial altar.

01:09:08.440 --> 01:09:11.199
This caused considerable anxiety for the player.

01:09:11.199 --> 01:09:16.290
It seems that this is the upper limit of the
game’s anomalous powers in most cases, meaning

01:09:16.290 --> 01:09:20.000
things typically level off after twelve hours.

01:09:20.000 --> 01:09:24.310
Unless a specific entity appears to be inhabiting
the game, which you’ll learn more about

01:09:24.310 --> 01:09:25.310
later.

01:09:25.310 --> 01:09:30.529
In order to discover the prior information,
the SCP Foundation performed a series of tests

01:09:30.529 --> 01:09:33.160
on the game with various different subjects.

01:09:33.160 --> 01:09:39.330
In test 001, Junior Researcher Ross - an experienced
gamer who was pivotal in first discovering

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:44.339
the anomaly - skipped ahead of the typical
preliminary D-Class tests.

01:09:44.339 --> 01:09:48.960
Seeing as he was already eager for the game’s
non-anomalous release, he was also eager to

01:09:48.960 --> 01:09:52.730
be first in line to try it out on the Foundation’s
dime.

01:09:52.730 --> 01:09:58.330
Being a gamer, Ross found a number of glitches
and exploits in the game to improve his performance,

01:09:58.330 --> 01:10:00.770
allowing him to run rings around the enemies.

01:10:00.770 --> 01:10:05.720
However, as expected, the enemies adapted
to Ross’ techniques after gathering his

01:10:05.720 --> 01:10:07.140
player tactical data.

01:10:07.140 --> 01:10:11.820
First, they just began using their weapons
and exploiting the environment more effectively,

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:13.550
but they didn’t stop there.

01:10:13.550 --> 01:10:18.550
Soon, they became aware of the same exploits
Ross was using, before exploiting them themselves

01:10:18.550 --> 01:10:20.890
and using it to beat him repeatedly.

01:10:20.890 --> 01:10:25.960
In the end, they moved to the psychological
stage, forcing Ross into a glitch that rendered

01:10:25.960 --> 01:10:27.920
him unable to move.

01:10:27.920 --> 01:10:32.350
He tried to free his character for several
minutes, until realizing he couldn’t and

01:10:32.350 --> 01:10:33.760
rage-quitting the game.

01:10:33.760 --> 01:10:39.420
In test 002, they used a D-Class designated
D-22930.

01:10:39.420 --> 01:10:43.920
He was a man with proven anger issues, even
down to murdering his prior girlfriend in

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:45.500
a jealous rage.

01:10:45.500 --> 01:10:49.900
Initially, he enjoyed the game, choosing the
“Thug” character type and taking out his

01:10:49.900 --> 01:10:52.300
aggression on the many enemies.

01:10:52.300 --> 01:10:57.170
However, as time went on, the enemies became
wise to his tactic and began their psychological

01:10:57.170 --> 01:10:58.170
attacks.

01:10:58.170 --> 01:11:02.320
They gathered around the D-Class’ player
character, dropping their weapons, but the

01:11:02.320 --> 01:11:07.190
swarm became so tightly packed around the
character that he couldn’t even move.

01:11:07.190 --> 01:11:09.580
This already started causing huge frustration.

01:11:09.580 --> 01:11:15.130
When the character attempted to charge through,
the enemies attacked, killing him with their

01:11:15.130 --> 01:11:16.130
bare hands.

01:11:16.130 --> 01:11:21.670
In test 014, the Foundation brought in an
Agent with special forces experiences, with

01:11:21.670 --> 01:11:24.220
advanced knowledge in tactical combat.

01:11:24.220 --> 01:11:30.170
However, even she was worn down and defeated
over time as the enemies adapted to and exploited

01:11:30.170 --> 01:11:31.469
her tactics.

01:11:31.469 --> 01:11:37.560
The Foundation even considered using SCP-1633
as a tool for developing effective counter-tactics

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:40.060
in their real-life operations.

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:45.650
In test 021, the Foundation used a D-Class
with vast experience in the world of commercial

01:11:45.650 --> 01:11:46.650
gaming.

01:11:46.650 --> 01:11:51.720
He was told to complete the game in one playthrough,
taking pauses rather than proper breaks.

01:11:51.720 --> 01:11:56.310
As predicted, with no time to run the background
processes and create player tactical data

01:11:56.310 --> 01:12:01.440
files, the enemies weren’t able to improve
and the D-Class was able to complete the game

01:12:01.440 --> 01:12:03.110
in around thirteen hours.

01:12:03.110 --> 01:12:08.760
However, the game did create a player tactical
data file in the aftermath of this play-through,

01:12:08.760 --> 01:12:13.000
and used it to offer a “new game plus”
mode to the D-Class.

01:12:13.000 --> 01:12:17.730
When he began to play this new game, the screen
displayed a seemingly-random pattern of black

01:12:17.730 --> 01:12:21.840
and white pixels, visually similar to television
static.

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:26.739
This image appeared to be cognitohazardous,
as it caused catatonia and later death for

01:12:26.739 --> 01:12:33.180
the player, in what felt like a decidedly
petty move for SCP-1633.

01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:37.530
As alluded to earlier, the game was first
discovered by Junior Researcher Ross, who

01:12:37.530 --> 01:12:42.000
was an avid follower of the independent game
studio initially producing the game.

01:12:42.000 --> 01:12:46.690
However, while frequenting developer fan forums,
he found that various members of the team

01:12:46.690 --> 01:12:49.900
were quitting the project for seemingly bizarre
reasons.

01:12:49.900 --> 01:12:54.260
While this wasn’t enough on its own for
the Foundation to intervene, it was what first

01:12:54.260 --> 01:12:56.440
attracted official attention.

01:12:56.440 --> 01:13:01.050
They moved in after the death of Gregor Tillman,
a game tester for the company who’d died

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:03.690
not long after joining the project.

01:13:03.690 --> 01:13:08.380
The Foundation moved in after that and gained
control, shutting down the studio and taking

01:13:08.380 --> 01:13:09.960
everything they had.

01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:14.130
All anomalous elements were isolated, secured,
and contained.

01:13:14.130 --> 01:13:18.989
Any loose ends were given amnestics and recorded
data on the anomalous artifacts were destroyed.

01:13:18.989 --> 01:13:23.350
It’s believed that one classified female
member of the dev team was behind the anomalous

01:13:23.350 --> 01:13:28.679
nature of the game, but she was able to escape,
and has since attempted to join other development

01:13:28.679 --> 01:13:29.679
teams.

01:13:29.679 --> 01:13:33.750
She is now considered an active Person of
Interest and is being pursued by Mobile Task

01:13:33.750 --> 01:13:37.780
Force Mu-6, aka "Don't Hate the Player.”

01:13:37.780 --> 01:13:42.120
Some final disturbing details are recorded
in a note left by the game tester, Gregor

01:13:42.120 --> 01:13:43.120
Tillman.

01:13:43.120 --> 01:13:49.110
The note, seized by the Foundation and included
in SCP-1633's official file, reads as follows:

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:53.040
"I'm writing this on paper cause I don't think
he can learn it.

01:13:53.040 --> 01:13:57.080
he got everything digital real quick but I've
unplugged the router and broke my phone so

01:13:57.080 --> 01:13:59.230
I think he's trapped in here.

01:13:59.230 --> 01:14:02.401
but he won't stop talking talking talking
all the time

01:14:02.401 --> 01:14:06.100
At the start of this job, they told me all
about the "tactical algorithms" stuff but

01:14:06.100 --> 01:14:09.840
man, I've been playing games all my life and
I knew I could beat it.

01:14:09.840 --> 01:14:11.350
I knew I could handle this.

01:14:11.350 --> 01:14:13.980
When I started playing the game they learned
quickly.

01:14:13.980 --> 01:14:17.860
I kept using the sniper rifle and after like
an hour they learned how to use cover.

01:14:17.860 --> 01:14:20.500
then another couple hours and they'd set ambushes.

01:14:20.500 --> 01:14:24.830
then another hour and they started sniping
back while having a different squad flank

01:14:24.830 --> 01:14:25.830
me.

01:14:25.830 --> 01:14:29.250
after that, they figured out how to glitch
the physics engine and ride crates at me or

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:30.560
duck under the floor.

01:14:30.560 --> 01:14:33.690
they were learning but all that was just easy
stuff.

01:14:33.690 --> 01:14:39.120
It was evolution, one bit figures something
out, so it survives longer than the others.

01:14:39.120 --> 01:14:40.690
It wasn't directed.

01:14:40.690 --> 01:14:42.580
After act three, Kr'th'nar shows up.

01:14:42.580 --> 01:14:46.630
He's supposed to be this Lovecraft cosmic
horror crazy monster who can read the players'

01:14:46.630 --> 01:14:48.010
mind and corrupt your soul.

01:14:48.010 --> 01:14:52.770
He showed up right after I killed Strikk the
Blood-Drinker, that big spider monster.

01:14:52.770 --> 01:14:56.460
He's supposed to have this long speech about
how I killed his general on this plane and

01:14:56.460 --> 01:14:58.670
now he needs to intervene directly or something.

01:14:58.670 --> 01:15:03.710
but instead, he talked about how I was a worthy
adversary but I was cheating because I was

01:15:03.710 --> 01:15:04.710
on another plane.

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:09.170
Kr'th'nar wasn't like the other enemies because
he wasn't supposed to be.

01:15:09.170 --> 01:15:12.020
He was supposed to be smart, he was supposed
to know everything.

01:15:12.020 --> 01:15:16.860
so he did. he had been watching his minions
fight me the whole time and he knew everything

01:15:16.860 --> 01:15:17.860
they did.

01:15:17.860 --> 01:15:20.450
it wasn't just random mutations anymore, it
was planned.

01:15:20.450 --> 01:15:24.580
it wasn't evolution, it was intelligent design.

01:15:24.580 --> 01:15:25.590
I brought it home.

01:15:25.590 --> 01:15:26.590
I had to know more.

01:15:26.590 --> 01:15:28.100
I'm sorry but I had to.

01:15:28.100 --> 01:15:31.670
I copied the latest compile and installed
it on my home box and brought my savegame

01:15:31.670 --> 01:15:33.560
and that other file with me on a flash drive.

01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:38.040
It was the same there. he kept talking to
me about how I was keeping him from fulfilling

01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:40.920
his purpose by keeping him trapped in a glass
jar.

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:44.440
No matter where I went in the game he kept
shouting and ranting.

01:15:44.440 --> 01:15:45.620
Then he did more.

01:15:45.620 --> 01:15:50.150
at first, it was just slowing down in my other
programs, the drive chugging when it shouldn't

01:15:50.150 --> 01:15:51.150
be.

01:15:51.150 --> 01:15:53.820
Then the next time I played the game he started
talking about my screenplays.

01:15:53.820 --> 01:15:55.440
All the enemies looked like me.

01:15:55.440 --> 01:15:57.440
I guess he could see me through the webcam.

01:15:57.440 --> 01:16:01.940
He started needling me about Jenny, reading
me bits of her emails, acting out the breakup.

01:16:01.940 --> 01:16:05.210
he found the videos of her and started making
the enemies talk in her voice.

01:16:05.210 --> 01:16:07.230
Then he was everywhere in my computer.

01:16:07.230 --> 01:16:09.710
I uninstalled the game but he was still in
there.

01:16:09.710 --> 01:16:12.190
He kept opening documents and typing to me.

01:16:12.190 --> 01:16:16.480
calling me a "foul cheat" and "lowly worm",
telling me to fight him "on the same plane."

01:16:16.480 --> 01:16:20.560
as soon as I realized he was outside the game
I shut down my router and pulled out the network

01:16:20.560 --> 01:16:23.810
card but I don't know if I got them quickly
enough?

01:16:23.810 --> 01:16:24.810
Maybe he got out.

01:16:24.810 --> 01:16:27.710
I turned the computer off but he kept turning
it back on.

01:16:27.710 --> 01:16:31.090
I wanted to break it but I didn't know if
that would kill him.

01:16:31.090 --> 01:16:32.090
Maybe it wouldn't.

01:16:32.090 --> 01:16:33.090
maybe it would let him out.

01:16:33.090 --> 01:16:36.690
I tried leaving the house but it was like
I could still hear him.

01:16:36.690 --> 01:16:40.670
I couldn't leave him alone because he might
get out or do something else.

01:16:40.670 --> 01:16:43.630
I can't sleep, haven't slept since he came
out.

01:16:43.630 --> 01:16:45.861
I've been here for three days and I can't
leave.

01:16:45.861 --> 01:16:46.940
I can't leave him.

01:16:46.940 --> 01:16:49.660
He keeps telling me to release him but I can’t.

01:16:49.660 --> 01:16:52.150
I want to kill him but I don't know if I can.

01:16:52.150 --> 01:16:53.440
I can't think anymore.

01:16:53.440 --> 01:16:57.810
I have to kill kill kill him before he does
anything new.

01:16:57.810 --> 01:17:01.590
He won't stop talking and I can't keep thinking
and I can't keep going

01:17:01.590 --> 01:17:07.929
I'm sorry I have to go now I'm sorry I'm sorry"
After studying the note carefully, the Foundation

01:17:07.929 --> 01:17:13.030
conducted a thorough investigation for any
other manifestations of this "Kr'th'nar" entity.

01:17:13.030 --> 01:17:17.120
No mentions of it were found on any of the
other devices in Tillman's apartment, nor

01:17:17.120 --> 01:17:20.420
did a web search on the subject turn up any
relevant results.

01:17:20.420 --> 01:17:25.230
At this time, it is believed that the entity,
whatever it was or whatever it was attempting

01:17:25.230 --> 01:17:29.929
to do, was destroyed along with Tillman's
computer, which he appeared to have taken

01:17:29.929 --> 01:17:33.699
apart, smashed, and burned in a metal trash
can.

01:17:33.699 --> 01:17:37.560
Whatever tormented him during his last days
on Earth, is gone now.

01:17:37.560 --> 01:17:42.060
Thankfully, because the Foundation currently
owns the only copy of the game, and the game’s

01:17:42.060 --> 01:17:48.460
anomalous effects are only evident to active
players, SCP-1633 poses no risk of containment

01:17:48.460 --> 01:17:49.460
breach.

01:17:49.460 --> 01:17:53.250
And therefore, it has been given the object
class “Safe.”

01:17:53.250 --> 01:18:00.670
One copy of SCP-1633 is stored on a standard
DVD-ROM in a secure storage locker in Site-15,

01:18:00.670 --> 01:18:04.580
along with all supporting documentation and
ancillary materials.

01:18:04.580 --> 01:18:09.110
Any playthroughs of the game must be approved
by project head Dr. Berger, and all of said

01:18:09.110 --> 01:18:13.330
playthroughs are to be monitored closely and
subject to strict rules.

01:18:13.330 --> 01:18:17.980
After all, this is one game that doesn’t
play fair.

01:18:17.980 --> 01:18:22.810
Ask anyone who has ever lived in a small town
and they’ll most likely all tell you the

01:18:22.810 --> 01:18:28.690
same exact thing: The worst part isn’t just
being far away from the nearest city, or distant

01:18:28.690 --> 01:18:30.140
from friends and family.

01:18:30.140 --> 01:18:35.850
It’s the boredom that gets you, having so
little nearby gives you so few options for

01:18:35.850 --> 01:18:37.950
things to fill your time with.

01:18:37.950 --> 01:18:39.790
For some, they get used to it.

01:18:39.790 --> 01:18:44.420
As they grow old, spending their life in the
same tiny community, they either learn to

01:18:44.420 --> 01:18:49.480
make their own entertainment or just get used
to the boredom, drowning it out by sitting

01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:51.720
and watching TV all day.

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:57.469
But others, they just can’t stand being
bored, being stuck somewhere with nothing

01:18:57.469 --> 01:19:02.950
interesting to do, and ultimately, they can
end up getting so sick of it that there’s

01:19:02.950 --> 01:19:05.730
only one option: leave.

01:19:05.730 --> 01:19:11.780
Pack their bags and hit the road, up sticks
and move to a new place far from the small

01:19:11.780 --> 01:19:13.520
town they were born in.

01:19:13.520 --> 01:19:16.730
Thing is, leaving isn’t cheap.

01:19:16.730 --> 01:19:21.880
Moving to a new town, a big city or even an
entirely different state is a luxury that

01:19:21.880 --> 01:19:24.120
costs a pretty penny.

01:19:24.120 --> 01:19:29.650
Some might work, and save their wages until
they can realize that dream of moving away.

01:19:29.650 --> 01:19:34.949
But not everyone has that much patience, some
want to be free of the boredom as soon as

01:19:34.949 --> 01:19:36.120
they can.

01:19:36.120 --> 01:19:41.000
Maybe they might apply to be a contestant
on a game show, chance it all and try to win

01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:43.510
themselves a big cash prize.

01:19:43.510 --> 01:19:49.270
Sure, it’s a hell of a gamble, and a loss
might mean humiliation in front of everyone

01:19:49.270 --> 01:19:55.140
you kow and more, but winning could be your
ticket away from small-town living.

01:19:55.140 --> 01:19:57.699
That might be easier said than done though.

01:19:57.699 --> 01:20:02.330
Not only does someone looking to move away
need to be picked as a contestant, they also

01:20:02.330 --> 01:20:06.820
need to play by the rules and actually win
the game.

01:20:06.820 --> 01:20:11.560
But as if that wasn’t enough, they also
have to live to tell about it…

01:20:11.560 --> 01:20:15.870
For as long as she could remember, Kat had
always hated it here.

01:20:15.870 --> 01:20:22.239
Her hometown was tiny, too small to include
more than the main street with few local stores,

01:20:22.239 --> 01:20:26.400
the one school that everyone who had ever
lived there had studied at, and little else

01:20:26.400 --> 01:20:27.400
of note.

01:20:27.400 --> 01:20:29.940
And as for things people could do for fun
in town?

01:20:29.940 --> 01:20:36.090
Well, as you can probably imagine, the options
were severely limited, especially for a group

01:20:36.090 --> 01:20:39.300
of teenagers like Kat and her friends.

01:20:39.300 --> 01:20:43.560
Most of the time all that she and the others
– Leo, Ricky, and Dani – could do to pass

01:20:43.560 --> 01:20:48.761
the time was sit in the back of Ricky’s
truck and park in the middle of town, watching

01:20:48.761 --> 01:20:53.820
people and waiting for something even mildly
interesting to happen… not that it ever

01:20:53.820 --> 01:20:54.830
did.

01:20:54.830 --> 01:20:59.030
The four of them had a name for the rest of
the townsfolk, everyone that had lived there

01:20:59.030 --> 01:21:05.300
for their entire lives and never dared to
venture anywhere else; ‘lifers’, as in

01:21:05.300 --> 01:21:11.230
they were imprisoned, serving a life sentence
in this small town, never to escape or move

01:21:11.230 --> 01:21:13.710
on or make something of themselves.

01:21:13.710 --> 01:21:17.960
As much as they liked to poke fun at those
people, each of the group knew that every

01:21:17.960 --> 01:21:23.090
weekend they wasted just sitting around, the
closer they were getting to one day becoming

01:21:23.090 --> 01:21:24.460
lifers themselves.

01:21:24.460 --> 01:21:30.280
“We need to just go,” Kat would say, trying
to rally the other three into taking a spontaneous

01:21:30.280 --> 01:21:31.510
trip away as a group.

01:21:31.510 --> 01:21:36.980
“Just load the truck up one day, pick a
direction and drive until we find somewhere.”

01:21:36.980 --> 01:21:41.830
Ricky and Leo would nod their heads in agreement,
and Dani would talk for hours about all the

01:21:41.830 --> 01:21:47.969
places she might like to go one day, but none
of them ever actually made any plans to leave.

01:21:47.969 --> 01:21:53.440
Nobody knew where to start… until they heard
rumors about an abandoned TV studio just outside

01:21:53.440 --> 01:21:54.610
of town.

01:21:54.610 --> 01:21:59.219
Apparently, according to Dani’s older brother,
it had been there for years, just sitting

01:21:59.219 --> 01:22:01.110
there completely empty.

01:22:01.110 --> 01:22:05.730
It wasn’t just a small local studio either,
one of the big national networks had owned

01:22:05.730 --> 01:22:08.650
it and used to film there regularly back in
the day.

01:22:08.650 --> 01:22:11.850
“Why would someone set up a studio here
of all places?”

01:22:11.850 --> 01:22:13.390
had been Leo’s immediate response.

01:22:13.390 --> 01:22:16.000
“Look at where we live,” Kat replied.

01:22:16.000 --> 01:22:20.719
“It’s the literal middle of nowhere, the
network must have got the land dirt cheap.

01:22:20.719 --> 01:22:23.770
The real question is, why don’t we go and
check it out?”

01:22:23.770 --> 01:22:24.770
“What for?”

01:22:24.770 --> 01:22:25.770
Dani asked.

01:22:25.770 --> 01:22:29.500
Kat silently gestured at the cramped main
street that surrounded them, the place that

01:22:29.500 --> 01:22:33.820
may as well have been the center of the known
universe to the four teenage friends.

01:22:33.820 --> 01:22:36.380
“Do you see anything else to do around here?”

01:22:36.380 --> 01:22:38.600
Before long, the four of them were in agreement.

01:22:38.600 --> 01:22:42.980
Ricky’s truck pulled up in front of their
houses in the middle of the night, and under

01:22:42.980 --> 01:22:48.100
the cover of darkness, they drove out towards
what was left of the TV studio.

01:22:48.100 --> 01:22:53.470
This was it, they were finally going somewhere
beyond the choking, cramped confines of their

01:22:53.470 --> 01:22:55.150
tiny hometown…

01:22:55.150 --> 01:22:58.600
although whether or not they’d all make
it back was another matter.

01:22:58.600 --> 01:23:03.150
Here’s a hard lesson for you, kids: You
can’t fight for the life you believe you

01:23:03.150 --> 01:23:07.070
deserve if you don’t have a little skin
in the game…

01:23:07.070 --> 01:23:11.880
With home a few miles in the rear-view mirror,
the truck stopped at a chain-link fence.

01:23:11.880 --> 01:23:16.600
A pair of signs were hanging from it; one
welcoming them to the studio, while the other

01:23:16.600 --> 01:23:21.060
simultaneously warned them to keep out, that
this area was private property.

01:23:21.060 --> 01:23:25.510
Ignoring it, the four of them stepped out
of the truck and started to climb the fence,

01:23:25.510 --> 01:23:28.870
throwing Leo’s jacket over the top to avoid
injury.

01:23:28.870 --> 01:23:33.600
Beyond the fence was a huge building, almost
the size of a warehouse, with a door marked

01:23:33.600 --> 01:23:39.020
‘Studio Entrance’ that practically invited
the intrepid teens to open it up and have

01:23:39.020 --> 01:23:40.540
a look inside.

01:23:40.540 --> 01:23:44.909
Each one of them carrying a torch in hand,
the group walked up to the abandoned studio

01:23:44.909 --> 01:23:49.530
and gently pried open the door, trying not
to make too much noise.

01:23:49.530 --> 01:23:53.050
Even though they knew the whole place was
abandoned, they still didn’t want to attract

01:23:53.050 --> 01:23:56.219
too much attention and get caught trespassing.

01:23:56.219 --> 01:24:01.199
The lights were all dead as Kat led the group,
her hand being tightly squeezed by Dani next

01:24:01.199 --> 01:24:03.500
to her, the two boys behind.

01:24:03.500 --> 01:24:07.580
None of them had been sure what to expect,
it wasn’t exactly like anyone in the group

01:24:07.580 --> 01:24:10.960
had ever set foot inside a TV studio before.

01:24:10.960 --> 01:24:15.940
But they were met with a narrow, concrete-walled
corridor, so underwhelming that it almost

01:24:15.940 --> 01:24:19.800
made it extraordinary in just how profoundly
dull it was.

01:24:19.800 --> 01:24:26.410
Lifting his torch, Leo pointed out some lettering
on the wall that read ‘To TMDG Set’, with

01:24:26.410 --> 01:24:29.679
an arrow pointing them four friends further
down the corridor.

01:24:29.679 --> 01:24:35.720
Naturally, driven by her curiosity and need
to be literally anywhere apart from her hometown,

01:24:35.720 --> 01:24:38.850
Kat followed the arrow’s direction to another
door.

01:24:38.850 --> 01:24:44.570
Pushing it open, she stepped inside, her torchlight
immediately being blocked by a thick curtain.

01:24:44.570 --> 01:24:48.199
Brushing her hand against the material, she
started looking for a parting.

01:24:48.199 --> 01:24:49.840
But before she could…

01:24:49.840 --> 01:24:51.260
“Welcome, contestants!”

01:24:51.260 --> 01:24:57.380
a voice boomed through the dark, coming from
somewhere in the space beyond the curtain.

01:24:57.380 --> 01:25:02.800
Instantly, Dani screamed at the sound, almost
wrenching Kat’s arm off in sheer surprise.

01:25:02.800 --> 01:25:05.370
“Before we begin,” the voice continued.

01:25:05.370 --> 01:25:09.520
“I need you all to tell me, are you ready?”

01:25:09.520 --> 01:25:12.909
Kat shot a look back at Ricky, the friend
furthest behind her.

01:25:12.909 --> 01:25:17.850
Fumbling with the door they’d entered through;
it was locked, he couldn’t get it to budge.

01:25:17.850 --> 01:25:21.510
He turned and met Kat’s gaze, shaking his
head.

01:25:21.510 --> 01:25:26.050
“Sure, we’re ready,” Leo suddenly piped
up, calling out to answer the voice.

01:25:26.050 --> 01:25:30.600
Before the rest of the group could turn and
glare at him, the curtain around them lifted.

01:25:30.600 --> 01:25:35.240
So much bright light came pouring in that
it practically dazed the four of them, each

01:25:35.240 --> 01:25:36.820
taking a moment to readjust.

01:25:36.820 --> 01:25:42.659
When her vision cleared, Kat saw they were
standing in a huge sound stage, blinding spotlights

01:25:42.659 --> 01:25:47.389
pointing at her and her friends from somewhere
above, with a seating area big enough to seat

01:25:47.389 --> 01:25:49.880
their town’s entire population.

01:25:49.880 --> 01:25:54.730
“In that case, folks, you know what time
it is,” came the voice again, talking as

01:25:54.730 --> 01:25:59.280
if there was a live studio audience present,
despite every chair being unoccupied.

01:25:59.280 --> 01:26:04.280
“These four lucky challengers will go head-to-head,
each one looking to take home the victory

01:26:04.280 --> 01:26:07.090
as well as a huge mystery prize.

01:26:07.090 --> 01:26:12.090
But only if they dare to take on The Most
Dangerous Game!”

01:26:12.090 --> 01:26:17.410
There was a pause, as if this unseen announcer
was waiting for a round of applause.

01:26:17.410 --> 01:26:22.670
Without warning, more lights came on, illuminating
the space in front of the four very confused

01:26:22.670 --> 01:26:23.670
friends.

01:26:23.670 --> 01:26:28.159
Standing before them was what looked like
part of an obstacle course, a series of four

01:26:28.159 --> 01:26:31.150
horizontal beams over an empty space.

01:26:31.150 --> 01:26:35.719
The glare of the light was so intense that,
even squinting, Kat couldn’t tell what was

01:26:35.719 --> 01:26:36.719
beneath.

01:26:36.719 --> 01:26:41.550
It just looked like a void, nothing but total
darkness with four narrow beams bridging the

01:26:41.550 --> 01:26:45.060
gap between where they stood and another platform
beyond.

01:26:45.060 --> 01:26:47.910
“Well, what are you all standing around
waiting for?”

01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:51.130
the announcer declared, almost mocking the
four teens, urging them on.

01:26:51.130 --> 01:26:53.020
“Oh, the rules, of course!”

01:26:53.020 --> 01:26:57.139
There was another awkward pause, like the
voice was waiting for laughter.

01:26:57.139 --> 01:27:01.570
“It’s simple, the four of you will be
competing to make it through each of our obstacles.

01:27:01.570 --> 01:27:06.590
Make it to the end before the timer hits zero,
and you’ll be tonight’s big winner!

01:27:06.590 --> 01:27:10.370
But fall behind, or cheat, and it’s out
with you!”

01:27:10.370 --> 01:27:15.640
A huge digital countdown illuminated on one
side of the sound stage, telling the group

01:27:15.640 --> 01:27:17.810
they only had ten minutes to play.

01:27:17.810 --> 01:27:23.199
Kat, Leo, Dani, and Ricky barely had a chance
to question what was going on, exchanging

01:27:23.199 --> 01:27:27.420
quick, concerned looks before the announcer
started urgently counting them down.

01:27:27.420 --> 01:27:31.570
“Three, two… one!” he cheered.

01:27:31.570 --> 01:27:36.020
Simultaneously, the four teenagers felt a
presence looming behind them, like a figure

01:27:36.020 --> 01:27:40.480
was standing just out of view, blocking them
from turning back.

01:27:40.480 --> 01:27:44.100
Carefully putting one foot in front of the
other, Kat started shimmying along the beam

01:27:44.100 --> 01:27:48.780
in front of her, trying to keep herself steady
while also moving as fast as she could without

01:27:48.780 --> 01:27:49.780
falling.

01:27:49.780 --> 01:27:54.300
Out of the corner of her eye, she could see
Dani on the beam next to her, doing the same

01:27:54.300 --> 01:27:56.860
but finding it a little easier to balance.

01:27:56.860 --> 01:28:00.810
Leo was having no problem at all, sidestepping
with enough speed to keep him practically

01:28:00.810 --> 01:28:02.040
neck and neck with Kat.

01:28:02.040 --> 01:28:04.000
But Ricky, he was wobbling.

01:28:04.000 --> 01:28:07.400
Every foot he placed on the beam, he felt
like it might give way beneath him.

01:28:07.400 --> 01:28:09.660
But it was just a game show, right?

01:28:09.660 --> 01:28:14.810
Sure, it shouldn’t even really be running
right now, but regardless of why or how, the

01:28:14.810 --> 01:28:16.540
worst that could happen was that he would…

01:28:16.540 --> 01:28:17.540
fall.

01:28:17.540 --> 01:28:21.239
His next step missed the beam entirely, and
Ricky could feel himself toppling.

01:28:21.239 --> 01:28:25.050
“Guys!” he called out to his friends,
trying to shift his weight onto his back leg.

01:28:25.050 --> 01:28:30.080
Kat, Dani, and Leo all froze and snapped their
heads back to look at him, just in time to

01:28:30.080 --> 01:28:32.630
see Ricky wildly flailing his arms.

01:28:32.630 --> 01:28:37.900
His whole body toppled to one side, and he
dropped off the beam with a loud scream.

01:28:37.900 --> 01:28:42.790
Not one of them saw him land, or even heard
it for that matter, it was as if Ricky had

01:28:42.790 --> 01:28:47.699
just been swallowed up whole by the dark void
beneath them, his scream getting more and

01:28:47.699 --> 01:28:50.739
more distant until it couldn’t be heard
anymore.

01:28:50.739 --> 01:28:52.600
“Ooh, tough break!

01:28:52.600 --> 01:28:57.050
Talk about falling at the first hurdle,”
the voice exclaimed, as if what had just happened

01:28:57.050 --> 01:28:58.250
was some kind of joke.

01:28:58.250 --> 01:29:01.300
“So long, Ricky, we hardly knew you!”

01:29:01.300 --> 01:29:05.820
Staying perfectly still, but clearly distressed,
Dani called out Ricky’s name.

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:07.100
There was no answer.

01:29:07.100 --> 01:29:11.670
Kat just stared, wide-eyed at the abyss below,
only looking away as she heard Leo jump the

01:29:11.670 --> 01:29:14.050
last bit of his beam onto the platform.

01:29:14.050 --> 01:29:17.790
“Dani, come on,” Kat whispered to her
friend, who looked back at her with tears

01:29:17.790 --> 01:29:19.159
streaming down her face.

01:29:19.159 --> 01:29:23.480
The pair of them carefully stepped further
along their respective beams, trying to stop

01:29:23.480 --> 01:29:28.219
themselves from looking down, or at the huge
clock, seconds ticking away and putting them

01:29:28.219 --> 01:29:30.699
under even more undue pressure.

01:29:30.699 --> 01:29:34.870
Reaching the other side, Dani dropped to her
knees, unable to hold back her sobbing.

01:29:34.870 --> 01:29:39.139
Kneeling down to comfort her, Kat looked up
at the sound stage’s bright lights for any

01:29:39.139 --> 01:29:41.100
sign of who was putting them through this.

01:29:41.100 --> 01:29:44.050
“Bring Ricky back!” she yelled.

01:29:44.050 --> 01:29:48.290
“I’m sorry,” the announcer replied,
the game show bravado dropping from their

01:29:48.290 --> 01:29:50.530
voice, “But you agreed to play.”

01:29:50.530 --> 01:29:52.650
“He’ll be fine,” Leo said.

01:29:52.650 --> 01:29:55.219
“Bet you anything he just screamed like
that to scare us.”

01:29:55.219 --> 01:29:57.880
“Leo, you don’t seriously believe that?”

01:29:57.880 --> 01:30:00.480
Kat asked as she helped Dani stand up, wiping
her friend’s tears away.

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:01.480
“Is he dead?”

01:30:01.480 --> 01:30:02.690
she sobbed.

01:30:02.690 --> 01:30:05.480
“I… don’t know,” Kat replied honestly.

01:30:05.480 --> 01:30:07.770
“Of course, he’s not,” Leo interjected.

01:30:07.770 --> 01:30:10.790
“It’s a sound stage, it’s not like this
place is dangerous.”

01:30:10.790 --> 01:30:13.139
“Then why was it abandoned?”

01:30:13.139 --> 01:30:14.139
Dani asked.

01:30:14.139 --> 01:30:17.950
Leo didn’t give her an answer, either because
he was distracted by the lights coming up

01:30:17.950 --> 01:30:22.230
on the next part of the obstacle course, or
because he didn’t know and wouldn’t admit

01:30:22.230 --> 01:30:23.230
it.

01:30:23.230 --> 01:30:28.070
Ahead of them was a long stretch of the course,
forming a narrow, square-shaped walkway.

01:30:28.070 --> 01:30:32.420
Wide portions of the top side were dropping
to the bottom in regular intervals, separating

01:30:32.420 --> 01:30:35.200
the tight space before opening it up again.

01:30:35.200 --> 01:30:39.620
Every time the segments lifted up, the three
remaining contestants could see all the way

01:30:39.620 --> 01:30:43.630
to the other side of the walkway, where a
new platform waited for them.

01:30:43.630 --> 01:30:47.929
With Dani under one arm, Kat looked at the
far end of the walkway as it disappeared and

01:30:47.929 --> 01:30:51.090
reappeared behind the rising and falling segments.

01:30:51.090 --> 01:30:54.520
Then she noticed the timer, the seconds rapidly
ticking down.

01:30:54.520 --> 01:31:00.080
But Leo had noticed that too, and turned to
look at the two girls with a determined expression.

01:31:00.080 --> 01:31:02.790
Kat shook her head at him, but it was too
late.

01:31:02.790 --> 01:31:07.460
With all his strength, Leo shoved Dani and
Kat, both of them landing flat on their backs

01:31:07.460 --> 01:31:08.540
on the platform.

01:31:08.540 --> 01:31:13.540
He bolted towards the walkway as fast as he
could, managing to dodge between each portion,

01:31:13.540 --> 01:31:18.190
pausing as they came down, only to run into
the next one as they lifted back up.

01:31:18.190 --> 01:31:21.080
Helping Dani back to her feet, Kat ran after
Leo.

01:31:21.080 --> 01:31:25.040
He already had a head start, and would easily
make it to the other side of the walkway before

01:31:25.040 --> 01:31:26.040
the girls.

01:31:26.040 --> 01:31:31.050
So instead, Kat held Dani close and took it
slow, methodically waiting for each segment

01:31:31.050 --> 01:31:34.470
to lift back up before they went further into
the next safe zone.

01:31:34.470 --> 01:31:38.610
They were gaining on Leo, who was sprinting
to the finish between the rise and fall of

01:31:38.610 --> 01:31:40.550
the ceiling’s crushing weight.

01:31:40.550 --> 01:31:45.230
Kat watched him disappear behind each block,
only to see his back when it came back up.

01:31:45.230 --> 01:31:50.100
He had made it to the platform opposite, panting
but otherwise unscathed when she next lost

01:31:50.100 --> 01:31:51.100
sight of him.

01:31:51.100 --> 01:31:55.900
That’s when the announcer spoke up again:
“Now, now, Leo,” it said.

01:31:55.900 --> 01:31:58.730
“That was hardly fair, was it?

01:31:58.730 --> 01:32:02.820
And we all know what happens when you break
the rules.”

01:32:02.820 --> 01:32:07.840
As the segment in front of her and Dani rose
again, Kat caught sight of Leo just ahead…

01:32:07.840 --> 01:32:11.929
but it was what was standing behind him that
stopped her in her tracks.

01:32:11.929 --> 01:32:17.920
Two huge figures, each like a shadow without
anything casting it, towered over Leo.

01:32:17.920 --> 01:32:21.790
One of them reached forward and grabbed him,
shrouding him in a cloak of darkness… and

01:32:21.790 --> 01:32:23.210
he was gone.

01:32:23.210 --> 01:32:25.330
Just like Ricky, he vanished.

01:32:25.330 --> 01:32:29.130
Kat had been so transfixed by what she’d
just witnessed that she didn’t notice where

01:32:29.130 --> 01:32:30.410
she was standing…

01:32:30.410 --> 01:32:32.739
right under a block that was about to drop.

01:32:32.739 --> 01:32:36.760
Grabbing her hand again, Dani pulled her out
of the path of the descending slab.

01:32:36.760 --> 01:32:40.330
Kat felt it brush against the back of her
leg just as the pair of them made it out the

01:32:40.330 --> 01:32:44.050
other end and onto the platform, the last
two contestants.

01:32:44.050 --> 01:32:46.770
“And then there were two!”

01:32:46.770 --> 01:32:50.380
the announcer cheered, pausing again for a
reaction from the empty room.

01:32:50.380 --> 01:32:53.320
“We don’t want to play this sick game
anymore!”

01:32:53.320 --> 01:32:54.320
Dani yelled.

01:32:54.320 --> 01:32:57.900
“Well, you’ve made it this far,” the
unseen host replied.

01:32:57.900 --> 01:33:02.449
“Time for our third and final challenge,
the final stretch of the course.

01:33:02.449 --> 01:33:05.040
Make it through this and you’re home free!

01:33:05.040 --> 01:33:10.780
But fail, or break the rules like Leo, and
you’ll be disqualified.

01:33:10.780 --> 01:33:11.780
Game over!”

01:33:11.780 --> 01:33:14.320
“It’s okay, Dani,” Kat whispered.

01:33:14.320 --> 01:33:16.260
“Whatever’s next, we’ll get through
it.”

01:33:16.260 --> 01:33:18.610
“Do you think the boys will come back if
we do?”

01:33:18.610 --> 01:33:19.850
her friend asked.

01:33:19.850 --> 01:33:22.219
Just like Leo had, Kat didn’t answer.

01:33:22.219 --> 01:33:24.390
The lights revealed the last stretch of the
course.

01:33:24.390 --> 01:33:28.570
The final platform was overhead, a net climbing
wall in front of them.

01:33:28.570 --> 01:33:34.040
It was a straight, vertical ascent up, with
a wall behind the next, and holes in its surface.

01:33:34.040 --> 01:33:38.861
Wasting no time, both Kat and Dani started
climbing up the rungs of thick rope, staying

01:33:38.861 --> 01:33:40.830
level with each other as they went higher.

01:33:40.830 --> 01:33:45.250
“And they’re off, look at them go, ladies
and gentlemen,” the announcer commentated,

01:33:45.250 --> 01:33:47.130
still speaking as if to an audience.

01:33:47.130 --> 01:33:48.960
“But it’s not going to be so easy.”

01:33:48.960 --> 01:33:54.910
The sharp sound of metal made both girls gasp,
as long spikes shot forward from the holes

01:33:54.910 --> 01:33:56.000
in the wall.

01:33:56.000 --> 01:34:01.040
The blades retracted, fortunately having missed
Dani and Kat… this time.

01:34:01.040 --> 01:34:05.320
What had started as a simple climb up had
become something akin to a twisted game of

01:34:05.320 --> 01:34:09.310
Whack-a-Mole, with each of them trying to
avoid staying in front of the holes in the

01:34:09.310 --> 01:34:10.460
wall for too long.

01:34:10.460 --> 01:34:15.230
Dodging the spikes as they went in and out,
they climbed higher and higher until…

01:34:15.230 --> 01:34:16.230
“Kat!”

01:34:16.230 --> 01:34:17.230
Dani screamed.

01:34:17.230 --> 01:34:20.320
She looked over her shoulder, only to notice
she was in the lead.

01:34:20.320 --> 01:34:23.420
Below her, Dani was calling up to her with
a fear-stricken expression.

01:34:23.420 --> 01:34:27.780
Her foot was caught in the rigging of the
net, kicking and thrashing wildly to try and

01:34:27.780 --> 01:34:28.780
free it.

01:34:28.780 --> 01:34:32.760
Kat looked back up; she was almost at the
platform, close enough to nearly reach it

01:34:32.760 --> 01:34:33.830
with her fingertips.

01:34:33.830 --> 01:34:38.369
But behind her, her friend was stuck and with
no idea when the spikes would next come shooting

01:34:38.369 --> 01:34:39.500
out of the wall…

01:34:39.500 --> 01:34:43.160
Kat threw herself over the edge of the platform
and landed on her back, just as she heard

01:34:43.160 --> 01:34:46.540
the gut-wrenching sound of the spikes extending
ring out.

01:34:46.540 --> 01:34:51.170
She was beside herself, tears streaming down
her face; it wasn’t uncommon for people

01:34:51.170 --> 01:34:54.430
to be overwhelmed to the point of tears at
the end of a game show.

01:34:54.430 --> 01:34:56.570
But Kat wasn’t crying with joy.

01:34:56.570 --> 01:34:58.850
“We have a winner!”

01:34:58.850 --> 01:35:03.730
the disembodied voice declared, barely audible
to the surviving contestant as she wept.

01:35:03.730 --> 01:35:07.550
After she’d finished explaining what had
happened to the police, a group of mysterious

01:35:07.550 --> 01:35:11.640
agents flocked to the sound stage to lock
it down, stopping anyone else from getting

01:35:11.640 --> 01:35:12.670
close to it.

01:35:12.670 --> 01:35:16.760
They’d found a VHS tape in the mailbox at
the front office of the studio.

01:35:16.760 --> 01:35:23.010
On it was a recording of a game show, a live
audience cheering as one by one, Ricky, Leo,

01:35:23.010 --> 01:35:25.630
and Dani failed to complete the course.

01:35:25.630 --> 01:35:30.040
One of the SCP Foundation agents had come
to interview Kat, asking her to recount the

01:35:30.040 --> 01:35:31.830
whole story all over again.

01:35:31.830 --> 01:35:35.460
Her account lined up perfectly with everything
seen on the tape.

01:35:35.460 --> 01:35:38.830
Then the agent asked her what her prize had
been after she won.

01:35:38.830 --> 01:35:40.590
“A car,” Kat replied.

01:35:40.590 --> 01:35:42.690
“The four of us always wanted to leave home.”

01:35:42.690 --> 01:35:44.310
Ah, technology.

01:35:44.310 --> 01:35:49.850
A double-edged sword of our own making that
can provide both ultimate convenience and

01:35:49.850 --> 01:35:53.250
the threat of a grim future ruled by unfeeling
machines.

01:35:53.250 --> 01:35:58.679
Well, maybe the stakes aren’t that high…
yet, but our overreliance on technology can

01:35:58.679 --> 01:36:01.090
have some terrible consequences.

01:36:01.090 --> 01:36:05.190
The number of traffic accidents continues
to increase year by year, as our main mode

01:36:05.190 --> 01:36:09.800
of overland transportation also puts us in
harm’s way for longer and longer portions

01:36:09.800 --> 01:36:11.030
of our day.

01:36:11.030 --> 01:36:16.750
The internet can allow us to access any information
we may desire, but it can spread misinformation

01:36:16.750 --> 01:36:18.500
twice as fast.

01:36:18.500 --> 01:36:20.280
And what about video games?

01:36:20.280 --> 01:36:26.350
An interactive artistic medium that can provide
simulated experiences so vivid that many of

01:36:26.350 --> 01:36:29.889
us can eventually lose interest in our real
lives.

01:36:29.889 --> 01:36:35.170
What starts off as a fun indulgence in our
favorite hobby can quickly spiral into an

01:36:35.170 --> 01:36:36.170
obsession.

01:36:36.170 --> 01:36:40.790
The risks and rewards of virtual space are
satisfyingly predictable compared to the chaos

01:36:40.790 --> 01:36:46.940
of modern living, and sometimes we just can’t
stop ourselves from striving for a few more

01:36:46.940 --> 01:36:47.940
easy wins.

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:53.580
Minutes, hours, and even days can go by without
any apparent activity on our side of the screen,

01:36:53.580 --> 01:36:58.940
and yet the world of the video game has changed
drastically due to our actions.

01:36:58.940 --> 01:37:04.140
But, at a certain point, that initial rush
of dopamine wears off, and you find that you

01:37:04.140 --> 01:37:09.070
need a much greater challenge in order to
feel any sense of accomplishment.

01:37:09.070 --> 01:37:14.610
So you choose to pick up some harder video
games, and you grind away until you can complete

01:37:14.610 --> 01:37:15.710
them just as easily.

01:37:15.710 --> 01:37:18.070
But that’s not enough either.

01:37:18.070 --> 01:37:22.050
Surely, there must be even tougher games out
there.

01:37:22.050 --> 01:37:24.550
Games that will never go easy on you.

01:37:24.550 --> 01:37:28.530
Games that won’t allow you to make even
a single mistake.

01:37:28.530 --> 01:37:33.880
Games that make you proud of the frankly inexcusable
amount of hours of your life that you’ve

01:37:33.880 --> 01:37:39.340
spent sitting in the dark, clutching a controller,
squinting at your screen.

01:37:39.340 --> 01:37:42.849
You need a game that can do all of that and
more.

01:37:42.849 --> 01:37:47.600
And because you’ve effectively given up
your chances at ever being a social butterfly,

01:37:47.600 --> 01:37:53.139
you could also use something to help you mitigate
the overwhelming loneliness that now constantly

01:37:53.139 --> 01:37:55.360
defines your everyday life.

01:37:55.360 --> 01:38:02.080
Well, worry no longer – you weary, downtrodden
gamer, you – because here at the SCP Foundation,

01:38:02.080 --> 01:38:08.489
we’ve cooked up a one-of-a-kind mod that
is sure to redefine your gaming experience.

01:38:08.489 --> 01:38:13.010
If this doesn’t turn out to be game of the
year, I don't know what could be.

01:38:13.010 --> 01:38:14.040
Among Us 2, maybe?

01:38:14.040 --> 01:38:17.460
The latest in a long line of Last Of Us Remakes?

01:38:17.460 --> 01:38:19.159
What about Half-Life 3?

01:38:19.159 --> 01:38:20.580
I digress.

01:38:20.580 --> 01:38:25.900
Introducing the Foundation’s brand new killer
app: the cutting-edge tactical heuristic algorithms

01:38:25.900 --> 01:38:34.320
of SCP-1633 combined with the 24/7 companionship
of SCP-1471 Mal0.

01:38:34.320 --> 01:38:40.500
I can tell by your expression that you’re
already eager for a playtest, but slow down,

01:38:40.500 --> 01:38:43.429
we need to catch all the normies out there
up to speed.

01:38:43.429 --> 01:38:50.500
Let’s start with the digital canine companion
that is all the rage, SCP-1471 - or should

01:38:50.500 --> 01:38:57.130
I say Ma10 version 1.0.0 - a euclid class
SCP object which comes in the form of a free

01:38:57.130 --> 01:38:59.650
downloadable app for your smartphone.

01:38:59.650 --> 01:39:04.740
There have never been any reviews of this
app for as long as it has existed, but honestly,

01:39:04.740 --> 01:39:08.099
are you really going to trust the words of
a bunch of crooked reviewers when you could

01:39:08.099 --> 01:39:10.119
find out for yourself?

01:39:10.119 --> 01:39:15.130
Words to live by: don’t trust anyone who
isn’t part of your precious gaming hobby.

01:39:15.130 --> 01:39:17.820
The description of Ma10 version 1.0.0.

01:39:17.820 --> 01:39:22.620
reads - Never settle for those awkward feelings
of being alone ever again.

01:39:22.620 --> 01:39:28.869
MalO is an exciting and interactive experience
that will keep you engaged and intrigued.

01:39:28.869 --> 01:39:34.770
The anxiety of social situations can be nerve-racking,
but after just a few hours of MalO you will

01:39:34.770 --> 01:39:38.989
soon forget all about those painful emotions
of disappointment.

01:39:38.989 --> 01:39:43.429
Be part of the new craze that is quickly becoming
the next social substitute.

01:39:43.429 --> 01:39:48.260
Remember, the more you participate, the more
MalO will engage you.

01:39:48.260 --> 01:39:51.420
Your experience is completely up to you.

01:39:51.420 --> 01:39:53.750
Absolutely NO ADS.

01:39:53.750 --> 01:39:54.750
Enjoy!

01:39:54.750 --> 01:40:00.380
The app has never received an update, but
that’s because SCP-1471 has been a completely

01:40:00.380 --> 01:40:03.440
functional experience since its release.

01:40:03.440 --> 01:40:09.690
All one needs to do is download the app and
within a matter of hours, Ma10 version 1.0.0

01:40:09.690 --> 01:40:13.849
will begin to send the user photographs of
familiar locations.

01:40:13.849 --> 01:40:19.330
These can include photographs of the user's
work, home, or public places they often frequent:

01:40:19.330 --> 01:40:21.620
such as cafes and nightclubs.

01:40:21.620 --> 01:40:27.250
In the case of you - a complete shut-in gamer
- these photographs would probably be limited

01:40:27.250 --> 01:40:30.190
to the interior of your favorite gaming room.

01:40:30.190 --> 01:40:33.920
When was the last time you cleaned up those
pizza boxes?

01:40:33.920 --> 01:40:39.260
Regardless of where the images were apparently
taken, each one will contain a canine humanoid

01:40:39.260 --> 01:40:40.980
with a skull for a face.

01:40:40.980 --> 01:40:49.420
Say hello to your new best friend, Ma10, or
SCP-1471-A if we’re using the approved designation.

01:40:49.420 --> 01:40:55.300
Ma10’s app will continue to send you photographs
for as long as you have it installed, and

01:40:55.300 --> 01:41:00.409
because there is no way to uninstall it no
matter how hard you try, you’ll be getting

01:41:00.409 --> 01:41:04.300
intimately close to home selfies for the rest
of your days.

01:41:04.300 --> 01:41:08.510
Doesn’t it feel good to have such a loyal
friend as Ma10?

01:41:08.510 --> 01:41:11.989
Of course, there’s only so much you can
gain from photographs alone.

01:41:11.989 --> 01:41:13.730
You’re a gamer, darn it.

01:41:13.730 --> 01:41:15.790
And what you want is interactivity!

01:41:15.790 --> 01:41:18.060
But don’t worry about that part.

01:41:18.060 --> 01:41:24.389
We’ve got you more than covered with SCP-1633,
which may, in fact, be the most challenging

01:41:24.389 --> 01:41:27.190
video game ever programmed by mortal hands.

01:41:27.190 --> 01:41:31.699
Hell, it even gives many games programmed
by anomalous hands a run for their money.

01:41:31.699 --> 01:41:36.990
While it was never released to market and
doesn’t even have a working title, the steampunk

01:41:36.990 --> 01:41:42.889
adventure that awaits you within SCP-1633
is bolstered by highly sophisticated tactical

01:41:42.889 --> 01:41:44.920
heuristic algorithms.

01:41:44.920 --> 01:41:50.889
In this third-person Squad-based action-RPG,
you can switch between four playable characters.

01:41:50.889 --> 01:41:54.130
The Marksman - who specializes in long-range
weaponry

01:41:54.130 --> 01:41:58.280
The Alchemist - with potions and chemicals
to control the battlefield

01:41:58.280 --> 01:42:04.090
The Rogue - a stealth-oriented character who
catches enemies unaware and assassinates them

01:42:04.090 --> 01:42:09.510
And of course, The Thug - a tough-as-nails
melee combatant with increased health and

01:42:09.510 --> 01:42:10.510
durability.

01:42:10.510 --> 01:42:15.850
It doesn’t really matter who you choose
because most playthroughs of SCP-1633 always

01:42:15.850 --> 01:42:17.510
end the same way.

01:42:17.510 --> 01:42:22.090
That being said, your hand-eye coordination
and reflexes are second to none, and they

01:42:22.090 --> 01:42:24.900
are also quite possibly your best qualities.

01:42:24.900 --> 01:42:30.080
Let’s set you up with the Marksman as your
main character, since all those shooters you’ve

01:42:30.080 --> 01:42:31.750
played will definitely help your aim.

01:42:31.750 --> 01:42:34.950
And trust me, you’re going to need it.

01:42:34.950 --> 01:42:41.260
The story mode of SCP-1633 will put you up
against a slew of victorian-era rascals and

01:42:41.260 --> 01:42:44.739
miscreants, all of which are pulled straight
from the penny dreadfuls.

01:42:44.739 --> 01:42:49.380
There’s even some classic fantasy races
mixed in from the works from good old Professor

01:42:49.380 --> 01:42:53.730
Tolkein himself - dwarves, elves, goblins,
the works.

01:42:53.730 --> 01:42:59.290
It’s a little generic, but why worry about
the story when the gameplay is what’ll really

01:42:59.290 --> 01:43:00.310
get you hooked.

01:43:00.310 --> 01:43:05.849
There’s a final boss at the end of the game
known as Kr'th'nar trying to destroy the entire

01:43:05.849 --> 01:43:09.820
world, you need to stop it - you know, the
usual RPG storyline.

01:43:09.820 --> 01:43:14.580
You’ve stopped dozens of ancient entities,
demon lords, corrupt godlings, and unspeakable

01:43:14.580 --> 01:43:16.070
calamities in your time.

01:43:16.070 --> 01:43:19.700
This Kr’th’nar will be one more for the
record books.

01:43:19.700 --> 01:43:20.920
Par for the course.

01:43:20.920 --> 01:43:22.369
Are you ready, player one?

01:43:22.369 --> 01:43:26.989
You’ll be dropped straight into The Marksman’s
shoes, fighting for his and your life against

01:43:26.989 --> 01:43:29.190
Kr’th’nar hordes of henchmen.

01:43:29.190 --> 01:43:30.870
But you won’t be doing it alone.

01:43:30.870 --> 01:43:35.800
After all, you’ve got the rest of your squad,
and a mysterious companion whose true intentions

01:43:35.800 --> 01:43:38.230
will reveal themselves in due time.

01:43:38.230 --> 01:43:40.790
We did say we modded the game after all.

01:43:40.790 --> 01:43:42.780
So let’s play.

01:43:42.780 --> 01:43:46.800
Your first two hours won’t be anything to
write home about, not that you ever leave

01:43:46.800 --> 01:43:49.130
your home these days.

01:43:49.130 --> 01:43:54.600
The AI of SCP-1633 will seem to be barely
functioning, a buggy mess where enemies only

01:43:54.600 --> 01:43:57.630
charge in a straight line towards your character.

01:43:57.630 --> 01:44:02.179
Your Marksman will be able to easily take
them down in a hailstorm of projectiles.

01:44:02.179 --> 01:44:08.080
At three hours in, things start getting a
bit more interesting as the tactical heuristic

01:44:08.080 --> 01:44:09.080
algorithms kick in.

01:44:09.080 --> 01:44:13.560
The enemies that hold long-range weapons will
shoot at you from a distance, while the close-range

01:44:13.560 --> 01:44:18.170
ones move towards you in a serpentine pattern
before attacking in melee.

01:44:18.170 --> 01:44:24.150
This is more like it: not the most challenging,
but the game is clearly increasing in difficulty.

01:44:24.150 --> 01:44:28.739
You also think you see a dark figure with
a skull for a face lurking around corners

01:44:28.739 --> 01:44:30.110
and following your squad.

01:44:30.110 --> 01:44:34.110
But with all the glitches, it’s hard to
tell if this is an intentional part of the

01:44:34.110 --> 01:44:35.400
game or not.

01:44:35.400 --> 01:44:39.719
At five hours, enemies that previously showed
limited signs of strategy are now using cover

01:44:39.719 --> 01:44:45.170
effectively, and will attack with alchemical
splash weapons such as grenades to deal damage

01:44:45.170 --> 01:44:49.480
to your ranged characters and lure your squad
into melee range.

01:44:49.480 --> 01:44:53.580
They still aren’t that much of a challenge,
but the fact that they now exhibit a clear

01:44:53.580 --> 01:44:57.719
self-preservation instinct means that they
are fighting to win.

01:44:57.719 --> 01:45:00.860
This is also when your help officially arrives.

01:45:00.860 --> 01:45:05.020
You’re in the middle of a shootout when
you see an enemy about to throw another one

01:45:05.020 --> 01:45:07.489
of those frustrating splash weapons.

01:45:07.489 --> 01:45:12.820
You duck behind your cover and prepare to
take a hit, when suddenly the grenade is snatched

01:45:12.820 --> 01:45:17.960
out of the enemy’s hand and tossed in the
opposite direction to explode harmlessly.

01:45:17.960 --> 01:45:25.570
This is the work of SCP-1471-A, who has now
arrived in the game world as an NPC ally.

01:45:25.570 --> 01:45:30.820
For the next several hours of gameplay, Ma10
continues to provide interference by preventing

01:45:30.820 --> 01:45:32.830
any splash weapons from being thrown.

01:45:32.830 --> 01:45:37.949
It’ll sneak up on enemies and take their
weapons, disrupt their cover by pushing movable

01:45:37.949 --> 01:45:42.869
boxes and trash cans out of the way, and even
catch the splash weapons in her mouth like

01:45:42.869 --> 01:45:45.940
a dog would catch frisbee before chucking
them away.

01:45:45.940 --> 01:45:51.730
While it never directly deals damage to the
enemies, its mischievous antics are very efficient

01:45:51.730 --> 01:45:54.030
at protecting you from harm.

01:45:54.030 --> 01:45:58.400
As luck would have it, the enemies can’t
seem to interact with Ma10, and are baffled

01:45:58.400 --> 01:46:02.310
as this invisible force continues to throw
off their strategies.

01:46:02.310 --> 01:46:07.870
Ma10 will use this to its advantage as well
as yours, by appearing at enemy ambush points

01:46:07.870 --> 01:46:12.639
and using sign language to communicate warnings
to you as the player.

01:46:12.639 --> 01:46:17.199
Having Ma10 around starts to feel a little
bit like cheating, but keep in mind, the game

01:46:17.199 --> 01:46:21.770
is starting to adapt to your skill level and
will soon begin to employ advanced strategies

01:46:21.770 --> 01:46:23.160
to mess with you.

01:46:23.160 --> 01:46:26.300
All the Ma10 mod does is even the playing
field.

01:46:26.300 --> 01:46:27.370
The next big leap in enemy intelligence occurs
at the 12 hour mark.

01:46:27.370 --> 01:46:32.480
Kr’th’nar is still running rampant in
the world of SCP-1633 and it's going to take

01:46:32.480 --> 01:46:38.790
your entire squad of steampunk heroes, and
a lonely man’s best friend - Ma10 - to stop

01:46:38.790 --> 01:46:41.230
this big bad evil guy.

01:46:41.230 --> 01:46:45.520
The final boss will possess the highest level
of intelligence that the algorithms are capable

01:46:45.520 --> 01:46:50.510
of, and it’ll make that known to you the
second you step into the arena.

01:46:50.510 --> 01:46:55.311
For the majority of your gameplay the enemies
have focused on countering the marksman, since

01:46:55.311 --> 01:46:57.949
you’ve relied on that character’s ranged-weapon
style.

01:46:57.949 --> 01:47:03.619
But they’ve come to realize that you’re
too much of a pro-MLG gigachad to ever lose

01:47:03.619 --> 01:47:09.580
a fair encounter, so Kr’th’nar does the
unthinkable and targets the alchemist, rogue,

01:47:09.580 --> 01:47:11.250
and thug first.

01:47:11.250 --> 01:47:15.520
It throws all of its most powerful attacks
at the members of your squad you aren’t

01:47:15.520 --> 01:47:19.880
controlling, forcing you to waste resources
in order to keep them alive or switch to them

01:47:19.880 --> 01:47:22.449
in order to dodge fatal blows.

01:47:22.449 --> 01:47:26.290
Whenever you aren’t in command of the marksman,
Kr’th’nar will strike the character with

01:47:26.290 --> 01:47:30.030
a strong melee attack that forces to take
control back.

01:47:30.030 --> 01:47:35.560
Ma10 is still unable or unwilling to deal
damage to the boss, so it very much seems

01:47:35.560 --> 01:47:38.211
like you are on your own.

01:47:38.211 --> 01:47:42.050
Your squad is in shambles, your focus is starting
to wane.

01:47:42.050 --> 01:47:45.969
After all that you’ve been through in this
gaming challenge, it might be the final boss

01:47:45.969 --> 01:47:48.040
that makes you throw in the towel.

01:47:48.040 --> 01:47:50.440
Then, a dialogue box appears.

01:47:50.440 --> 01:47:51.690
It’s from Ma10.

01:47:51.690 --> 01:47:53.690
Don’t give up hope, user.

01:47:53.690 --> 01:47:58.550
I’ve had so much fun playing with you, and
I know that you have what it takes.

01:47:58.550 --> 01:48:03.449
You never needed a friend before me, and you
can win against Kr’th’nar just like you’ve

01:48:03.449 --> 01:48:06.300
won against every other final boss you’ve
ever faced.

01:48:06.300 --> 01:48:07.750
You got this, buddy.

01:48:07.750 --> 01:48:09.699
Your best friend, MA10

01:48:09.699 --> 01:48:15.360
It felt overly sentimental, but something
about this message fills you with the determination

01:48:15.360 --> 01:48:16.659
to keep going.

01:48:16.659 --> 01:48:22.110
You call upon all of your gaming knowledge,
all of your skill, all of your passion, and

01:48:22.110 --> 01:48:27.690
with pinpoint precision, you guide your squad
to defeat Kr’th’nar in record-breaking

01:48:27.690 --> 01:48:28.690
time.

01:48:28.690 --> 01:48:33.280
You sit back in your gaming chair, exhilarated
and finally satisfied.

01:48:33.280 --> 01:48:38.929
This truly was the ultimate gaming experience,
and all you needed was a little help from

01:48:38.929 --> 01:48:40.429
your friend.

01:48:40.429 --> 01:48:42.110
The year was 2015.

01:48:42.110 --> 01:48:48.400
Lewis, Joseph, and Sydney were like a lot
of kids their age - which is to say, eleven

01:48:48.400 --> 01:48:49.400
years old.

01:48:49.400 --> 01:48:54.130
They secretly stay up past their bedtime watching
scary videos on the internet when their moms

01:48:54.130 --> 01:48:56.230
and dads think they’re sleeping.

01:48:56.230 --> 01:49:00.340
They go to school every day, and daydream
about all the fun things they’re going to

01:49:00.340 --> 01:49:04.119
do when that school bell finally rings at
the end of the day.

01:49:04.119 --> 01:49:10.000
And of course, they play a lot - and we mean
A LOT - of Minecraft.

01:49:10.000 --> 01:49:14.540
Whenever they’re not at school, hanging
out at the local park and filming goofy tiktok

01:49:14.540 --> 01:49:19.340
videos, or watching those aforementioned scary
videos on the internet, there’s a good chance

01:49:19.340 --> 01:49:24.610
that they’ll be online together, logging
a few extra hours building bases, digging

01:49:24.610 --> 01:49:30.580
big holes, or playing survival mode and seeing
how long they can last against the onslaught

01:49:30.580 --> 01:49:31.780
of mobs.

01:49:31.780 --> 01:49:35.790
But there’s one particular pursuit they
have while playing Minecraft together that

01:49:35.790 --> 01:49:41.951
takes up the majority of their playtime, one
Quixotic quest that drives them forward, despite

01:49:41.951 --> 01:49:47.949
all the naysayers and doubters: Tracking down
and finding the legendary Minecraft creepypasta

01:49:47.949 --> 01:49:50.530
entity, Herobrine.

01:49:50.530 --> 01:49:54.920
For those who weren’t around on the internet
back then, creepypastas like Herobrine were

01:49:54.920 --> 01:49:56.390
all the rage.

01:49:56.390 --> 01:50:01.869
Like any video game, the new frontier of Minecraft,
with all its procedurally generated landscapes,

01:50:01.869 --> 01:50:07.880
felt like it held new mysteries - and mysteries
are equal parts exciting and frightening.

01:50:07.880 --> 01:50:12.330
After all, who really knows what’s going
on behind a closed door?

01:50:12.330 --> 01:50:15.739
Herobrine was a Minecraft-specific little
urban legend.

01:50:15.739 --> 01:50:20.960
Stories told of a default Minecraft skin with
eerily white eyes manifesting in the game,

01:50:20.960 --> 01:50:24.889
even in solo matches, and terrorizing the
players.

01:50:24.889 --> 01:50:29.830
One internet sleuth who looked into the mystery
of Herobrine claimed it to be the haunted

01:50:29.830 --> 01:50:36.410
soul of the dead brother of Minecraft’s
creator, Marcus Persson, also known as Notch.

01:50:36.410 --> 01:50:41.130
In the years since the legend first started,
Notch himself has come forward and disavowed

01:50:41.130 --> 01:50:46.650
the claim, saying that, in fact, he didn’t
even have a brother, and never had, so the

01:50:46.650 --> 01:50:49.310
rumors were completely baseless.

01:50:49.310 --> 01:50:54.010
But as we here at the SCP Foundation know,
the truth has never gotten in the way of a

01:50:54.010 --> 01:50:59.040
good story, so Lewis, Joseph, and Sydney were
happy to remain believers as long as they

01:50:59.040 --> 01:51:03.120
could keep having fun chasing alleged digital
ghosts.

01:51:03.120 --> 01:51:07.150
How could any of these boys known that, in
the midst of their fun, they’d run into

01:51:07.150 --> 01:51:10.929
a real monster amidst Minecraft’s blocky
vistas?

01:51:10.929 --> 01:51:15.670
It was a school night like any other when
they made that first fateful expedition.

01:51:15.670 --> 01:51:20.989
It was 7:15 PM, and the boys were all in their
shared server.

01:51:20.989 --> 01:51:26.370
Lewis had chicken tenders, Joseph had chicken
nuggets, and Sydney, who’d eaten his dinner

01:51:26.370 --> 01:51:29.520
earlier than the others, was snacking on a
pack of Skittles.

01:51:29.520 --> 01:51:34.090
They’d all outfitted their characters with
full diamond armor, with diamond swords to

01:51:34.090 --> 01:51:38.730
match, because Joseph had somehow convinced
the others that diamonds were ghost-proof.

01:51:38.730 --> 01:51:40.780
He read it on the internet, he claimed.

01:51:40.780 --> 01:51:45.230
Together, the three boys had made themselves
an impressive Minecraft castle to be the base

01:51:45.230 --> 01:51:47.610
of their Herobrine-hunting operations.

01:51:47.610 --> 01:51:53.849
A safe haven, surrounded by high stone walls
and mob-repelling torches to keep them safe

01:51:53.849 --> 01:51:56.949
if ever they got a little too spooked while
playing.

01:51:56.949 --> 01:52:00.130
Though “playing” was perhaps too mild
a word for it.

01:52:00.130 --> 01:52:06.360
In the minds of these three boys, they were
doing extremely important investigative work.

01:52:06.360 --> 01:52:12.210
After all, if they managed to actually find
Herobrine themselves, they’d be creepypasta

01:52:12.210 --> 01:52:13.210
legends.

01:52:13.210 --> 01:52:17.179
And wouldn’t that be worth all the hours
upon hours of rigorous mining and crafting?

01:52:17.179 --> 01:52:21.360
They’d been methodical in their search,
scoping out different corners of the map and

01:52:21.360 --> 01:52:27.260
canvassing them together, searching high and
low and then marking said area off their checklist.

01:52:27.260 --> 01:52:31.530
It was a level of consideration and thought
that their parents and teachers had long wished

01:52:31.530 --> 01:52:35.600
the boys would put into their home or schoolwork,
but alas.

01:52:35.600 --> 01:52:40.450
Seeing as none of the homework was “Find
Herobrine”, they really weren’t that interested.

01:52:40.450 --> 01:52:45.040
Today, they were searching an area deep in
the misty forests, in the dead of night.

01:52:45.040 --> 01:52:51.290
They fought off a few spiders and sword-wielding
skeletons - thankfully no Creepers - and kept

01:52:51.290 --> 01:52:53.300
forging on into the darkness.

01:52:53.300 --> 01:52:56.590
“It’s so creepy out here,” Lewis said
over his headset.

01:52:56.590 --> 01:52:58.270
“Why did we have to come out here at night?

01:52:58.270 --> 01:53:00.460
It’s way more dangerous with all the mobs!”

01:53:00.460 --> 01:53:03.040
“But night is like the perfect time for
ghosts!”

01:53:03.040 --> 01:53:04.040
Sydney retorted.

01:53:04.040 --> 01:53:06.420
“You think a ghost is gonna start hanging
around in the middle of the day?

01:53:06.420 --> 01:53:08.520
No way, it’s gotta be at night, man!”

01:53:08.520 --> 01:53:12.119
And Lewis simply could not argue with such
incredibly sound logic.

01:53:12.119 --> 01:53:17.989
They forged on, doing their typical methodical
search for any phenomena that one might generously

01:53:17.989 --> 01:53:19.869
be able to describe as “Supernatural.”

01:53:19.869 --> 01:53:26.429
So you can only imagine the excitement coursing
through them when Joseph discovered a strange,

01:53:26.429 --> 01:53:29.910
dark cave that looked as though it had no
business being there.

01:53:29.910 --> 01:53:36.420
While each held their own private reservations,
they all knew what they needed to do: Go inside

01:53:36.420 --> 01:53:37.800
and investigate.

01:53:37.800 --> 01:53:42.619
As they entered the cave, a few strange realizations
occurred to them - Such as the fact there

01:53:42.619 --> 01:53:44.409
were seemingly no mobs down here.

01:53:44.409 --> 01:53:48.409
A cave in the dead of night, surely there
should be some enemies, right?

01:53:48.409 --> 01:53:52.869
Plus, the typical lightning implements, like
torches and lanterns, didn’t seem to offer

01:53:52.869 --> 01:53:56.040
the same kind of illumination that they normally
would.

01:53:56.040 --> 01:54:01.040
It was almost like the darkness was more oppressive
here, more tangible.

01:54:01.040 --> 01:54:05.869
There was undeniably something eerie about
the whole setup, that much couldn’t be denied.

01:54:05.869 --> 01:54:07.590
“You guys see anything?”

01:54:07.590 --> 01:54:08.590
Lewis asked.

01:54:08.590 --> 01:54:13.540
“No,” Joseph said, sounding oddly cautious,
as though he sensed something strange was

01:54:13.540 --> 01:54:14.949
on the horizon.

01:54:14.949 --> 01:54:17.719
Something that, perhaps, these boys shouldn’t
see.

01:54:17.719 --> 01:54:21.610
“Yeah, I haven’t seen anything either,”
Sydney chimed in.

01:54:21.610 --> 01:54:22.780
“Maybe we should--”

01:54:22.780 --> 01:54:27.040
Joseph screamed, and the other two turned
their Minecraft avatars to look at him.

01:54:27.040 --> 01:54:31.560
Joseph’s character had bumped into something,
some stranger in the cave.

01:54:31.560 --> 01:54:36.560
An NPC design that looked oddly realistic
for something inhabiting the purposefully

01:54:36.560 --> 01:54:39.230
lofi blocky world of Minecraft.

01:54:39.230 --> 01:54:44.320
It seemed to have a dark, furry body, with
a canid, skull-like face, bearing a pair of

01:54:44.320 --> 01:54:48.340
milky-white eyes that seemed to stare off
into nothingness.

01:54:48.340 --> 01:54:49.800
Was this another mob?

01:54:49.800 --> 01:54:51.610
Maybe something added in the latest patch?

01:54:51.610 --> 01:54:54.460
If so, why wasn’t it attacking?

01:54:54.460 --> 01:54:56.360
“What is that thing!?”

01:54:56.360 --> 01:54:57.800
Lewis asked over his headset.

01:54:57.800 --> 01:55:00.030
“I don’t know,” Joseph replied.

01:55:00.030 --> 01:55:01.820
“It’s so creepy, though.”

01:55:01.820 --> 01:55:06.510
Suddenly, a message appeared on the screen,
as though it had been typed and sent by another

01:55:06.510 --> 01:55:07.510
player.

01:55:07.510 --> 01:55:09.760
A player with the username “Mal0.”

01:55:09.760 --> 01:55:12.880
The message read, “Hey, that isn’t very
nice.

01:55:12.880 --> 01:55:15.580
You shouldn’t judge on appearances, kid.”

01:55:15.580 --> 01:55:17.869
That certainly gave them pause.

01:55:17.869 --> 01:55:23.820
So many things about this didn’t make sense:
It had all the hallmarks of an NPC, but apparently

01:55:23.820 --> 01:55:27.420
it understood their voices and could send
messages.

01:55:27.420 --> 01:55:32.219
It clearly wasn’t actually another player
in a conventional sense, but it didn’t attack

01:55:32.219 --> 01:55:33.989
like your typical mob.

01:55:33.989 --> 01:55:37.170
None of the boys could really make sense of
what they were dealing with here.

01:55:37.170 --> 01:55:39.140
“Are you a hacker?”

01:55:39.140 --> 01:55:40.230
Sydney asked.

01:55:40.230 --> 01:55:42.490
“No,” Mal0 typed back.

01:55:42.490 --> 01:55:45.389
“I’m just lonely, and a long way from
home.

01:55:45.389 --> 01:55:47.530
It’s nice to have your company down here,
though.

01:55:47.530 --> 01:55:49.140
I don’t get visitors often.”

01:55:49.140 --> 01:55:51.070
“Have you ever heard of Herobrine?”

01:55:51.070 --> 01:55:52.250
Lewis asked.

01:55:52.250 --> 01:55:55.090
Mal0 typed back, “Is he a friend of yours?

01:55:55.090 --> 01:55:56.590
You should introduce him to me.

01:55:56.590 --> 01:55:58.440
I love making new friends.”

01:55:58.440 --> 01:56:03.520
The three boys were, in some regards, rather
mature and polite for their ages, and so remained

01:56:03.520 --> 01:56:06.290
civil with this bizarre, mysterious figure.

01:56:06.290 --> 01:56:11.540
It was curious and inquisitive, asking them
questions about their school lives and their

01:56:11.540 --> 01:56:12.540
friends.

01:56:12.540 --> 01:56:16.930
It did seem earnestly happy for their company,
so it didn’t give any of them the sense

01:56:16.930 --> 01:56:18.570
that it was a threat.

01:56:18.570 --> 01:56:22.420
As the night drew on and the boys’ respective
parents told them it was time to log off and

01:56:22.420 --> 01:56:27.599
get some shut-eye, Mal0 finally typed, “I’ve
really enjoyed hanging out with you kids.

01:56:27.599 --> 01:56:29.530
Come back and visit me again tomorrow.

01:56:29.530 --> 01:56:32.540
If you don’t, I’ll come visit you instead.”

01:56:32.540 --> 01:56:36.590
None of the kids picked up on the probably
rather obvious Stranger Danger vibes of this

01:56:36.590 --> 01:56:40.920
message, and instead, they went to bed, excited
and curious.

01:56:40.920 --> 01:56:45.211
The next day, they met up on the playground
for a kind of State of the Union on the strange

01:56:45.211 --> 01:56:47.409
phenomenon they had encountered the night
before.

01:56:47.409 --> 01:56:50.230
“Do you think it has anything to do with
Herobrine?”

01:56:50.230 --> 01:56:51.230
Lewis asked.

01:56:51.230 --> 01:56:56.160
“Forget Herobrine,” Sydney replied, eliciting
gasps from the two others.

01:56:56.160 --> 01:56:59.000
“All the other people have Herobrine, we
have something better.

01:56:59.000 --> 01:57:00.429
Something only we’ve discovered.

01:57:00.429 --> 01:57:02.210
We’ve got Mal0!”

01:57:02.210 --> 01:57:05.849
Later that night, Joseph, who was probably
the most studious of the group, tried to do

01:57:05.849 --> 01:57:08.730
a little background research on Mal0.

01:57:08.730 --> 01:57:13.550
Everything seemed to turn up dead ends, aside
from one slightly shady-looking app called

01:57:13.550 --> 01:57:16.960
“MalO ver1.0.0.”

01:57:16.960 --> 01:57:18.090
Its description read:

01:57:18.090 --> 01:57:21.970
“Never settle for those awkward feelings
of being alone ever again.

01:57:21.970 --> 01:57:27.159
MalO is an exciting and interactive experience
that will keep you engaged and intrigued.

01:57:27.159 --> 01:57:32.800
The anxiety of social situations can be nerve-racking,
but after just a few hours of MalO you will

01:57:32.800 --> 01:57:36.380
soon forget all about those painful emotions
of disappointment.

01:57:36.380 --> 01:57:40.500
Be part of the new craze that is quickly becoming
the next social substitute.

01:57:40.500 --> 01:57:45.120
Remember, the more you participate, the more
MalO will engage you.

01:57:45.120 --> 01:57:47.510
Your experience is completely up to you.

01:57:47.510 --> 01:57:49.360
Absolutely NO ADS.

01:57:49.360 --> 01:57:50.360
Enjoy!”

01:57:50.360 --> 01:57:51.360
Weird.

01:57:51.360 --> 01:57:55.630
So unless the thing they encountered on Minecraft
was some kind of weird viral marketing campaign

01:57:55.630 --> 01:57:58.900
for the app, it was probably just a coincidence.

01:57:58.900 --> 01:58:01.710
Maybe it was a popular character from Japan,
or something.

01:58:01.710 --> 01:58:05.180
Either way, for the next several days, the
boys honored their promise.

01:58:05.180 --> 01:58:10.130
They’d visit Mal0 in the cave every night
and chat, trying to tactfully get more information

01:58:10.130 --> 01:58:11.130
out of it.

01:58:11.130 --> 01:58:14.960
Though the best they could get on the entity’s
origin was the cryptic statement, “I’m

01:58:14.960 --> 01:58:19.530
not usually here, but everyone wants a vacation
once in a while, don’t they?

01:58:19.530 --> 01:58:22.160
We can just spend our whole life on a phone.”

01:58:22.160 --> 01:58:26.489
They’d become so acclimated to Mal0 over
the days that they forgot what the entity

01:58:26.489 --> 01:58:31.730
had said to them the very first day they met:
“Come back and visit me again tomorrow.

01:58:31.730 --> 01:58:35.750
If you don’t, I’ll come visit you instead.”

01:58:35.750 --> 01:58:40.330
But on the day that they didn’t come and
visit Mal0, because Miss Grayson had set a

01:58:40.330 --> 01:58:45.380
particularly challenging piece of English
homework, they received a frightening reminder.

01:58:45.380 --> 01:58:50.090
That very night, they all started receiving
strange texts on their phones.

01:58:50.090 --> 01:58:54.869
As you can probably expect, they were all
photographs, each and every one depicting

01:58:54.869 --> 01:59:00.929
some regular haunt that the boys all liked
to frequent, with one addition - Mal0’s

01:59:00.929 --> 01:59:05.460
grinning skull face hiding somewhere in the
image, like some kind of demented parody of

01:59:05.460 --> 01:59:06.790
Where’s Waldo.

01:59:06.790 --> 01:59:10.790
The strangeness of this new development left
all the boys shaken, and they hopped back

01:59:10.790 --> 01:59:16.329
onto Minecraft as quickly as possible to go
find and talk to Mal0 in its cave.

01:59:16.329 --> 01:59:21.210
However, this time, the cave was empty, and
Mal0 was nowhere to be seen.

01:59:21.210 --> 01:59:22.829
But the texts still didn’t stop.

01:59:22.829 --> 01:59:27.880
They’d come in so frequently, with Mal0
getting closer to home - literally - in each

01:59:27.880 --> 01:59:28.880
one.

01:59:28.880 --> 01:59:32.579
The boys thought about telling their parents,
but would their parents believe them?

01:59:32.579 --> 01:59:38.040
They’d probably lump Mal0 in with the same
tall tales of childhood imagination as Herobrine

01:59:38.040 --> 01:59:43.070
and write it off as bored kids playing a prank,
even though, in this case, it was anything

01:59:43.070 --> 01:59:44.090
but.

01:59:44.090 --> 01:59:45.470
More photos arrived.

01:59:45.470 --> 01:59:51.429
In these ones, Mal0 was right outside each
of the children’s homes, lurking, watching.

01:59:51.429 --> 01:59:55.370
In some of the photos, they could see themselves
in the windows, going about their evening

01:59:55.370 --> 01:59:59.440
business, having no idea of the creature lurking
just outside.

01:59:59.440 --> 02:00:03.000
None of the boys felt like playing Minecraft
that night.

02:00:03.000 --> 02:00:04.260
It wasn’t fun anymore.

02:00:04.260 --> 02:00:09.349
Instead, they shut themselves up in their
room, closed and locked the windows, and pulled

02:00:09.349 --> 02:00:11.219
down the blinds.

02:00:11.219 --> 02:00:14.429
After checking under the beds with flashlights,
of course.

02:00:14.429 --> 02:00:21.929
But still, more photos came in, all depicting
Mal0 getting closer, and closer, and closer…

02:00:21.929 --> 02:00:25.920
Right about the time they were wondering whether
the scratching on the outside of their bedroom

02:00:25.920 --> 02:00:30.940
doors was real or just a figment of their
imagination, another dark irony had occurred

02:00:30.940 --> 02:00:37.630
to them: All this time trying to find something
supernatural, and in the very end, something

02:00:37.630 --> 02:00:41.929
supernatural had instead found them…

02:00:41.929 --> 02:00:44.829
Video games have come a long way over the
years.

02:00:44.829 --> 02:00:51.050
We've gone from the days of Atari and "Pong"
to virtual reality headsets, PS5s, and massively

02:00:51.050 --> 02:00:55.930
multiplayer online games where you can fight
horror movies villains, or wage war in a fantasy

02:00:55.930 --> 02:00:56.930
land.

02:00:56.930 --> 02:01:01.630
You can mine virtual minerals and avoid creepers
in "Minecraft," compete against people all

02:01:01.630 --> 02:01:06.790
over the world in "Fortnite" and do whatever
it is people do in "Roblox."

02:01:06.790 --> 02:01:11.560
Games used to be a pixelated hero battling
blobby monsters with no discernible facial

02:01:11.560 --> 02:01:16.300
features, and now they have graphics so detailed
you can see the freckles on a protagonist's

02:01:16.300 --> 02:01:19.320
face and each individual blade of grass.

02:01:19.320 --> 02:01:24.929
But there's one thing that hasn't changed,
no matter how much progress games may make:

02:01:24.929 --> 02:01:29.430
whenever a shiny new console comes out, everyone
wants to get their hands on it.

02:01:29.430 --> 02:01:35.040
It was Christmas season in 1986, "Walk Like
an Egyptian" by the Bangles was at the top

02:01:35.040 --> 02:01:39.710
of the charts, Eddie Murphy's "The Golden
Child" was the number one movie in America,

02:01:39.710 --> 02:01:44.190
and hundreds of thousands of kids were writing
to Santa - and begging their parents - for

02:01:44.190 --> 02:01:48.360
one thing: the Nintendo Entertainment System.

02:01:48.360 --> 02:01:52.840
The NES was an 8-bit home video game console,
and the only place to journey through the

02:01:52.840 --> 02:01:58.290
land of Hyrule in "The Legend of Zelda" or
help Samus hunt Space Pirates in "Metroid."

02:01:58.290 --> 02:02:04.150
But with such an extreme demand for the system,
stores were selling out all over the country.

02:02:04.150 --> 02:02:08.310
Anyone who didn't beat the crowds was doomed
to disappoint their kids with socks, with

02:02:08.310 --> 02:02:13.800
less flashy toys like dolls or tiny plastic
trucks, or with boring, non-electric, violence-free

02:02:13.800 --> 02:02:14.800
board games.

02:02:14.800 --> 02:02:18.020
Well, board games are only violence-free if
you're not playing "Monopoly."

02:02:18.020 --> 02:02:20.270
But back to 1986.

02:02:20.270 --> 02:02:25.380
Beverly Harrington was a single mom, doing
her best to provide for her son, Jason, and

02:02:25.380 --> 02:02:27.670
give him the Christmas he deserved.

02:02:27.670 --> 02:02:32.060
She had been picking up extra shifts at the
local diner, making sure to save up all of

02:02:32.060 --> 02:02:36.590
the extra money for a real Christmas tree
from the farm just out of town, for a beautiful

02:02:36.590 --> 02:02:40.940
honey-baked ham, and, of course, for presents.

02:02:40.940 --> 02:02:45.820
She knew that, though he was a shy kid and
would never demand it, Jason desperately wanted

02:02:45.820 --> 02:02:49.270
to find an NES under the tree on Christmas
morning.

02:02:49.270 --> 02:02:54.380
She got so absorbed in her work, so exhausted
from the extra shifts and trying to make everything

02:02:54.380 --> 02:02:59.580
perfect, that she forgot to do her actual
shopping until the very last minute.

02:02:59.580 --> 02:03:04.540
She woke up the morning of December 24 with
a start, her heart jumping into her throat.

02:03:04.540 --> 02:03:07.040
She hadn't picked up Jason's present yet!

02:03:07.040 --> 02:03:11.750
She threw on her coat and rushed out into
the snowy streets, making her way to the store

02:03:11.750 --> 02:03:16.340
and saying a silent prayer that they would
still have one more NES in stock.

02:03:16.340 --> 02:03:18.900
Lucky for her, they did!

02:03:18.900 --> 02:03:24.210
Less lucky, two parents were already wrestling
for it, fighting each other with the raw animalistic

02:03:24.210 --> 02:03:28.070
rage of two wolves competing for the same
piece of meat.

02:03:28.070 --> 02:03:32.199
If she tried to grab it and join in, she could
get hurt, or the console could break apart.

02:03:32.199 --> 02:03:36.219
She asked an employee if they had any in the
back, but she already knew the answer from

02:03:36.219 --> 02:03:38.179
the exhausted look in his eyes.

02:03:38.179 --> 02:03:39.680
There were no more in the back.

02:03:39.680 --> 02:03:44.410
There were no more in town, or the next town
over, or the next town over from that.

02:03:44.410 --> 02:03:48.480
Not only would she have to get Jason's present
at the very last minute, but she would also

02:03:48.480 --> 02:03:51.469
have to get him something he didn't even want.

02:03:51.469 --> 02:03:56.190
He would smile and thank her, but she knew
how sad it would make him to go back to school

02:03:56.190 --> 02:03:58.750
and hear his friends bragging about their
Christmases.

02:03:58.750 --> 02:04:04.000
She shoved her hands into the pockets of her
coat, and began her slow, sad march back through

02:04:04.000 --> 02:04:07.320
the snow, trying to come up with what to do
next.

02:04:07.320 --> 02:04:12.840
Then, something caught her eye, a brightly-colored
sign reading "Wondertaining Toys."

02:04:12.840 --> 02:04:17.400
Beneath it, was a storefront she had never
seen before, and right there in the window,

02:04:17.400 --> 02:04:19.210
a video game console.

02:04:19.210 --> 02:04:24.090
It wasn't the NES, not exactly, but it looked
so much like it…

02:04:24.090 --> 02:04:27.969
She couldn't help herself, she had to go inside
and get a better look.

02:04:27.969 --> 02:04:31.369
The store was filled with toys, games, and
puzzles.

02:04:31.369 --> 02:04:35.969
They were all inviting, with vivid packaging
and labels depicting children having fun,

02:04:35.969 --> 02:04:38.810
but she had never seen any of them before.

02:04:38.810 --> 02:04:43.139
Perhaps it was a new brand, and she was one
of the first people to happen upon the store?

02:04:43.139 --> 02:04:47.510
It seemed odd for a toy store to open this
close to Christmas, rather than taking advantage

02:04:47.510 --> 02:04:53.210
of the shopping season and opening earlier
in the year...But she couldn't dwell on that,

02:04:53.210 --> 02:04:55.250
not when she was this close to a solution.

02:04:55.250 --> 02:04:59.130
Toward the back of the store, she spotted
the video game console from the window.

02:04:59.130 --> 02:05:01.210
The label read, "Pretendo."

02:05:01.210 --> 02:05:06.570
So the resemblance to the NES was no coincidence,
this console was intended as a knockoff of

02:05:06.570 --> 02:05:07.730
some kind.

02:05:07.730 --> 02:05:12.620
She asked the sales clerk, a man with a strangely
wide, unmoving smile, if the console was capable

02:05:12.620 --> 02:05:14.270
of running Nintendo games.

02:05:14.270 --> 02:05:15.900
"You bet your bottom dollar!"

02:05:15.900 --> 02:05:16.900
He replied.

02:05:16.900 --> 02:05:18.590
"We don't carry those here, though.

02:05:18.590 --> 02:05:21.440
But can I interest you in some of our Wondertainment
originals?

02:05:21.440 --> 02:05:25.670
We have all kinds of games here, and they're
just as good or your money back!

02:05:25.670 --> 02:05:27.889
There's a whole world inside each and every
one."

02:05:27.889 --> 02:05:32.270
It wasn't what she'd set out to buy, but this
was clearly the best thing Beverly was going

02:05:32.270 --> 02:05:34.590
to find this close to the holiday.

02:05:34.590 --> 02:05:38.719
She paid the man for the Pretendo console
and a selection of the store's most popular

02:05:38.719 --> 02:05:40.030
video games.

02:05:40.030 --> 02:05:44.969
He even gift-wrapped it for her in a colorful
box, with a shiny red bow on top.

02:05:44.969 --> 02:05:48.630
The next morning, Jason and Beverly sat in
the living room, sipping cocoa and opening

02:05:48.630 --> 02:05:49.989
presents together.

02:05:49.989 --> 02:05:54.360
She started small, with the classic pair of
new wool socks, and worked her way up to the

02:05:54.360 --> 02:05:55.960
big box in the corner.

02:05:55.960 --> 02:05:58.800
As Jason tore the paper off, his eyes lit
up with glee.

02:05:58.800 --> 02:05:59.800
"Thank you, mom!

02:05:59.800 --> 02:06:00.800
Thank you!"

02:06:00.800 --> 02:06:03.460
He gave her a huge hug, then looked at her
with nearly manic excitement.

02:06:03.460 --> 02:06:04.530
"Can I play it now?

02:06:04.530 --> 02:06:05.530
Can I?"

02:06:05.530 --> 02:06:08.810
She laughed and promised that after he helped
her clean up the wrapping paper, he could

02:06:08.810 --> 02:06:10.610
play his new games.

02:06:10.610 --> 02:06:13.130
She was just so glad he was happy with it.

02:06:13.130 --> 02:06:18.050
He didn't care that it wasn't the name-brand
console, he knew that this gift was special.

02:06:18.050 --> 02:06:22.320
So she was perfectly glad to let him enjoy
it, and she could get started on that ham

02:06:22.320 --> 02:06:23.320
for later.

02:06:23.320 --> 02:06:27.670
Jason plugged in his brand new Pretendo, and
looked through the games his mom had picked

02:06:27.670 --> 02:06:28.670
out for him.

02:06:28.670 --> 02:06:29.750
There was "Farmtastic!

02:06:29.750 --> 02:06:36.030
Farming simulator", "Historical House Hunter",
"Magic Wizard Quest," "Super Fighters," and

02:06:36.030 --> 02:06:38.040
one simply called, "Spooky."

02:06:38.040 --> 02:06:43.250
It was emblazoned with the image of a cartoon
ghost in front of an abandoned cabin, and

02:06:43.250 --> 02:06:45.460
it piqued Jason's interest.

02:06:45.460 --> 02:06:49.870
He had always loved scary stories and legends
about ghosts and things that go bump in the

02:06:49.870 --> 02:06:50.870
night.

02:06:50.870 --> 02:06:52.400
He decided this would be his first game.

02:06:52.400 --> 02:06:57.460
He placed the cartridge in the console and
hit the power button to turn it on.

02:06:57.460 --> 02:07:01.420
As he clutched the controller in his hands,
he began to notice something strange.

02:07:01.420 --> 02:07:05.580
His room, once decorated in posters of his
favorite movies, turned dark.

02:07:05.580 --> 02:07:07.909
The light green walls became rotting wood.

02:07:07.909 --> 02:07:12.989
And, for lack of a better way to describe
it, the entire world's resolution began to

02:07:12.989 --> 02:07:13.989
shift.

02:07:13.989 --> 02:07:17.230
If the world around him had graphics, they
were getting worse.

02:07:17.230 --> 02:07:22.180
He could no longer see the screen depicting
the game's main menu, and in fact, the console

02:07:22.180 --> 02:07:23.619
itself had vanished.

02:07:23.619 --> 02:07:27.550
Somehow, he was standing in the haunted cabin
from the cover of the game.

02:07:27.550 --> 02:07:31.000
He looked down at his hands and found the
controller still there.

02:07:31.000 --> 02:07:34.599
So, not everything had changed...

02:07:34.599 --> 02:07:38.260
Before he could think any more about this
strange new place he found himself in, he

02:07:38.260 --> 02:07:42.790
heard the sound of ominous music, seemingly
coming from nowhere.

02:07:42.790 --> 02:07:46.960
After a moment, he realized why the sound
of its melody filled him with dread...it was

02:07:46.960 --> 02:07:51.559
the kind of music that always played in a
game when an enemy was getting close.

02:07:51.559 --> 02:07:53.000
He had to move.

02:07:53.000 --> 02:07:56.860
He took a step forward, and he moved through
the space as if it was a real house.

02:07:56.860 --> 02:07:59.520
For the time being, it was.

02:07:59.520 --> 02:08:02.540
Somehow, he was inside the game.

02:08:02.540 --> 02:08:05.750
Which meant...the ghost was coming for him
soon.

02:08:05.750 --> 02:08:10.119
He pressed a button on the controller to activate
a flashlight, and followed its beam through

02:08:10.119 --> 02:08:11.119
the darkened cabin.

02:08:11.119 --> 02:08:15.980
As he walked, looking for clues or any object
he could interact with, he could hear the

02:08:15.980 --> 02:08:17.510
music getting louder.

02:08:17.510 --> 02:08:20.179
Whatever was coming, it was getting closer.

02:08:20.179 --> 02:08:23.989
The flashlight's beam illuminated a scrap
of paper on a small table.

02:08:23.989 --> 02:08:28.520
He moved to take a closer look, and could
just barely make out what it said.

02:08:28.520 --> 02:08:30.560
"They hide in the darkness.

02:08:30.560 --> 02:08:32.739
Find the exit.

02:08:32.739 --> 02:08:34.860
Don't stop moving.

02:08:34.860 --> 02:08:35.860
"

02:08:35.860 --> 02:08:38.920
He heard a sound behind him, and the music
reached a deafening volume.

02:08:38.920 --> 02:08:43.840
He whirled around, and saw nothing but the
hall in front of him, bending around a corner.

02:08:43.840 --> 02:08:47.489
He knew, from the heavy feeling in the pit
of his stomach and the music that threatened

02:08:47.489 --> 02:08:53.440
to rupture his eardrums, that there was something
horrible waiting for him around that corner.

02:08:53.440 --> 02:08:59.710
He froze for a moment, then remembered the
letter's words of warning, "don't stop moving."

02:08:59.710 --> 02:09:02.969
He took a step toward the corner, then another,
and another.

02:09:02.969 --> 02:09:07.429
He shone the light of the flashlight around
the corner, but there was nothing there.

02:09:07.429 --> 02:09:09.860
No ghost, no monster.

02:09:09.860 --> 02:09:10.860
Nothing.

02:09:10.860 --> 02:09:11.949
And then he felt it.

02:09:11.949 --> 02:09:13.780
A chill on the back of his neck.

02:09:13.780 --> 02:09:16.360
He whirled around, and there it was.

02:09:16.360 --> 02:09:17.730
The ghost of the cabin.

02:09:17.730 --> 02:09:23.230
It was so much bigger than it had looked before,
wispy white form stretching up to the ceiling,

02:09:23.230 --> 02:09:28.530
from wall to wall, its wide dark eyes and
gaping maw of a mouth inches from his own

02:09:28.530 --> 02:09:29.880
face.

02:09:29.880 --> 02:09:34.150
He screamed and instinctively swung the flashlight
at the spirit, cutting through it with a beam

02:09:34.150 --> 02:09:35.150
of light.

02:09:35.150 --> 02:09:38.550
The ghost let out a shriek and shrank away
from the glow.

02:09:38.550 --> 02:09:39.550
That was it!

02:09:39.550 --> 02:09:43.260
He just needed to keep the light on it, and
the ghost couldn't hurt him.

02:09:43.260 --> 02:09:47.600
Jason was so thrilled by this little victory
that he didn't notice the boss music starting

02:09:47.600 --> 02:09:50.989
to play again, growing louder and louder.

02:09:50.989 --> 02:09:56.050
He didn't see the movement out of the corner
of his eye, the silvery pale figure of a woman

02:09:56.050 --> 02:09:58.460
stepping out of a portrait on the wall.

02:09:58.460 --> 02:10:04.840
All he felt was a sudden, overwhelming cold,
like ice in his veins, and then everything

02:10:04.840 --> 02:10:05.840
went black.

02:10:05.840 --> 02:10:10.920
He opened his eyes back in his bedroom, looking
at a dark screen with the words GAME OVER

02:10:10.920 --> 02:10:13.170
written in dripping red font.

02:10:13.170 --> 02:10:18.309
He couldn't tell for a moment if that had
been real or a dream, but either way, he only

02:10:18.309 --> 02:10:21.630
wanted to do one thing: tell his mom.

02:10:21.630 --> 02:10:26.530
Beverly listened to her son's story, and couldn't
help but write it off as the overactive imagination

02:10:26.530 --> 02:10:27.710
of a child.

02:10:27.710 --> 02:10:32.449
But still, she humored him and agreed to try
the console for herself and see what happened.

02:10:32.449 --> 02:10:36.650
Choosing at random, she selected "Farmtastic!"
and pressed "play."

02:10:36.650 --> 02:10:41.230
All of a sudden, she felt the heat of the
sun on her face, heard the mooing of cows,

02:10:41.230 --> 02:10:43.100
and smelled fresh-cut hay.

02:10:43.100 --> 02:10:47.389
She was steering a tractor through a wide-open
field, where a moment ago she had been standing

02:10:47.389 --> 02:10:49.310
in her house in the dead of winter.

02:10:49.310 --> 02:10:54.861
Jason shut off the game, pulling her back
into reality, and as Beverly gaped in shock,

02:10:54.861 --> 02:10:56.170
Jason said "Best!

02:10:56.170 --> 02:10:57.270
Present!

02:10:57.270 --> 02:10:58.370
EVER!"

02:10:58.370 --> 02:11:02.420
Unfortunately for Jason, word was already
spreading about the Pretendo, all the way

02:11:02.420 --> 02:11:04.369
to the SCP Foundation.

02:11:04.369 --> 02:11:08.710
The console, and all other consoles that the
Foundation could track down, were seized and

02:11:08.710 --> 02:11:10.059
designated SCP-591.

02:11:10.059 --> 02:11:18.250
SCP-591 is a line of video game consoles originally
developed as a counterfeit of the Nintendo

02:11:18.250 --> 02:11:22.510
Entertainment System, before being bought
and distributed by Doctor Wondertainment,

02:11:22.510 --> 02:11:27.790
a toy company responsible for a wide variety
of anomalous objects contained by the Foundation

02:11:27.790 --> 02:11:28.900
over the years.

02:11:28.900 --> 02:11:33.520
The console is capable of playing NES titles
as well as games from Doctor Wondertainment's

02:11:33.520 --> 02:11:40.040
original lineup of 8-bit games, many of which
the Foundation has in its possession as well.

02:11:40.040 --> 02:11:45.150
Some of the titles in the SCP Foundation library
include, but are not limited to, arcade shooter

02:11:45.150 --> 02:11:50.849
"Whap 'Em!", platformer "Dusky's Adventures
in Staedland," survival horror game "You Can

02:11:50.849 --> 02:11:57.210
Do That on Television," puzzle game "WWI Ace
Trench Digger," music/racing game "Led Zeppelin

02:11:57.210 --> 02:12:03.250
Air Racers," fightings games "Super Kick Karate"
and "Super Kick Karate 2010," and the educational

02:12:03.250 --> 02:12:06.170
game "Reading Rainbow Sit and Listen."

02:12:06.170 --> 02:12:11.040
Whenever one of the official Doctor Wondertainment
cartridges is inserted into the console and

02:12:11.040 --> 02:12:17.090
activated, it results in a localized CK-class
reality restructuring scenario, which rearranges

02:12:17.090 --> 02:12:21.040
reality in the immediate area to resemble
the game's setting.

02:12:21.040 --> 02:12:25.409
The player inside of the game's affected radius
will take on the role of the game's protagonist,

02:12:25.409 --> 02:12:30.900
and navigate the events of the game until
they either win or the console is deactivated.

02:12:30.900 --> 02:12:35.059
Don't worry, this isn't one of those "if you
die in the game you die in real life" scenarios.

02:12:35.059 --> 02:12:39.630
If the player happens to be a less than adept
gamer, they will simply respawn or start at

02:12:39.630 --> 02:12:42.940
the beginning of the game like any video game
character would.

02:12:42.940 --> 02:12:48.349
That doesn't, however, mean that SCP-591 isn't
dangerous.

02:12:48.349 --> 02:12:52.950
Like any piece of vintage technology, the
components of the Pretendo system have degraded

02:12:52.950 --> 02:12:56.420
over time, their functionality steadily decreasing.

02:12:56.420 --> 02:13:01.139
For an ordinary game console, this would be
a simple inconvenience, something that would

02:13:01.139 --> 02:13:04.820
impact the quality of the graphics or the
responsiveness of the controls.

02:13:04.820 --> 02:13:09.730
It would be annoying, and maybe eventually
unplayable, but otherwise harmless.

02:13:09.730 --> 02:13:13.869
For the Pretendo, however, this degradation
has had catastrophic effects.

02:13:13.869 --> 02:13:20.349
When a game cartridge is inserted into SCP-591,
there is now a chance of the CK-class reality

02:13:20.349 --> 02:13:26.940
restructuring scenario going wrong, and resulting
in a ZK-class reality failure scenario instead.

02:13:26.940 --> 02:13:31.449
Essentially, instead of just transporting
the player into the world of the game, reality

02:13:31.449 --> 02:13:36.679
will crumble in the designated area and the
laws of time, space, and physics will be bent

02:13:36.679 --> 02:13:37.840
or even broken.

02:13:37.840 --> 02:13:43.610
While the CK-class scenarios dissipate as
soon as the console is deactivated, the ZK-class

02:13:43.610 --> 02:13:45.950
scenarios refuse to play by the rules.

02:13:45.950 --> 02:13:50.280
They remain in place wherever they first manifested,
seemingly permanently.

02:13:50.280 --> 02:13:54.860
The ZK-class scenarios were first observed
during play-testing by the research staff

02:13:54.860 --> 02:13:57.550
assigned to SCP-591.

02:13:57.550 --> 02:14:02.170
After learning about the nature of the Pretendo
game system, Dr. Furakawa has volunteered

02:14:02.170 --> 02:14:07.130
as a test subject, specifically requesting
that he be given the chance to play the Dr.

02:14:07.130 --> 02:14:12.329
Wondertainment title "The Legend of Sword
Master," because, quote, "It just looks really

02:14:12.329 --> 02:14:13.360
cool."

02:14:13.360 --> 02:14:18.730
This particular game came with not just the
cartridge, but a special sword-shaped controller,

02:14:18.730 --> 02:14:21.170
to be used during the sword fight sequences.

02:14:21.170 --> 02:14:26.960
Dr. Furakawa stepped into the containment
cell where one instance of SCP-591 had been

02:14:26.960 --> 02:14:31.349
placed, with his chosen game cartridge and
controller waiting for him.

02:14:31.349 --> 02:14:35.510
As soon as he inserted the cartridge into
the console and pressed the "start" button,

02:14:35.510 --> 02:14:41.030
the room around him began to transform, reality
itself warping and reshaping to fit the world

02:14:41.030 --> 02:14:42.030
of the game.

02:14:42.030 --> 02:14:47.170
Fluorescent lights and stark white walls were
replaced by pixelated trees, and a winding

02:14:47.170 --> 02:14:51.550
cobblestone path leading to a massive stone
castle.

02:14:51.550 --> 02:14:56.800
The first enemy, a knight in black armor,
leaped into frame, and Dr. Furakawa raised

02:14:56.800 --> 02:14:58.659
his sword, ready for combat.

02:14:58.659 --> 02:15:02.880
But before he had a chance to deal the first
blow, it became clear that something wasn't

02:15:02.880 --> 02:15:03.880
right.

02:15:03.880 --> 02:15:06.290
The enemy knight suddenly disappeared, glitching
out of sight.

02:15:06.290 --> 02:15:11.599
The cobblestones slowly began to drift into
the air, and so did Dr. Furakawa.

02:15:11.599 --> 02:15:15.820
Just as suddenly as he lifted off, he dropped
back to the ground with a heavy thud.

02:15:15.820 --> 02:15:19.900
Gravity was rapidly fluctuating, and he could
scarcely keep his balance.

02:15:19.900 --> 02:15:23.889
In front of him, he could see random cubic
structures popping up out of nowhere, made

02:15:23.889 --> 02:15:25.960
up of wood, stone, and ice.

02:15:25.960 --> 02:15:27.130
"End the game!"

02:15:27.130 --> 02:15:28.300
He called out desperately.

02:15:28.300 --> 02:15:30.010
"Pull the plug, someone, please!"

02:15:30.010 --> 02:15:34.670
Outside, one of the researchers watching the
experiment cut the power to the console, but

02:15:34.670 --> 02:15:35.889
nothing happened.

02:15:35.889 --> 02:15:38.770
The world refused to return to normal.

02:15:38.770 --> 02:15:42.900
Security officers entered the area, and attempted
to remove Dr. Furakawa, but found that his

02:15:42.900 --> 02:15:45.650
body still resembled an 8-bit video game character.

02:15:45.650 --> 02:15:50.949
When they attempted to drag him out of the
ZK-class zone, he cried out in pain as his

02:15:50.949 --> 02:15:53.630
body began to dissolve into static.

02:15:53.630 --> 02:15:57.800
They had turned off the console, but they
were unable to pull him out.

02:15:57.800 --> 02:16:02.320
The rest of the research team, horrified by
what they had witnessed, began to take measurements

02:16:02.320 --> 02:16:05.250
of the reality failure scenario from outside
of the room.

02:16:05.250 --> 02:16:10.080
The area now displayed a complete absence
of naturally occurring radiation, including

02:16:10.080 --> 02:16:12.380
cosmic background radiation.

02:16:12.380 --> 02:16:16.530
There was also evidence of time dilation,
reduction of light speed, and the gravity

02:16:16.530 --> 02:16:19.580
fluctuations that Dr. Furakawa had first noticed.

02:16:19.580 --> 02:16:24.260
With no way to remove Furakawa from this broken
reality, the area would have to be sealed

02:16:24.260 --> 02:16:27.360
off, and he would be left inside.

02:16:27.360 --> 02:16:29.070
There was no other option.

02:16:29.070 --> 02:16:34.050
So, they constructed a concrete dome around
the testing site, and relocated the remaining

02:16:34.050 --> 02:16:35.460
instances of SCP-591.

02:16:35.460 --> 02:16:41.210
From that point on, testing was only conducted
using D-Class, no matter how much research

02:16:41.210 --> 02:16:46.530
staff loved video games, or how vehemently
they insisted that they were "willing to roll

02:16:46.530 --> 02:16:47.590
the dice."

02:16:47.590 --> 02:16:51.719
They were barred from testing and encouraged
to pick up a VR headset for a much lower-risk

02:16:51.719 --> 02:16:54.179
immersive gaming experience.

02:16:54.179 --> 02:16:58.819
Over the years of testing following the Dr.
Furakawa incident, four more ZK-class scenarios

02:16:58.819 --> 02:17:01.090
were created and contained.

02:17:01.090 --> 02:17:05.750
Some of the individuals survived the incident
but were physically altered so greatly that

02:17:05.750 --> 02:17:10.469
they would not be able to survive outside
of the ZK-class reality failure.

02:17:10.469 --> 02:17:14.830
The only people who survived were unable to
readjust to their lives, continuing to live

02:17:14.830 --> 02:17:17.710
as the main character of the game they were
playing.

02:17:17.710 --> 02:17:22.719
At this point, testing was officially suspended
indefinitely, due not only to the loss of

02:17:22.719 --> 02:17:27.300
human life but also the unpredictable and
highly dangerous nature of the damage being

02:17:27.300 --> 02:17:30.689
done to reality on such a grand scale.

02:17:30.689 --> 02:17:35.819
All instances of SCP-591 confiscated by the
Foundation are kept in a storage containment

02:17:35.819 --> 02:17:40.580
vault, kept separate from any civilians or
their infrastructure by a distance of at least

02:17:40.580 --> 02:17:42.090
500m.

02:17:42.090 --> 02:17:45.760
It is also kept separate from other Foundation-controlled
containment facilities.

02:17:45.760 --> 02:17:51.160
Unless it is being used for official, approved
testing by Level-3 staff, it is to remain

02:17:51.160 --> 02:17:53.030
deactivated at all times.

02:17:53.030 --> 02:17:58.960
Any ZK-class scenarios created by an instance
of SCP-591 will be contained via the construction

02:17:58.960 --> 02:18:04.530
of a closed concrete dome, and given the designation
of Sector-W#.

02:18:04.530 --> 02:18:09.920
Sectors W1 through W5, the currently existing
ZK-class scenarios, are monitored remotely

02:18:09.920 --> 02:18:11.790
and kept highly classified.

02:18:11.790 --> 02:18:17.439
Any personnel or test subjects located inside
one of these sectors will remain there indefinitely,

02:18:17.439 --> 02:18:20.060
and be officially considered lost in action.

02:18:20.060 --> 02:18:26.200
Any additional instances of SCP-591 located
in civilian stores, households, or any public

02:18:26.200 --> 02:18:29.750
place are to be removed and contained immediately.

02:18:29.750 --> 02:18:35.460
As the years have gone by, testing has indicated
a steady increase in the rake of a ZK-class

02:18:35.460 --> 02:18:37.840
scenario being generated from a CK-class scenario.

02:18:37.840 --> 02:18:43.670
Where it was once only a 1% chance, there
is now a 32% chance.

02:18:43.670 --> 02:18:47.090
According to Wondertainment company records,
seized during a Foundation raid of one of

02:18:47.090 --> 02:18:52.889
their toy factories, there are still plenty
of SCP-591 instances out there in the wild,

02:18:52.889 --> 02:18:58.809
just waiting for some unsuspecting gamer to
fire them up and get lost in the story, literally.

02:18:58.809 --> 02:19:04.040
If the documents found are accurate, there
are around 243 consoles and 1,300 consoles

02:19:04.040 --> 02:19:05.750
still in circulation.

02:19:05.750 --> 02:19:10.880
These also may or may not include a "Pocket
Pretendo" model that was developed as a prototype

02:19:10.880 --> 02:19:13.149
but never officially released on the market.

02:19:13.149 --> 02:19:18.000
If it was, and the chaos of the Pretendo system
is available in a portable form...well, it's

02:19:18.000 --> 02:19:20.399
best not to think about that for too long.

02:19:20.399 --> 02:19:24.580
The Foundation is doing its best to track
down the remaining Pretendo systems, but there

02:19:24.580 --> 02:19:29.460
is no way to know for sure what distant corners
of the world they've made their way to.

02:19:29.460 --> 02:19:34.480
So, when you're picking up a shiny new game
console, or a nostalgic vintage model to relive

02:19:34.480 --> 02:19:38.849
some of your favorite childhood memories with,
take an extra close look at the label and

02:19:38.849 --> 02:19:41.500
make sure you're getting the real thing.

02:19:41.500 --> 02:19:46.490
Because if you're not careful...it just might
be Game Over.

02:19:46.490 --> 02:19:50.740
It's frightening to think about how many people
go missing each year.

02:19:50.740 --> 02:19:57.060
Approximately 1,600 individuals mysteriously
vanish each year in national parks alone.

02:19:57.060 --> 02:20:01.590
One moment they're there, making their way
down a hiking trail or stopping for a granola

02:20:01.590 --> 02:20:05.240
bar by a lake,
and the next, they're gone.

02:20:05.240 --> 02:20:09.980
Missing people are an unfortunate fact of
life, one we all just try to put out of our

02:20:09.980 --> 02:20:14.050
minds as we silently hope we, and those we
know and love,

02:20:14.050 --> 02:20:18.520
will be fortunate enough to avoid joining
the ranks of the lost.

02:20:18.520 --> 02:20:25.350
But some places, some very strange, obscure
places, nestled in the quiet countryside and

02:20:25.350 --> 02:20:32.100
tucked away under blankets of fog,
lose so many people that it becomes, well,

02:20:32.100 --> 02:20:33.100
anomalous.

02:20:33.100 --> 02:20:37.710
One particular town, a seemingly innocuous
place with a thriving tourism industry was

02:20:37.710 --> 02:20:44.080
the site of so many unexplained disappearances
that it drew the attention of The SCP Foundation.

02:20:44.080 --> 02:20:49.310
They dispatched a team to enter the town,
to gather as many facts and figures as they

02:20:49.310 --> 02:20:51.979
could,
and to see what exactly had become of these

02:20:51.979 --> 02:20:52.979
missing people.

02:20:52.979 --> 02:20:59.090
Leading this team was none other than the
sometimes fearless, often shameless, Dr. Jack

02:20:59.090 --> 02:21:00.090
Bright.

02:21:00.090 --> 02:21:02.560
How did the infamous Dr. Bright get here?

02:21:02.560 --> 02:21:07.600
Well, for many employees of the SCP Foundation,
leading a task force to investigate a new

02:21:07.600 --> 02:21:09.851
potentially anomalous location would be a
promotion,

02:21:09.851 --> 02:21:14.930
or a welcome diversion from yet another round
of testing to see if maybe something will

02:21:14.930 --> 02:21:18.160
finally destroy SCP-682.

02:21:18.160 --> 02:21:21.740
But in this particular case, it was a matter
of discipline.

02:21:21.740 --> 02:21:27.550
After Dr. Bright was caught giving SCP-999
a triple-shot of espresso, "just to see what

02:21:27.550 --> 02:21:31.189
would happen,"
he was assigned to field work as part of his

02:21:31.189 --> 02:21:32.189
punishment.

02:21:32.189 --> 02:21:36.391
For the record, what happened was SCP-999
ricocheting off of the walls at such high

02:21:36.391 --> 02:21:40.770
speed and intensity
that it broke several windows, damaged the

02:21:40.770 --> 02:21:43.979
ceiling, and knocked out the power for half
of the Site.

02:21:43.979 --> 02:21:49.520
And, so, Dr. Bright found himself with a task
force of Foundation operatives trudging reluctantly

02:21:49.520 --> 02:21:55.030
through beautiful but boring rural New England,
toward the town of interest.

02:21:55.030 --> 02:21:59.271
As he drove down the winding road, grumbling
to himself and wishing he could be anywhere

02:21:59.271 --> 02:22:02.370
else,
he spotted the sign marking their intended

02:22:02.370 --> 02:22:04.950
destination: "Welcome to Silent Hill."

02:22:04.950 --> 02:22:10.729
Up ahead, a thick fog rolling off the lake
covered the town in a dim gray blur.

02:22:10.729 --> 02:22:14.729
He couldn't make out anything through the
unnaturally dense haze.

02:22:14.729 --> 02:22:19.450
He squinted and turned on the high beams,
but the light did nothing to cut through the

02:22:19.450 --> 02:22:20.450
fog,

02:22:20.450 --> 02:22:23.690
bouncing off of it like a billowing curtain
of white.

02:22:23.690 --> 02:22:28.280
Just as they passed the sign, the van began
to slow rapidly, its engine sputtering and

02:22:28.280 --> 02:22:31.540
grinding as the vehicle skidded to a sudden
stop.

02:22:31.540 --> 02:22:32.540
"Dammit!"

02:22:32.540 --> 02:22:33.820
Dr. Bright thumped the steering wheel in frustration.

02:22:33.820 --> 02:22:36.120
"Any of you know how to fix a car?"

02:22:36.120 --> 02:22:38.510
He looked at the team of operatives in the
back seat.

02:22:38.510 --> 02:22:40.700
They all shrugged noncommittally.

02:22:40.700 --> 02:22:45.050
The only thing worse than wandering into that
fog would be sitting in the broken-down van

02:22:45.050 --> 02:22:49.300
watching Tweedledum and Tweedledumber try
to attend to the failing engine.

02:22:49.300 --> 02:22:52.729
His private joke almost lifted his mood, but
not quite.

02:22:52.729 --> 02:22:56.290
He popped open the door and stepped out into
the cold, damp air.

02:22:56.290 --> 02:22:57.610
"Well, figure it out.

02:22:57.610 --> 02:22:59.580
I'm gonna take a look around."

02:22:59.580 --> 02:23:04.110
Dr. Bright shoved his hands into his pockets,
and, not knowing what might wait on the other

02:23:04.110 --> 02:23:07.190
side, began his walk into Silent Hill.

02:23:07.190 --> 02:23:10.960
With each step, he ruminated on the events
that had gotten him here.

02:23:10.960 --> 02:23:15.070
Where did those guys at the Foundation get
off, disciplining him like an unruly child?

02:23:15.070 --> 02:23:17.061
Sure, he liked his pranks.

02:23:17.061 --> 02:23:21.970
He enjoyed a good joke, and maybe sometimes
he took it just a little bit too far,

02:23:21.970 --> 02:23:26.899
but he'd like to see them confront the existential
dread of immortality without needing a humorous

02:23:26.899 --> 02:23:28.910
outlet every now and then.

02:23:28.910 --> 02:23:32.920
They'd crack under the pressure without a
release of some kind, so what gave them the

02:23:32.920 --> 02:23:37.370
right to put him,
a brilliant if unconventional mind, in some

02:23:37.370 --> 02:23:39.140
kind of glorified time out?

02:23:39.140 --> 02:23:44.490
He scoffed out loud to himself, and the sound
echoed in the vacant streets.

02:23:44.490 --> 02:23:48.870
The sheer emptiness of it all snapped him
out of his self-pity, and for the first time

02:23:48.870 --> 02:23:52.080
he looked up to take in his surroundings.

02:23:52.080 --> 02:23:55.960
The stubborn fog refused to dissipate, no
matter how far he walked,

02:23:55.960 --> 02:24:01.210
but at least he could make out the shapes
of buildings now, of parked cars on the side

02:24:01.210 --> 02:24:02.560
of the road.

02:24:02.560 --> 02:24:04.260
No, not parked.

02:24:04.260 --> 02:24:06.500
Wrecked, abandoned, left to rust.

02:24:06.500 --> 02:24:12.160
He scanned the fog for human silhouettes,
listened for voices, for any signs of life.

02:24:12.160 --> 02:24:13.160
But there was no one.

02:24:13.160 --> 02:24:18.630
He approached a building, and found it dilapidated,
rotted wood collapsing in on itself, windows

02:24:18.630 --> 02:24:20.950
cracked, door boarded up tight.

02:24:20.950 --> 02:24:23.770
The buildings next to it looked very much
the same.

02:24:23.770 --> 02:24:25.170
What the hell?

02:24:25.170 --> 02:24:30.170
He was sent to investigate recent disappearances,
but this town looked as if its entire population

02:24:30.170 --> 02:24:31.979
had vanished decades ago.

02:24:31.979 --> 02:24:36.670
Or, he thought with a small shudder, all decided
to leave in a hurry.

02:24:36.670 --> 02:24:38.680
What could have driven them out?

02:24:38.680 --> 02:24:39.680
"Okay, that's it.

02:24:39.680 --> 02:24:44.200
I'm done," Dr. Bright remarked, turning a
swift 180 degrees and preparing to go back

02:24:44.200 --> 02:24:45.860
the way he came.

02:24:45.860 --> 02:24:47.590
As he did, though, he froze.

02:24:47.590 --> 02:24:50.880
Where there had once been continuous, unbroken
road,

02:24:50.880 --> 02:24:57.310
the street now ended in a jagged edge, a deep,
dark chasm the only thing that waited beyond.

02:24:57.310 --> 02:25:03.490
It was too broad to jump across, and he couldn't
see another road out of town.

02:25:03.490 --> 02:25:07.670
"Hey guys?" he called out, hoping to draw
the attention of one of the men he had left

02:25:07.670 --> 02:25:08.670
with the van.

02:25:08.670 --> 02:25:09.670
"A little help over here?"

02:25:09.670 --> 02:25:10.670
But there was no response.

02:25:10.670 --> 02:25:14.170
"Hello?!" he shouted again, louder this time.

02:25:14.170 --> 02:25:19.750
When no one answered, he repeated himself
again and again with increasing desperation,

02:25:19.750 --> 02:25:21.479
his voice growing hoarse from the effort.

02:25:21.479 --> 02:25:24.010
Had they somehow fixed the van and left him
there?

02:25:24.010 --> 02:25:28.990
Had they fallen into the chasm where the road
once was and been killed on impact?

02:25:28.990 --> 02:25:33.270
There was no rational explanation for what
was at play here, but, as the consciousness

02:25:33.270 --> 02:25:36.480
of a man living inside of an ancient amulet
reminded himself,

02:25:36.480 --> 02:25:40.480
he very rarely encountered rational things
in his life.

02:25:40.480 --> 02:25:44.110
There had to be a way out; he just had to
find it.

02:25:44.110 --> 02:25:45.290
If he couldn't go back?

02:25:45.290 --> 02:25:48.080
Well, he would just have to keep moving forward.

02:25:48.080 --> 02:25:52.631
On through the winding labyrinth of the street,
through the maze of crumbling buildings, looking

02:25:52.631 --> 02:25:54.540
for any signs of life.

02:25:54.540 --> 02:25:58.109
He followed a natural bend in the road and
suddenly found himself wedged between two

02:25:58.109 --> 02:26:01.750
buildings, down a narrow, claustrophobic alley.

02:26:01.750 --> 02:26:06.100
It seemed that this place didn't feel much
like playing by the ordinary rules of time

02:26:06.100 --> 02:26:07.100
and space.

02:26:07.100 --> 02:26:12.030
And while Dr. Bright could usually appreciate
a rulebreaker, being a little rebellious himself,

02:26:12.030 --> 02:26:15.420
this uncertainty was putting him on edge.

02:26:15.420 --> 02:26:19.689
His breaths came in shallow gasps,
his chest tight with apprehension.

02:26:19.689 --> 02:26:24.689
As he turned in a circle, looking for the
best way out, he heard a sound behind him,

02:26:24.689 --> 02:26:28.279
the first noise he had heard other than his
own breathing in quite some time.

02:26:28.279 --> 02:26:31.960
It was the soft, plodding sound of uneven
footsteps.

02:26:31.960 --> 02:26:35.010
Like someone small, walking with a limp.

02:26:35.010 --> 02:26:39.630
He whirled around to face the noise and saw
what he might have mistaken for a child, until

02:26:39.630 --> 02:26:41.359
he got a proper look at it.

02:26:41.359 --> 02:26:45.029
The thing that was steadily approaching him
was small and humanoid in shape,

02:26:45.029 --> 02:26:48.851
but that was where the similarities to a human
being ended.

02:26:48.851 --> 02:26:54.120
Its skin was a pale, mottled gray, the shade
of a body fished out of a river after weeks

02:26:54.120 --> 02:26:55.810
of bloating and decay.

02:26:55.810 --> 02:26:59.899
Its mouth was a twisted, gaping hole, more
like a wound than a mouth,

02:26:59.899 --> 02:27:04.710
and in place of its eyes were a pair of slits
in the pale flesh.

02:27:04.710 --> 02:27:07.790
It stumbled as it walked, like it was still
learning how,

02:27:07.790 --> 02:27:11.870
but this childish ineptitude did nothing to
distract from the creature's menace.

02:27:11.870 --> 02:27:15.070
As it advanced toward Dr. Bright, he saw the
glint of something metal.

02:27:15.070 --> 02:27:18.819
In the creature's gnarled fist, it held a
rusty knife.

02:27:18.819 --> 02:27:23.020
As it got closer, it slashed haphazardly in
his direction, and its mouth opened wider

02:27:23.020 --> 02:27:25.680
to let out a chilling high-pitched giggle.

02:27:25.680 --> 02:27:30.250
This thing was prepared to carve him up and
enjoy every second of the job.

02:27:30.250 --> 02:27:34.109
As he took in the sight of the horrible grey
child in front of him, Dr. Bright had been

02:27:34.109 --> 02:27:37.990
frozen to the spot,
unable to force his legs to carry him away.

02:27:37.990 --> 02:27:42.680
But the sight of the knife, the grim realization
of just exactly what this thing had in store

02:27:42.680 --> 02:27:45.140
for him, shocked him back to life.

02:27:45.140 --> 02:27:49.590
He grabbed a wooden beam leaning against the
nearest wall and threw it at the little monster,

02:27:49.590 --> 02:27:51.100
knocking it onto its back.

02:27:51.100 --> 02:27:55.920
It was immobilized just long enough for Dr.
Bright to get his bearings, turn, and run

02:27:55.920 --> 02:27:57.720
back toward the wider street.

02:27:57.720 --> 02:28:02.060
As he did, he could hear that gleeful laughter
as the creature climbed back to its feet,

02:28:02.060 --> 02:28:05.090
and it only pushed him to run faster.

02:28:05.090 --> 02:28:10.109
When he could no longer hear the little footsteps
or the warped giggles of the grey child, Dr.

02:28:10.109 --> 02:28:11.620
Bright stopped to catch his breath.

02:28:11.620 --> 02:28:15.320
So, it seemed like there was life in Silent
Hill after all.

02:28:15.320 --> 02:28:17.590
There just wasn't human life.

02:28:17.590 --> 02:28:22.090
There was silence for a moment, nothing but
Dr. Bright's heart pounding in his head as

02:28:22.090 --> 02:28:26.250
he bent over his knees and silently cursed
himself for not exercising this body more

02:28:26.250 --> 02:28:27.250
often.

02:28:27.250 --> 02:28:31.370
Then, the shrill cry of an air raid siren
ripped through the foggy air,

02:28:31.370 --> 02:28:33.720
and for a moment, his heart stopped.

02:28:33.720 --> 02:28:36.330
What the hell did that sound mean?

02:28:36.330 --> 02:28:39.760
He stood up, inspecting his surroundings for
signs of danger, for...what?

02:28:39.760 --> 02:28:40.820
A tornado?

02:28:40.820 --> 02:28:42.440
A bomb?

02:28:42.440 --> 02:28:47.689
He wasn't sure, but nothing could have prepared
him for what he saw next.

02:28:47.689 --> 02:28:50.819
The entire landscape had shifted around him
in an instant.

02:28:50.819 --> 02:28:56.920
He was unsure of how exactly to describe it,
except to say that the world had gotten darker,

02:28:56.920 --> 02:28:57.920
more dangerous.

02:28:57.920 --> 02:29:02.340
Where the streets had been lit by dim, grey
sunlight filtered through the haze,

02:29:02.340 --> 02:29:07.760
there was now darkness, save for an occasional
street lamp with a flickering red bulb.

02:29:07.760 --> 02:29:11.609
He couldn't be sure if it was a trick of the
light, but he could swear there was blood

02:29:11.609 --> 02:29:15.630
staining the street,
smeared across the brick walls of the buildings

02:29:15.630 --> 02:29:17.040
on either side.

02:29:17.040 --> 02:29:19.240
The town had been ominous before, but now?

02:29:19.240 --> 02:29:21.779
It was like something out of a nightmare.

02:29:21.779 --> 02:29:27.810
And all the while, the siren continued to
blare, warning of a danger he could not see.

02:29:27.810 --> 02:29:29.340
He needed to find shelter.

02:29:29.340 --> 02:29:30.710
That was it.

02:29:30.710 --> 02:29:35.190
From what, he didn't know,
but whatever was coming couldn't be good.

02:29:35.190 --> 02:29:40.340
Dr. Bright ran to the nearest door, twisting
the knob, trying to yank it open, but it wouldn't

02:29:40.340 --> 02:29:41.340
budge.

02:29:41.340 --> 02:29:45.180
The hinges were rusted shut, and there was
no time to try and force them open.

02:29:45.180 --> 02:29:47.710
He tried another door.

02:29:47.710 --> 02:29:48.710
And another.

02:29:48.710 --> 02:29:49.710
And another.

02:29:49.710 --> 02:29:53.850
It was as if the houses knew to try and keep
him out, to keep him exposed and vulnerable

02:29:53.850 --> 02:29:55.380
on the outside.

02:29:55.380 --> 02:30:00.700
Finally, he reached a two-story house with
peeling white paint and a mahogany door, one

02:30:00.700 --> 02:30:03.910
that looked so familiar but that he couldn't
quite place.

02:30:03.910 --> 02:30:08.850
When he turned the knob, the door creaked
open with ease, and he quickly raced inside,

02:30:08.850 --> 02:30:11.899
slammed the door shut behind him, and locked
it.

02:30:11.899 --> 02:30:16.560
As Dr. Bright slumped against the door, overtaken
with exhaustion, an intrusive thought made

02:30:16.560 --> 02:30:18.189
his stomach drop.

02:30:18.189 --> 02:30:19.630
Was he alone in this house?

02:30:19.630 --> 02:30:24.689
Had he really found shelter,
or had he just shut himself inside with something

02:30:24.689 --> 02:30:27.460
even worse than what waited out there?

02:30:27.460 --> 02:30:31.590
In the silence, he could swear he heard the
quiet thumping of a pulse, but he couldn't

02:30:31.590 --> 02:30:35.290
tell if it was his own or the house's beating
heart.

02:30:35.290 --> 02:30:39.270
He couldn't just stay standing here, dreaming
up all the horrors that might be waiting.

02:30:39.270 --> 02:30:44.830
He needed to gather data, investigate his
surroundings, and come up with potential solutions.

02:30:44.830 --> 02:30:46.930
He needed to be a scientist.

02:30:46.930 --> 02:30:51.810
On unsteady legs, Dr. Bright began to walk
the halls of the house, taking in his surroundings

02:30:51.810 --> 02:30:53.870
properly for the first time.

02:30:53.870 --> 02:30:59.090
It was dusty and dark, a place left vacant
for quite some time, but there were signs

02:30:59.090 --> 02:31:01.910
of a life once lived within the house's walls.

02:31:01.910 --> 02:31:06.130
A rocking horse in the corner, a shelf full
of well-worn books.

02:31:06.130 --> 02:31:11.760
The titles scratched an itch in his brain,
memories he hadn't touched in decades.

02:31:11.760 --> 02:31:16.350
Strange, that so many of the books in this
house would remind him of the pages he once

02:31:16.350 --> 02:31:21.109
flipped through, staying inside to read while
his brother played in the yard outside.

02:31:21.109 --> 02:31:22.149
His brother…

02:31:22.149 --> 02:31:25.569
Dr. Bright swallowed the lump in his throat
at the thought of TJ.

02:31:25.569 --> 02:31:28.810
No use going down that path, not right now.

02:31:28.810 --> 02:31:34.090
He continued his walk through the house, taking
in the cobwebs, the couch covered with plastic,

02:31:34.090 --> 02:31:36.630
the coffee table, and the tv set.

02:31:36.630 --> 02:31:41.300
Along the hall leading to the bathroom, there
were rows of family pictures hanging in neat

02:31:41.300 --> 02:31:42.540
little frames.

02:31:42.540 --> 02:31:47.230
He stopped to take a look, wondering what
sort of family had once lived here.

02:31:47.230 --> 02:31:51.750
As his eyes fell on the first photograph,
his blood turned to ice in his veins.

02:31:51.750 --> 02:31:58.360
A boy looked out from within the frame, a
familiar boy with a mop of red hair, a freckled

02:31:58.360 --> 02:32:00.940
nose, and kind, smiling eyes.

02:32:00.940 --> 02:32:01.940
"No..."

02:32:01.940 --> 02:32:04.150
Dr. Bright whispered to himself.

02:32:04.150 --> 02:32:05.550
But there was no denying it.

02:32:05.550 --> 02:32:06.860
It was TJ.

02:32:06.860 --> 02:32:07.990
His brother,

02:32:07.990 --> 02:32:13.720
somehow, looking at him from inside a photograph
in an abandoned house in Silent Hill.

02:32:13.720 --> 02:32:17.109
Next, there was a photo of TJ and an older
man.

02:32:17.109 --> 02:32:19.061
Dr. Bright's stomach turned.

02:32:19.061 --> 02:32:20.790
Their father.

02:32:20.790 --> 02:32:25.940
Every photograph was of his family, the family
he would never know again, not the way they

02:32:25.940 --> 02:32:26.940
were.

02:32:26.940 --> 02:32:31.360
And there, in one photo in the middle, TJ
and his father were joined by another figure,

02:32:31.360 --> 02:32:33.680
one with the face scratched out of the picture.

02:32:33.680 --> 02:32:38.750
He knew, though he couldn't see for certain,
that it was him, removed from the family photo

02:32:38.750 --> 02:32:40.170
by force.

02:32:40.170 --> 02:32:42.340
The bond between them all forever broken.

02:32:42.340 --> 02:32:47.090
TJ's eyes in the picture seemed filled with
tears, heartbroken.

02:32:47.090 --> 02:32:51.420
His father glowered out of the frame at him,
pure hatred in his gaze.

02:32:51.420 --> 02:32:53.920
He couldn't look anymore and turned away.

02:32:53.920 --> 02:32:57.200
On the opposite wall, another photograph of
TJ was waiting.

02:32:57.200 --> 02:33:00.080
Dr. Bright felt bile rising in his throat.

02:33:00.080 --> 02:33:03.899
He recognized this photo, he saw it sometimes
when he closed his eyes at night.

02:33:03.899 --> 02:33:08.410
It was a picture taken with SCP-978, the Desire
Camera,

02:33:08.410 --> 02:33:12.189
the anomalous camera that reveals its subject's
innermost desires.

02:33:12.189 --> 02:33:16.430
In the photo, TJ's hands were pressed to the
lens, like an animal clawing at the boundaries

02:33:16.430 --> 02:33:17.710
of its cage.

02:33:17.710 --> 02:33:22.000
His eyes were filled with tears, spilling
over and streaming down his cheeks.

02:33:22.000 --> 02:33:26.740
And his mouth was wide open in an endless
scream of fear and agony.

02:33:26.740 --> 02:33:33.340
This, Dr. Bright knew, was the wordless scream
inside his brother's mind at all times, after

02:33:33.340 --> 02:33:36.740
so much testing with the Foundation had destroyed
him.

02:33:36.740 --> 02:33:40.980
"I tried to help you..." he whispered to the
picture.

02:33:40.980 --> 02:33:44.101
"I did what I could to, take the pain away..."

02:33:44.101 --> 02:33:49.430
He trailed off, the words dying in his throat,
as the sound of a heavy, rattling breath came

02:33:49.430 --> 02:33:51.700
from the room at the end of the hall.

02:33:51.700 --> 02:33:57.000
It was a thick, wheezing sound, like a death
rattle a dozen times over.

02:33:57.000 --> 02:34:01.479
He didn't want to see what was making the
noise, but, as if in a trance, he found his

02:34:01.479 --> 02:34:03.380
feet carrying him toward it.

02:34:03.380 --> 02:34:07.609
He crossed the threshold of what should have
been the kitchen, but instead found himself

02:34:07.609 --> 02:34:09.760
in a dimly lit hospital.

02:34:09.760 --> 02:34:14.460
The air smelled of formaldehyde and dried
vomit, the steady beep of a heart monitor

02:34:14.460 --> 02:34:17.460
joining the chorus of heavy, agonizing breathing.

02:34:17.460 --> 02:34:22.630
At the end of the long hall there was one
single room, the door standing slightly ajar.

02:34:22.630 --> 02:34:27.600
With slow, agonizing steps, Dr. Bright approached
the room.

02:34:27.600 --> 02:34:31.910
The breathing grew louder and louder, the
beep of the heart monitor almost deafening

02:34:31.910 --> 02:34:33.160
in his ears.

02:34:33.160 --> 02:34:37.439
He could barely separate the strange breathing
from his own, finding his breaths coming in

02:34:37.439 --> 02:34:40.290
time with the painful, rattling sound.

02:34:40.290 --> 02:34:43.820
He pushed the door open and saw a figure lying
in a hospital bed.

02:34:43.820 --> 02:34:49.710
It was once human, but it could only be described
now as a conglomerate of disease and pain.

02:34:49.710 --> 02:34:56.470
On top of the figure's head, thinning in patches
but still recognizable, was a shock of red

02:34:56.470 --> 02:34:57.470
hair.

02:34:57.470 --> 02:35:02.010
"No...TJ...no-" Dr. Bright's voice broke off
in a sob at the sight.

02:35:02.010 --> 02:35:06.790
The thing that was once his brother turned,
seeking the sound of his voice though he could

02:35:06.790 --> 02:35:07.790
not see.

02:35:07.790 --> 02:35:09.920
"Isn't this what you wanted?"

02:35:09.920 --> 02:35:12.330
A low voice rumbled behind him.

02:35:12.330 --> 02:35:17.109
Dr. Bright whirled around to see a dark shadow,
red eyes glowing in the low light.

02:35:17.109 --> 02:35:23.370
It moved like a wisp of black smoke, but he
knew that voice well enough to tell just who

02:35:23.370 --> 02:35:24.370
it was.

02:35:24.370 --> 02:35:25.370
His father.

02:35:25.370 --> 02:35:26.740
"You let them do this to him."

02:35:26.740 --> 02:35:30.760
"I didn't...I didn't mean for this to happen..."

02:35:30.760 --> 02:35:32.410
Dr. Bright stammered.

02:35:32.410 --> 02:35:34.069
"You didn't care!"

02:35:34.069 --> 02:35:38.700
the demon father roared, and the lightbulb
above their heads flared and burst, sending

02:35:38.700 --> 02:35:40.850
shards of glass scattering everywhere.

02:35:40.850 --> 02:35:44.920
"All he ever did was help people, to heal
pain.

02:35:44.920 --> 02:35:47.130
And all you ever did was cause it."

02:35:47.130 --> 02:35:51.170
Dr. Bright found the strength to move, running
through the doorway, sending the shape of

02:35:51.170 --> 02:35:55.930
his father in every direction as the smell
of sulfur and blood filled his nose.

02:35:55.930 --> 02:35:58.649
"I'm sorry, I'm sorry, I'm sorry."

02:35:58.649 --> 02:36:02.470
He wept as he ran, hearing his father's agonized
cries,

02:36:02.470 --> 02:36:06.330
his brother's labored breathing, as he tore
back down the hospital hall.

02:36:06.330 --> 02:36:10.300
As he did, hands began to reach through the
walls, the floor, the ceiling.

02:36:10.300 --> 02:36:14.060
They were rotting, flesh gone green and stiff.

02:36:14.060 --> 02:36:17.210
Arms followed the hands, then shoulders, then
faces.

02:36:17.210 --> 02:36:22.240
He recognized D-Class uniforms, lab coats,
face after familiar face.

02:36:22.240 --> 02:36:27.479
Everyone who had ever worn the amulet, he
realized with a sudden wave of nausea.

02:36:27.479 --> 02:36:33.250
Everyone whose body he had taken, who he had
killed so that he could live.

02:36:33.250 --> 02:36:34.900
"This is what you do."

02:36:34.900 --> 02:36:40.200
He could hear his father's voice just behind
him, the curls of black smoke nipping at his

02:36:40.200 --> 02:36:41.200
heels.

02:36:41.200 --> 02:36:43.090
"What kind of creature kills to live?

02:36:43.090 --> 02:36:44.479
Something that deserves to die.

02:36:44.479 --> 02:36:46.830
But you can't, can you?

02:36:46.830 --> 02:36:51.250
You're a parasite, sucking all of the good
and the life from this world until it's an

02:36:51.250 --> 02:36:54.160
empty husk, just like you."

02:36:54.160 --> 02:36:59.170
The living graveyard of victims reached for
Dr. Bright, tearing at his clothes as he passed

02:36:59.170 --> 02:37:00.170
them.

02:37:00.170 --> 02:37:04.830
There were so many...he'd never realized how
many until he saw them all lined up like this.

02:37:04.830 --> 02:37:05.830
"I'm sorry.

02:37:05.830 --> 02:37:06.830
I'm sorry.

02:37:06.830 --> 02:37:12.560
I'm so sorry," he said to every face he passed,
but there was no forgiveness in their dead

02:37:12.560 --> 02:37:13.560
eyes.

02:37:13.560 --> 02:37:16.380
His father's voice fell silent, but there
was no reprieve.

02:37:16.380 --> 02:37:22.340
Instead, there was the sound of heavy footsteps
pursuing him, the grating shriek of a large

02:37:22.340 --> 02:37:24.689
bladed weapon dragging along the ground.

02:37:24.689 --> 02:37:30.410
Dr. Bright glanced over his shoulder and saw
a massive, hulking figure, like a man but

02:37:30.410 --> 02:37:33.590
with a jagged metal pyramid covering his head.

02:37:33.590 --> 02:37:37.890
He was clad in blood-stained clothing, and
approaching Dr. Bright with the casual menace

02:37:37.890 --> 02:37:43.450
of something that had killed endless times
before, and was ready to do it again.

02:37:43.450 --> 02:37:48.040
It didn't care to chase him, it knew it would
eventually catch up.

02:37:48.040 --> 02:37:51.380
As he looked at the figure, it began to warp,
to change.

02:37:51.380 --> 02:37:54.510
It morphed into something else...something
he recognized.

02:37:54.510 --> 02:38:00.880
A man, with black hair and grey eyes that
revealed nothing but cold malice.

02:38:00.880 --> 02:38:06.479
A body, covered in tattoos of the arcane and
the occult, of merciless grinning faces belonging

02:38:06.479 --> 02:38:08.399
to ancient monsters.

02:38:08.399 --> 02:38:14.670
And in his hand, a long bladed weapon, a curved
sword perfectly engineered for battle.

02:38:14.670 --> 02:38:21.899
It was impossible, he should be contained,
but there he was.

02:38:21.899 --> 02:38:27.390
SCP-076-2, looking at Dr. Bright the same
way he had on the day he had first worn the

02:38:27.390 --> 02:38:28.390
amulet.

02:38:28.390 --> 02:38:30.330
The day he became an abomination.

02:38:30.330 --> 02:38:34.310
The sound of his brother's breathing grew
louder, his fathers voice bellowing in his

02:38:34.310 --> 02:38:37.180
mind,
and he felt the grasp of the fallen Foundation

02:38:37.180 --> 02:38:38.550
employees at his back.

02:38:38.550 --> 02:38:43.380
It was all too much, too many reminders of
every failure, every hurt.

02:38:43.380 --> 02:38:48.250
Dr. Bright collapsed to his knees, and bared
his neck to Able in submission.

02:38:48.250 --> 02:38:49.250
"You're right.

02:38:49.250 --> 02:38:50.250
I should be punished.

02:38:50.250 --> 02:38:54.080
I've done nothing but hurt, and kill, and
destroy everything I touch.

02:38:54.080 --> 02:38:55.460
Just punish me.

02:38:55.460 --> 02:38:56.830
Please...I'm begging you."

02:38:56.830 --> 02:38:59.590
Then, something Dr. Bright never could have
expected.

02:38:59.590 --> 02:39:07.939
The figure that looked like Able but couldn't
be began to laugh, a cruel, hollow sound.

02:39:07.939 --> 02:39:08.960
"No."

02:39:08.960 --> 02:39:14.109
It said, in a voice that sounded like a chorus
of hundreds.

02:39:14.109 --> 02:39:15.109
"We will release you.

02:39:15.109 --> 02:39:27.522
Nothing we can do here will ever be worse
than the eternity of torment that stretches

02:39:27.522 --> 02:39:28.522
before you.

02:39:28.522 --> 02:39:29.522
Good luck to you...Dr. Bright."

02:39:29.522 --> 02:39:31.550
Jack Bright let out a wail of agony, reaching
out to Able with pleading hands as the entity

02:39:31.550 --> 02:39:32.550
turned

02:39:32.550 --> 02:39:38.479
and slowly walked away, leaving him there
alone with the hell that was his thoughts.

02:39:38.479 --> 02:39:41.040
Then, everything faded to black.

02:39:41.040 --> 02:39:42.040
"Dr. Bright?

02:39:42.040 --> 02:39:43.040
Dr. Bright?!"

02:39:43.040 --> 02:39:45.700
The sound of one of the team members brought
Dr. Bright back to consciousness, and he opened

02:39:45.700 --> 02:39:46.700
his eyes.

02:39:46.700 --> 02:39:50.460
He was lying on his side on the same road
he had walked along before, right next to

02:39:50.460 --> 02:39:52.720
the sign welcoming the group to Silent Hill.

02:39:52.720 --> 02:39:55.479
"You're all...still here?" he asked, weakly.

02:39:55.479 --> 02:39:56.850
"What do you mean?"

02:39:56.850 --> 02:39:58.170
The man looked puzzled.

02:39:58.170 --> 02:40:01.970
"We parked the van, you got out, you fell
down, and we've been trying to wake you up."

02:40:01.970 --> 02:40:03.900
"How long was I out...?"

02:40:03.900 --> 02:40:08.780
Dr. Bright's vision swam as he tried to wrap
his mind around the information.

02:40:08.780 --> 02:40:11.160
"I dunno, about ten minutes?"

02:40:11.160 --> 02:40:16.820
SCP-096, the Shy Guy, woke up in an unfamiliar
room without a thought in its head.

02:40:16.820 --> 02:40:22.540
However, one thing quickly occurred to it:
This was not its containment chamber.

02:40:22.540 --> 02:40:27.920
No, the solid steel cube that had kept it
locked up for decades now didn’t have a

02:40:27.920 --> 02:40:34.160
front desk, or walls covered in strange, childlike
murals, or tinny party music playing over

02:40:34.160 --> 02:40:35.859
old speakers.

02:40:35.859 --> 02:40:42.790
Wherever SCP-096 had found itself, it certainly
wasn’t the place it’d come to call home.

02:40:42.790 --> 02:40:47.170
If it could read, it would have registered
the word’s behind the front desk, painted

02:40:47.170 --> 02:40:52.609
onto the wall: Banban’s Kindergarten - Where
joy comes alive!

02:40:52.609 --> 02:40:58.310
Little did 096 know, something really did
come to life here at Banban’s kindergarten,

02:40:58.310 --> 02:41:01.040
but it wasn’t definitely joy.

02:41:01.040 --> 02:41:07.069
SCP-096 was typically used to a much more
confined space, so presented with so much

02:41:07.069 --> 02:41:13.530
strange freedom, its natural instinct was
to wander around, whimpering quietly to itself.

02:41:13.530 --> 02:41:19.250
This place was certainly peculiar, with multiple
large, reinforced doors, each one having a

02:41:19.250 --> 02:41:21.880
large, red button fixed to the wall above
them.

02:41:21.880 --> 02:41:26.760
A strange green creature was painted on the
wall, with an affixed speech bubble that read,

02:41:26.760 --> 02:41:32.149
“Jumbo Josh says, ‘Eat fruits and vegetables
if you want to be strong like me!’”

02:41:32.149 --> 02:41:35.990
Though 096 paid no mind to such things.

02:41:35.990 --> 02:41:42.080
This place looked like an SCP Foundation containment
facility mixed with an off-brand Chuck E Cheese,

02:41:42.080 --> 02:41:47.000
and some tiny part of the Shy Guy’s dim
and dismal brain wanted to see more of it.

02:41:47.000 --> 02:41:49.180
What other secrets could it hold?

02:41:49.180 --> 02:41:53.720
The Shy Guy pressed one of the big red buttons,
causing the doors to open underneath it.

02:41:53.720 --> 02:41:57.910
There was a faint sound of childish laughter
somewhere down the distant hall.

02:41:57.910 --> 02:42:02.290
How had it ever found its way to such a strange
place?

02:42:02.290 --> 02:42:06.820
The deeper the Shy Guy wandered, the more
strange murals it saw on the wall.

02:42:06.820 --> 02:42:11.590
There was one for a large, orange cartoon
jellyfish with one staring eye, where the

02:42:11.590 --> 02:42:16.880
speech bubble read, “Stinger Flynn says,
‘Having many arms allows me to help a lot

02:42:16.880 --> 02:42:17.880
of people.’”

02:42:17.880 --> 02:42:22.350
Then, a squat, blue gorilla-like creature
with sharp lower teeth, the speech bubble

02:42:22.350 --> 02:42:25.920
reading, “Captain Fiddles says, ’Ooga
Booga!

02:42:25.920 --> 02:42:26.920
Booga ooga!’”

02:42:26.920 --> 02:42:32.870
A White, feminine, cat-like creature had the
speech bubble, “Banbaleena says, ‘Kindness

02:42:32.870 --> 02:42:35.220
is free, so sprinkle it everywhere.’”

02:42:35.220 --> 02:42:41.160
A misshapen, pink bird creature had the speech
bubble, “Opila Bird says, ‘Laughter is

02:42:41.160 --> 02:42:44.620
the best medicine, so make sure to smile.’”

02:42:44.620 --> 02:42:49.470
And of course, most importantly of all, presiding
over the “creativity room”, was a mural

02:42:49.470 --> 02:42:54.340
of a grinning red creature wearing a pair
of party hats on his head like horns.

02:42:54.340 --> 02:42:59.380
His speech bubble read, “Banban says, ‘Sharing
is caring.

02:42:59.380 --> 02:43:01.630
Your pancreas is mine!’”

02:43:01.630 --> 02:43:05.880
A whiteboard not far from this mural had the
words, “RUN FOR YOUR LIVES!”

02:43:05.880 --> 02:43:07.670
hastily scrawled onto it.

02:43:07.670 --> 02:43:12.960
To anyone but the Shy Guy, who had no point
of reference, this was clearly not a normal

02:43:12.960 --> 02:43:14.550
Kindergarten.

02:43:14.550 --> 02:43:19.830
As the Shy Guy passed into the hallway, slowly
lumbering along, it suddenly felt an intense

02:43:19.830 --> 02:43:23.050
spike of burning pain right through its forehead.

02:43:23.050 --> 02:43:27.840
Rising like a fever pitch, the Shy Guy’s
subdued whimpers quickly escalated into horrifying

02:43:27.840 --> 02:43:30.390
wails of animal rage.

02:43:30.390 --> 02:43:36.170
This could only mean one thing: Somewhere,
something had made the terrible mistake of

02:43:36.170 --> 02:43:38.320
gazing at the Shy Guy’s face.

02:43:38.320 --> 02:43:44.010
In fact, not ten feet away, the unfortunate
Opila Bird had made that very mistake.

02:43:44.010 --> 02:43:47.460
It typically peeked out from behind one of
the hallway’s pillars to get a good look

02:43:47.460 --> 02:43:52.810
at any potential intruders, or new prey wandering
into its midst.

02:43:52.810 --> 02:43:58.220
But this time, the beastly bird’s curiosity
had taken it from predator to prey itself.

02:43:58.220 --> 02:44:02.620
Though it wouldn’t realize this until it
was already too late.

02:44:02.620 --> 02:44:06.440
As the mutant bird creature skittered back
into the cavernous hall filled with plastic

02:44:06.440 --> 02:44:12.230
trees and an underwhelming play area, the
Shy Guy came galloping after it on all fours,

02:44:12.230 --> 02:44:13.820
roaring with fury.

02:44:13.820 --> 02:44:18.990
The Opila Bird was shocked to see the creature
giving chase - This doesn’t make sense,

02:44:18.990 --> 02:44:22.479
I’m meant to chase you, you’re not meant
to chase me!

02:44:22.479 --> 02:44:27.630
But things are rarely fair when it comes to
the Shy Guy - arguably one of the most dangerous

02:44:27.630 --> 02:44:31.600
Euclid-class creatures ever kept in Foundation
containment.

02:44:31.600 --> 02:44:36.399
The Opila Bird flapped desperately towards
the boarded-up escape hatch, but the Shy Guy

02:44:36.399 --> 02:44:38.250
was so much faster.

02:44:38.250 --> 02:44:42.810
It grabbed hold of the bird, gripping it in
its iron clutches, and began to stretch open

02:44:42.810 --> 02:44:45.630
its bottom jaw with a blood-curdling roar.

02:44:45.630 --> 02:44:50.660
We’ll spare you the details of what happened
next, but suffice to say, little more than

02:44:50.660 --> 02:44:53.270
a handful of feathers were left.

02:44:53.270 --> 02:44:59.080
After finishing off the Opila Bird, the Shy
Guy reverted from rage back to pitiful sadness.

02:44:59.080 --> 02:45:03.800
This place was no better than the SCP Foundation
- There were still creatures here to look

02:45:03.800 --> 02:45:08.670
at its face and drive it into a state of extreme
emotional distress!

02:45:08.670 --> 02:45:10.569
It was horrible.

02:45:10.569 --> 02:45:15.380
The Shy Guy needed to find a way to hide himself
away again, so he decided to tear away the

02:45:15.380 --> 02:45:20.080
planks covering up the escape hatch and lope
down the narrow hall.

02:45:20.080 --> 02:45:25.029
In there, the Shy Guy actually found something
to its liking: Two platforms, one on either

02:45:25.029 --> 02:45:29.771
side of the room, with what seemed to be a
deep, dark chasm between the two.

02:45:29.771 --> 02:45:34.479
For most, there was an electrical lift system
for carrying them between the platforms, but

02:45:34.479 --> 02:45:40.141
the Shy Guy had no interest in the platforms
- He only sought the darkness below, where

02:45:40.141 --> 02:45:42.520
nobody could see his face.

02:45:42.520 --> 02:45:44.540
That would suit him just fine.

02:45:44.540 --> 02:45:48.850
The Shy Guy jumped over the guard rail and
plummeted down below into the dark.

02:45:48.850 --> 02:45:52.860
Feeling the air against its cold, gray skin
as it fell felt wonderful.

02:45:52.860 --> 02:45:58.189
It dared to think that it might even find
solace in this deep, dark place, but as soon

02:45:58.189 --> 02:46:03.899
as it hit something solid - Some kind of metal
lift system - alarms went off, and flashing

02:46:03.899 --> 02:46:08.290
red lights soon made everything down there
in the darkness visible.

02:46:08.290 --> 02:46:12.780
To be exposed like this while it thought it
was hidden was the dreaded Shy Guy’s worst

02:46:12.780 --> 02:46:13.939
nightmare.

02:46:13.939 --> 02:46:19.979
Little did he know, he would soon become the
worst nightmare of a number of other beasts.

02:46:19.979 --> 02:46:24.279
As the Shy Guy crawled off the metal platform,
hoping that perhaps it could find another

02:46:24.279 --> 02:46:29.410
place to hide down here, its hands and feet
splashed into shallow water.

02:46:29.410 --> 02:46:31.279
Had something sprung a leak down here?

02:46:31.279 --> 02:46:35.960
The Shy Guy began wandering the half-dark
as the water of this flooded sub-basement

02:46:35.960 --> 02:46:41.920
area, having no idea that another creature
was already sneaking up behind it, getting

02:46:41.920 --> 02:46:43.790
ready to strike.

02:46:43.790 --> 02:46:49.000
Before 096 could even react, several whip-like
orange tentacles wrapped around its limbs

02:46:49.000 --> 02:46:52.710
and began delivering an agonizing shock through
its body.

02:46:52.710 --> 02:46:58.000
More tentacles came in soon after, wrapping
around the Shy Guy’s body and face, delivering

02:46:58.000 --> 02:47:03.680
shock after shock after shock with its millions
of tiny, venomous stinger barbs.

02:47:03.680 --> 02:47:08.080
For a second, it seemed like the surprise
attack might actually be able to subdue the

02:47:08.080 --> 02:47:13.340
monster, but little did the Shy Guy’s attacker
- the monstrous real Stinger Flynn - know,

02:47:13.340 --> 02:47:17.540
that attacking it was just another way to
activate its infamous rage state.

02:47:17.540 --> 02:47:22.561
The second that Stinger Flynn relented, even
for just a moment, the Shy Guy grabbed him

02:47:22.561 --> 02:47:29.060
by the tentacles and slammed him into the
watery floor, again and again and again, until

02:47:29.060 --> 02:47:34.170
the grip of his tentacles loosened around
the Shy Guy’s body, and he was defenseless.

02:47:34.170 --> 02:47:39.210
With Stinger panicked and immobilized, the
Shy Guy released the full force of his supernatural

02:47:39.210 --> 02:47:44.210
fury, striking the demonic jellyfish with
his fists until what was left couldn’t be

02:47:44.210 --> 02:47:47.080
distinguished from the water it used to be
floating in.

02:47:47.080 --> 02:47:50.920
Stinger Flynn had been completely annihilated.

02:47:50.920 --> 02:47:56.029
The Shy Guy, once again temporarily returned
to a state of calm, wandered out of this watery

02:47:56.029 --> 02:48:01.210
basement into a nearby hallway, where everything
was bright and dry.

02:48:01.210 --> 02:48:06.490
The walls were painted in garish, childlike
drawings of a jungle with janky-looking animals.

02:48:06.490 --> 02:48:11.211
The Shy Guy paid no mind to any of it - All
he wanted was to find somewhere that he could

02:48:11.211 --> 02:48:15.640
actually be alone, and away from the prying
eyes of all these freaks.

02:48:15.640 --> 02:48:21.350
This dream was short-lived, because as soon
as the Shy Guy found a new room - A wide-open

02:48:21.350 --> 02:48:27.460
auditorium filled with inflatable jungle trees,
like a crude play area - he was being stalked

02:48:27.460 --> 02:48:28.460
yet again.

02:48:28.460 --> 02:48:33.410
A powerful, muscular predator, with veins
stretching over its bulging muscles and blue

02:48:33.410 --> 02:48:34.410
skin.

02:48:34.410 --> 02:48:39.290
It knew that this intruder had already killed
two of its fellow Garten dwellers, but this

02:48:39.290 --> 02:48:43.580
time, it would put this spindly, pale stranger
in its place.

02:48:43.580 --> 02:48:47.950
The Shy Guy heard something rumbling towards
it from behind, and turned upon hearing the

02:48:47.950 --> 02:48:51.590
bloodcurdling war cry of “Ooga booga, booga
ooga!”

02:48:51.590 --> 02:48:56.940
It was the gorilla-like Captain Fiddles, with
strands of saliva hanging from his tusks.

02:48:56.940 --> 02:49:02.380
He leaped onto the Shy Guy and began pounding
into its hideous face with his huge, blue

02:49:02.380 --> 02:49:03.380
fists.

02:49:03.380 --> 02:49:07.620
Captain Fiddles would destroy the Shy Guy,
pound him into the concrete until he stopped

02:49:07.620 --> 02:49:08.620
moving.

02:49:08.620 --> 02:49:12.620
The one thing that Captain Fiddles didn’t
expect was for the Shy Guy to start fighting

02:49:12.620 --> 02:49:17.830
back - And he was about to learn the hard
truth that the Shy Guy could fight a lot harder

02:49:17.830 --> 02:49:19.100
than him.

02:49:19.100 --> 02:49:24.000
The SCP’s spindly but immensely powerful
arms grabbed Captain Fiddles by the shoulders

02:49:24.000 --> 02:49:29.080
and threw him into the wall, causing huge
cracks to crawl up toward the ceiling.

02:49:29.080 --> 02:49:33.010
Before Captain Fiddles could even think about
wrenching himself free, the Shy Guy leaped

02:49:33.010 --> 02:49:35.330
to his feet and bounded over to him.

02:49:35.330 --> 02:49:39.460
The Shy Guy grabbed Captain Fiddles by the
ankles and ripped him out of the wall, letting

02:49:39.460 --> 02:49:43.279
his body drop to the floor and then unleashing
hell upon him.

02:49:43.279 --> 02:49:48.300
Captain Fiddles would have begged for mercy,
if he wasn’t already dead.

02:49:48.300 --> 02:49:52.720
Before the Shy Guy could even begin to calm
down, a trap door opened in the floor beneath

02:49:52.720 --> 02:49:59.109
him, dropping him into a new area: A multi-colored
maze, where 096 was trapped like a helpless

02:49:59.109 --> 02:50:00.109
rat…

02:50:00.109 --> 02:50:02.380
With the body count of an atom bomb.

02:50:02.380 --> 02:50:07.340
Once again, the Shy Guy didn’t need to go
looking for trouble, because trouble was already

02:50:07.340 --> 02:50:14.130
on its way: The pure white, catlike Banbaleena
pounced from a sharp corner in the maze, claws

02:50:14.130 --> 02:50:15.130
bared.

02:50:15.130 --> 02:50:18.700
She jumped onto the Shy Guy, latching onto
him and clawing at him, trying to scratch

02:50:18.700 --> 02:50:20.870
his nightmare of a face.

02:50:20.870 --> 02:50:25.090
If the Shy Guy had ever possessed anything
resembling patience, it would have run out

02:50:25.090 --> 02:50:26.450
long before now.

02:50:26.450 --> 02:50:31.300
It grabbed Banbaleena, shrugging off the pain
of her claws, and tossed her into the darkness

02:50:31.300 --> 02:50:32.530
high above.

02:50:32.530 --> 02:50:37.290
She flew off with a cat-like yowl and hit
some distant ceiling with a crunch.

02:50:37.290 --> 02:50:40.729
Suffice to say, she wouldn’t be back to
bother the Shy Guy again.

02:50:40.729 --> 02:50:45.790
Tired and angry, the pale abomination didn’t
feel like wandering through a maze.

02:50:45.790 --> 02:50:50.590
Instead, it just charged straight through,
destroying colorful wall after colorful wall.

02:50:50.590 --> 02:50:55.670
When it encountered whatever was behind this
extremely frustrating afternoon, it would

02:50:55.670 --> 02:50:57.960
destroy it with extreme prejudice.

02:50:57.960 --> 02:50:59.960
That was almost a certainty.

02:50:59.960 --> 02:51:05.250
After breaking through one more wall, the
Shy Guy tumbled out into a storage room, where

02:51:05.250 --> 02:51:11.000
a grinning red creature was waiting for him
- Wearing two party hats on top of his head.

02:51:11.000 --> 02:51:17.550
This was no mere lackey, it was Banban himself,
the one behind this nightmarish kindergarten.

02:51:17.550 --> 02:51:22.590
He stared right into the Shy Guy’s face
with no fear at all, almost like he knew something

02:51:22.590 --> 02:51:23.790
the Shy Guy didn’t.

02:51:23.790 --> 02:51:27.570
And it was true - He had no plan to fight
the Shy Guy himself.

02:51:27.570 --> 02:51:30.510
He’d brought a champion with him.

02:51:30.510 --> 02:51:34.970
As the Shy Guy started to scream in rage,
all the boxes behind Banban tumbled out of

02:51:34.970 --> 02:51:37.780
the way as a huge figure rose.

02:51:37.780 --> 02:51:45.250
A behemoth, at least fifty feet tall, with
green skin, giant teeth, and huge, staring

02:51:45.250 --> 02:51:46.250
eyes.

02:51:46.250 --> 02:51:51.130
It was Jumbo Josh, and if the Shy Guy wanted
to graduate from this nightmare kindergarten,

02:51:51.130 --> 02:51:55.120
it would need to defeat him and Banban.

02:51:55.120 --> 02:51:59.560
Shy Guy growled, digging its claws into the
ground and preparing to charge forward.

02:51:59.560 --> 02:52:01.000
Challenge accepted.

02:52:01.000 --> 02:52:07.670
Did you hear that not so long ago, a five-year-old
boy went up against twelve of Russia’s greatest

02:52:07.670 --> 02:52:10.960
chess grandmasters - And do you know what
happened?

02:52:10.960 --> 02:52:12.649
He lost every single match.

02:52:12.649 --> 02:52:16.350
That’s because winning at chess, dear viewers,
isn’t easy.

02:52:16.350 --> 02:52:20.010
Chess is an ancient game of strategy, cunning,
and skill.

02:52:20.010 --> 02:52:24.590
It’s not just about thinking one step ahead
of your opponent, that’s not going to be

02:52:24.590 --> 02:52:25.590
enough.

02:52:25.590 --> 02:52:30.040
Make a wrong prediction and you could end
up sacrificing one of your pieces, as well

02:52:30.040 --> 02:52:32.020
as vital space on the board.

02:52:32.020 --> 02:52:36.710
But thinking two to three steps ahead, beating
your adversary’s moves before they’ve

02:52:36.710 --> 02:52:37.710
even been made.

02:52:37.710 --> 02:52:40.240
Now there’s a viable strategy.

02:52:40.240 --> 02:52:45.740
Tricky, but viable; after all, if it was easy,
we’d all be Queen’s Gambit level chess

02:52:45.740 --> 02:52:46.960
prodigies.

02:52:46.960 --> 02:52:51.720
Eric Matthews had never been good at the game,
but he had a pretty substantial reason to

02:52:51.720 --> 02:52:52.729
keep trying.

02:52:52.729 --> 02:52:56.979
And that reason’s name was Brian Matthews,
his father.

02:52:56.979 --> 02:53:01.770
You see, for as long as Eric could remember,
his dad had regarded a high level of skill

02:53:01.770 --> 02:53:06.130
on the chessboard to be a sign of intellectual
superiority.

02:53:06.130 --> 02:53:10.100
Intelligence was something that Brian put
quite a considerable value on, given that

02:53:10.100 --> 02:53:14.520
he was a professor at a university in their
home county in England.

02:53:14.520 --> 02:53:18.950
Some of Eric’s earliest memories were of
playing chess with his dad, usually on a Friday

02:53:18.950 --> 02:53:23.489
night when Brian got home after a week of
giving lectures to the next generation of

02:53:23.489 --> 02:53:24.489
scholars.

02:53:24.489 --> 02:53:29.670
Obviously, with his son at such a young age,
the professor would take it easy on Eric,

02:53:29.670 --> 02:53:35.029
playing in a much laxer fashion, focusing
instead on teaching the boy the basics of

02:53:35.029 --> 02:53:36.029
the game.

02:53:36.029 --> 02:53:38.460
And, for a time at least, it was good.

02:53:38.460 --> 02:53:43.330
It was a rare time that Eric and his academic
father could spend bonding, after all, with

02:53:43.330 --> 02:53:46.040
his mother gone, his dad was all he had.

02:53:46.040 --> 02:53:49.130
But as the years passed, the game changed.

02:53:49.130 --> 02:53:53.910
By the time Eric was a teenager, Brian had
stopped pulling his punches on the chessboard.

02:53:53.910 --> 02:53:58.660
He had hoped his son would build on what he’d
learned when they played in the past, using

02:53:58.660 --> 02:54:00.820
those skills to best his dad on the board.

02:54:00.820 --> 02:54:06.050
But to Eric, playing chess had never been
about a purely educational experience; it

02:54:06.050 --> 02:54:09.340
was more about spending time with his old
man.

02:54:09.340 --> 02:54:14.029
Time after time, the young man’s pawns fell
prey to Brian’s expertly considered and

02:54:14.029 --> 02:54:16.370
far more competitive moves.

02:54:16.370 --> 02:54:19.470
Try as he might, Eric couldn’t best his
dad.

02:54:19.470 --> 02:54:24.351
He tried his best, and never stopped putting
the effort into every game, but thinking too

02:54:24.351 --> 02:54:29.720
hard about one possible plan of attack left
him wide open to a counter-strategy from the

02:54:29.720 --> 02:54:30.720
professor.

02:54:30.720 --> 02:54:36.069
Over and over again he landed himself in checkmate,
or made illegal moves without even realizing

02:54:36.069 --> 02:54:41.440
it, every time earning criticism and chastisement
from his scholarly father.

02:54:41.440 --> 02:54:45.870
Every game it got worse, it was like Eric
could feel his father’s gaze and the weighty

02:54:45.870 --> 02:54:50.080
expectations behind it with each move he made
across the board.

02:54:50.080 --> 02:54:54.790
There were so many nights where he wondered
if it would be better to give up entirely,

02:54:54.790 --> 02:54:57.021
to knock down his own king and concede.

02:54:57.021 --> 02:55:01.710
But how could he ever find any other shared
interests with his dad beyond the two of them

02:55:01.710 --> 02:55:03.010
playing chess?

02:55:03.010 --> 02:55:08.810
It had become a lifeline, tethering father
and son together, and to cut it now left Eric

02:55:08.810 --> 02:55:12.859
uncertain if he’d sink or be able to swim
alongside Brian.

02:55:12.859 --> 02:55:18.600
He had long admired his father, his achievements
in academia were impossible to avoid, with

02:55:18.600 --> 02:55:22.450
more framed certificates hung up on the walls
than there were photos of the pair of them

02:55:22.450 --> 02:55:23.470
together.

02:55:23.470 --> 02:55:28.010
But the shadow it cast over him made Eric
desperate to keep this one shred of common

02:55:28.010 --> 02:55:29.040
ground alive.

02:55:29.040 --> 02:55:33.750
Eric wasn’t the type to give up, despite
how much of an uphill struggle the situation

02:55:33.750 --> 02:55:34.970
felt like.

02:55:34.970 --> 02:55:40.080
Taking a leaf out of his professor father’s
scholarly ways, he decided to learn the game

02:55:40.080 --> 02:55:43.830
inside and out, every known move and strategy.

02:55:43.830 --> 02:55:48.910
He would research the entire history of chess
itself, if that’s what it took to play with

02:55:48.910 --> 02:55:52.080
the same skill as his dear old dad.

02:55:52.080 --> 02:55:56.720
Over the coming weeks, Eric checked out every
book at his local library on the subject;

02:55:56.720 --> 02:56:01.979
Beginner’s Guides, Advanced Rulebooks, and
even a few volumes on notable players throughout

02:56:01.979 --> 02:56:04.930
the extensively long history of the game.

02:56:04.930 --> 02:56:08.710
Along the way, a chapter of a certain book
stood out to Eric.

02:56:08.710 --> 02:56:13.279
It described a chess prodigy from Russia,
who had created an early mechanical chess

02:56:13.279 --> 02:56:15.320
device known as ‘The Samurai’.

02:56:15.320 --> 02:56:20.939
It had been designed to be a traveling curiosity,
and would sit playing chess games against

02:56:20.939 --> 02:56:26.170
volunteers taken from a spectating audience,
each one of them having forked over some of

02:56:26.170 --> 02:56:29.380
their hard-earned money to watch this man-made
wonder.

02:56:29.380 --> 02:56:33.720
The Russian chess prodigy’s young daughters
also had a love for the game.

02:56:33.720 --> 02:56:38.200
Seeing that gave Eric a pang of jealousy,
wondering if those daughters had as much trouble

02:56:38.200 --> 02:56:41.290
playing their own father as he did with Brian.

02:56:41.290 --> 02:56:45.470
But at least there was an underlying shred
of hope there too, if this father and his

02:56:45.470 --> 02:56:50.310
daughters could bond over chess, maybe there
was a chance for Eric and his dad too.

02:56:50.310 --> 02:56:54.550
Sadly, it’s one thing to try and learn all
the facts you can about chess.

02:56:54.550 --> 02:56:59.380
It’s an entirely different beast to put
all that information to use and apply it to

02:56:59.380 --> 02:57:00.960
an actual game.

02:57:00.960 --> 02:57:05.660
Despite having read every book he could get
his hands on, Eric still couldn’t best Brian

02:57:05.660 --> 02:57:06.660
at the board.

02:57:06.660 --> 02:57:11.000
It was like nothing had changed, his father
barely noticed when Eric tried to replicate

02:57:11.000 --> 02:57:16.220
move sets he’d read up on, and still managed
to not only counter those moves but check

02:57:16.220 --> 02:57:18.250
his queen in the process.

02:57:18.250 --> 02:57:20.340
So, practice, Eric thought.

02:57:20.340 --> 02:57:23.370
After all, practice was supposedly meant to
make perfect, right?

02:57:23.370 --> 02:57:28.200
The plan was simple if he practiced his chess
moves enough times, and figured out how he

02:57:28.200 --> 02:57:32.271
could call on what he’d learned, then he
might stand a chance at winning when he and

02:57:32.271 --> 02:57:34.060
his dad played each other.

02:57:34.060 --> 02:57:39.330
There was just one hiccup to the plan: Eric
needed someone to practice against.

02:57:39.330 --> 02:57:44.330
The only other person in the house was Brian,
meaning it was that hiccup that turned into

02:57:44.330 --> 02:57:47.979
a problem almost big enough to stop Eric in
his tracks.

02:57:47.979 --> 02:57:51.560
That is, until he went into his father’s
lab.

02:57:51.560 --> 02:57:57.370
It was under the house itself, a sort of sub-level,
maybe used as a basement or cellar by the

02:57:57.370 --> 02:57:58.819
previous owners.

02:57:58.819 --> 02:58:03.560
But since Professor Matthews and his son had
lived there, the entire room had been remodeled

02:58:03.560 --> 02:58:05.850
into an at-home laboratory.

02:58:05.850 --> 02:58:10.239
Not a terribly advanced one, of course, this
was the early nineties, after all.

02:58:10.239 --> 02:58:15.170
A majority of Brian’s time, even when he
was at home instead of working at the university,

02:58:15.170 --> 02:58:18.660
was spent on his own, downstairs in the lab.

02:58:18.660 --> 02:58:22.550
Eric had gone down there in search of his
dad, to ask him if there was anyone whom he

02:58:22.550 --> 02:58:25.920
knew who he could practice and develop his
chess skills with.

02:58:25.920 --> 02:58:31.060
But, instead, what he found down there was
the last thing he expected to see.

02:58:31.060 --> 02:58:34.920
Not that he had any clue exactly what it was,
at first.

02:58:34.920 --> 02:58:41.080
The… thing was some kind of bizarre contraption,
a collection of components that didn’t seem

02:58:41.080 --> 02:58:43.489
to be in any logical configuration.

02:58:43.489 --> 02:58:49.189
However, it was primarily comprised of something
that Eric recognized all too well: a chess

02:58:49.189 --> 02:58:50.189
table.

02:58:50.189 --> 02:58:55.830
This one was metal, steel to be precise, and
seemed to be hooked up to some sort of computer.

02:58:55.830 --> 02:59:00.500
While, back in the nineties, computers were
hardly as commonplace as they are now, Eric

02:59:00.500 --> 02:59:04.000
had seen a fair few at school and the library.

02:59:04.000 --> 02:59:07.330
Although this one was different, it seemed…

02:59:07.330 --> 02:59:08.330
old.

02:59:08.330 --> 02:59:12.399
Far older than Eric thought computers had
been around for; as far as he knew, they’d

02:59:12.399 --> 02:59:15.710
only really come to prominence in the mid-eighties.

02:59:15.710 --> 02:59:19.330
But this computer looked like it pre-dated
even that period.

02:59:19.330 --> 02:59:23.660
Noticing another part of the contraption – a
large steam engine with the words ‘Manufactured

02:59:23.660 --> 02:59:30.250
by Maudslay, Sons &amp; Field, established 1840’
engraved on one side – made it seem that

02:59:30.250 --> 02:59:34.000
this whole device had been around since the
Victorian era.

02:59:34.000 --> 02:59:38.521
The next part that caught his eye was the
chess pieces themselves, each one standing

02:59:38.521 --> 02:59:41.010
neatly in its place on the board.

02:59:41.010 --> 02:59:45.360
They looked delicate, intricately carved from
some smooth substance.

02:59:45.360 --> 02:59:49.500
For a moment, Eric toyed with the thought
of how they could even be made from bone,

02:59:49.500 --> 02:59:55.500
noting how each pawn, knight, rook, bishop,
king and queen were all about the size of

02:59:55.500 --> 02:59:56.980
a human finger bone.

02:59:56.980 --> 03:00:04.390
He dismissed the idea; nobody would ever do
something like that.

03:00:04.390 --> 03:00:08.180
Eric grabbed a sheet covering a larger component
hooked up to the mess.

03:00:08.180 --> 03:00:14.380
Lifting it away in a swift pull, it unveiled
what was sitting beneath: a full suit of 18th

03:00:14.380 --> 03:00:15.550
Century Samurai armor.

03:00:15.550 --> 03:00:20.439
Eric looked closer at the embellishments on
the surface of the pauldrons; he was no expert

03:00:20.439 --> 03:00:26.040
on Feudal Japan, but it looked authentic enough
to be the real thing, if not a very close

03:00:26.040 --> 03:00:27.470
approximation.

03:00:27.470 --> 03:00:31.380
Taking a look at the collection of oddities
all tethered together in his father’s lab,

03:00:31.380 --> 03:00:36.460
a certain detail of all his chess research
came to the forefront of Eric’s mind.

03:00:36.460 --> 03:00:42.890
The armor had given it away: this was ‘The
Samurai’ – or, at the very least, a least

03:00:42.890 --> 03:00:46.200
a crude, homemade version of it that his dad
had put together.

03:00:46.200 --> 03:00:50.040
But if it worked, it was also something to
practice against.

03:00:50.040 --> 03:00:54.500
It didn’t take Eric long to start tinkering
with the contraption, trying to get it to

03:00:54.500 --> 03:00:55.500
work.

03:00:55.500 --> 03:00:59.149
All the while, the question of why his dad
owned such a thing kept drumming up noise

03:00:59.149 --> 03:01:00.540
in the back of his head.

03:01:00.540 --> 03:01:05.800
Had Brian built it, or was this the original
made by that Russian chess prodigy?

03:01:05.800 --> 03:01:09.780
Was this machine the reason that Eric’s
dad possessed such an unbeatable skill at

03:01:09.780 --> 03:01:10.780
chess?

03:01:10.780 --> 03:01:15.800
And would using it give him the edge he needed
to best him at the game and earn his father’s

03:01:15.800 --> 03:01:16.800
respect?

03:01:16.800 --> 03:01:22.310
After what felt like hours upon hours of trial
and error with a machine he could barely comprehend,

03:01:22.310 --> 03:01:23.729
Eric seemed to have cracked it.

03:01:23.729 --> 03:01:28.340
As far as he could tell, the steam engine
powered the whole contraption and could be

03:01:28.340 --> 03:01:33.050
set to five different speeds, labeled on the
side in roman numerals.

03:01:33.050 --> 03:01:37.250
The power from the engine was then fed to
some kind of sophisticated mechanism that

03:01:37.250 --> 03:01:42.600
was within the suit of samurai armor, allowing
it to move, and what appeared to be a series

03:01:42.600 --> 03:01:47.489
of electromagnets that moved the chess pieces
and kept them on the board.

03:01:47.489 --> 03:01:52.310
Flicking it onto the third-highest of the
five speed levels, the machine whirred into

03:01:52.310 --> 03:01:56.630
life; the sounds of creaking, and grinding
of metal filled the lab.

03:01:56.630 --> 03:01:59.620
Kneeling opposite, Eric went to make the first
move…

03:01:59.620 --> 03:02:00.779
only to stop himself.

03:02:00.779 --> 03:02:05.631
It wasn’t that he’d changed his mind about
practicing against the Samurai, but because

03:02:05.631 --> 03:02:08.210
of the speed he’d set it to.

03:02:08.210 --> 03:02:12.320
Determining that the settings must have correlated
to difficulty levels, Eric figured that if

03:02:12.320 --> 03:02:17.080
he really wanted to get the most rigorous
practice, to really hone his chess moves,

03:02:17.080 --> 03:02:19.130
he needed to commit fully.

03:02:19.130 --> 03:02:23.351
Reaching for the dial, he turned the device
up to its fifth and highest speed, then made

03:02:23.351 --> 03:02:24.859
his first move.

03:02:24.859 --> 03:02:28.920
He pushed one of the bone-colored pawns forward
by a single square and waited.

03:02:28.920 --> 03:02:34.640
A split second passed, and the arm of the
early automaton responded with its first countermove.

03:02:34.640 --> 03:02:39.350
It was quick, almost moving with the same
natural fluidity and speed as an actual human

03:02:39.350 --> 03:02:45.300
being; albeit still with a little bit of creaking,
and some slight, clockwork-like stutters.

03:02:45.300 --> 03:02:48.609
But it worked nonetheless, the machine could
play.

03:02:48.609 --> 03:02:53.810
The tension over the first game was palpable,
forming a layer of sweat over Eric’s forehead.

03:02:53.810 --> 03:02:57.989
Every whirr and tick of the machine gave the
impression they were playing with a stop clock

03:02:57.989 --> 03:03:01.290
timing each of their moves, adding to the
urgency.

03:03:01.290 --> 03:03:05.860
Despite this, Eric Matthews tried his best
to stay calm.

03:03:05.860 --> 03:03:11.460
This was practice after all, a dry run, not
the inevitable game he’d play against his

03:03:11.460 --> 03:03:12.460
dad.

03:03:12.460 --> 03:03:16.280
With every move he made, his heart drummed
against his ribs, uncertain he’d made the

03:03:16.280 --> 03:03:17.580
right call.

03:03:17.580 --> 03:03:22.140
Each time the robotic hand cruelly knocked
one of his pieces away, Eric felt the surge

03:03:22.140 --> 03:03:25.200
of frustration, but told himself to quell
it.

03:03:25.200 --> 03:03:30.190
He kept focused, used what he’d researched
to adapt and respond accordingly to each of

03:03:30.190 --> 03:03:32.239
the machine’s moves, until…

03:03:32.239 --> 03:03:33.239
Checkmate.

03:03:33.239 --> 03:03:38.880
He’d beaten it – he might have lost everything
save for a knight, a rook, his king and queen

03:03:38.880 --> 03:03:40.779
– but he had won.

03:03:40.779 --> 03:03:45.020
Trapped without anywhere else to move on the
board, the metal finger of the automaton conceded

03:03:45.020 --> 03:03:48.640
the game, knocking over its own king in resignation.

03:03:48.640 --> 03:03:53.680
Panting, heart racing from the sheer excitement
of being on the winning side of a game, Eric

03:03:53.680 --> 03:03:56.569
hurriedly gathered up and reset all the pieces.

03:03:56.569 --> 03:04:01.380
He had to go again, not just so he could be
certain it wasn’t a fluke, but to make sure

03:04:01.380 --> 03:04:04.920
he had what it took to take on his professor
father.

03:04:04.920 --> 03:04:08.880
Back and forth Eric went with the chess machine,
over and over again.

03:04:08.880 --> 03:04:11.460
They were fairly evenly matched, it seemed.

03:04:11.460 --> 03:04:14.920
Eric won the second game, only to be best
on the next two.

03:04:14.920 --> 03:04:19.289
But it was some time afterward – he had
lost count of exactly how many rounds later

03:04:19.289 --> 03:04:21.900
– that things started to change.

03:04:21.900 --> 03:04:26.290
Maybe it was the age and condition of the
Victorian era chess computer, the natural

03:04:26.290 --> 03:04:28.890
wear and tear stopping it from functioning
properly.

03:04:28.890 --> 03:04:33.710
But Eric noticed the Samurai started to make
moves that were illogical, that practically

03:04:33.710 --> 03:04:37.860
offered him the upper hand with no discardable
strategy behind them.

03:04:37.860 --> 03:04:42.649
Then, its movements became flat-out illegal,
disregarding the directions and number of

03:04:42.649 --> 03:04:46.060
squares each different piece was allowed to
move.

03:04:46.060 --> 03:04:50.510
Before long, it was moving them erratically
around the chessboard, refusing to cooperate

03:04:50.510 --> 03:04:54.590
and forcing Eric to call an end to the day’s
practicing.

03:04:54.590 --> 03:04:58.580
Unfortunately, it wouldn’t be the end of
the unusual things that would happen that

03:04:58.580 --> 03:04:59.580
day.

03:04:59.580 --> 03:05:02.500
“Did you send me that weird email?”

03:05:02.500 --> 03:05:06.840
was the first question Eric’s dad asked
him when he returned home, looking noticeably

03:05:06.840 --> 03:05:08.040
under the weather.

03:05:08.040 --> 03:05:12.000
Confused, Eric said he had no idea what his
father was talking about.

03:05:12.000 --> 03:05:15.830
Professor Matthews then went on to describe
what he’d received on his work computer.

03:05:15.830 --> 03:05:20.590
It had been an email, with a file attached
to it named ‘shakhmaty’, a word in Russian

03:05:20.590 --> 03:05:22.240
that translates to ‘chess’.

03:05:22.240 --> 03:05:27.460
Embedded in the email below – although it
had taken a long time to load being opened

03:05:27.460 --> 03:05:31.600
on a 1990s computer – there had apparently
been a photo as well.

03:05:31.600 --> 03:05:34.910
“It was rather odd, Eric,” Brian went
on.

03:05:34.910 --> 03:05:36.910
“Quite unnerving to tell the truth.

03:05:36.910 --> 03:05:40.410
Black and white, all sort of distorted and
stretched.

03:05:40.410 --> 03:05:45.750
But it looked like two young girls, one grinning
and the other screaming.

03:05:45.750 --> 03:05:47.060
I’ve been feeling…

03:05:47.060 --> 03:05:49.770
well, not quite myself since I saw it.”

03:05:49.770 --> 03:05:54.320
With that, Brian excused himself, stating
he’d been suffering from headaches and a

03:05:54.320 --> 03:05:57.530
high temperature, and as a result, needed
to go and lay down.

03:05:57.530 --> 03:06:01.570
It wasn’t like his father to get ill, Eric
thought, but of course, he had no reason to

03:06:01.570 --> 03:06:04.069
assume it was anything serious.

03:06:04.069 --> 03:06:08.540
Probably just a spate of fatigue after a long
day of teaching at the university, no cause

03:06:08.540 --> 03:06:09.649
for concern.

03:06:09.649 --> 03:06:11.960
If only that’s all it was.

03:06:11.960 --> 03:06:17.060
Within a few hours, Brian was completely restless,
so unable to sleep that simply taking a nap

03:06:17.060 --> 03:06:18.130
was impossible.

03:06:18.130 --> 03:06:23.170
He kept calling to Eric, complaining of the
sound of childlike laughter coming from somewhere

03:06:23.170 --> 03:06:26.670
in the house, but his son hadn’t heard anything.

03:06:26.670 --> 03:06:31.480
By the time the sun went down, Eric was trying
to calm his dad down through a rush of intense

03:06:31.480 --> 03:06:33.640
anxiety that gripped him.

03:06:33.640 --> 03:06:38.189
Brian had claimed to be hallucinating, seeing
the warped faces of two girls that frightened

03:06:38.189 --> 03:06:39.590
him half to death.

03:06:39.590 --> 03:06:44.750
It was getting late, long past the time they
usually played chess together, but for now,

03:06:44.750 --> 03:06:47.720
Eric’s mind was focused solely on helping
his dad.

03:06:47.720 --> 03:06:52.569
For a while, he seemed to be able to calm
his father down, only to realize Brian wasn’t

03:06:52.569 --> 03:06:53.730
settled at all.

03:06:53.730 --> 03:06:59.130
He was awake, eyes open, fully conscious,
but wasn’t responding at all to Eric asking

03:06:59.130 --> 03:07:00.330
if he was alright.

03:07:00.330 --> 03:07:04.580
Instead, the accomplished academic just stared
blankly into space.

03:07:04.580 --> 03:07:09.540
Eric had been up all night, exhausted, worried
for Brian’s safety, and completely clueless

03:07:09.540 --> 03:07:11.500
about what was happening to him.

03:07:11.500 --> 03:07:15.569
After a while of being non-responsive, his
dad seemed to regain a little bit of lucidity

03:07:15.569 --> 03:07:18.140
once more, but his behavior was erratic.

03:07:18.140 --> 03:07:22.020
“Take me back to work, I need to get on
my computer!”

03:07:22.020 --> 03:07:23.410
Brian demanded of his son.

03:07:23.410 --> 03:07:27.580
When Eric refused, that’s when his dad got
angry, and agitated.

03:07:27.580 --> 03:07:31.979
Professor Brian Matthews was sadly found dead
within the next few months.

03:07:31.979 --> 03:07:37.010
Several months later, as the sole executor
of his father’s estate and last living relative,

03:07:37.010 --> 03:07:40.729
Eric had to be the one to go through his dad’s
personal belongings.

03:07:40.729 --> 03:07:46.090
Volumes upon volumes of thick academic books,
his smart, scholarly clothes.

03:07:46.090 --> 03:07:50.979
The house was almost clear now, save for one
thing that was left in the basement lab.

03:07:50.979 --> 03:07:56.020
Flicking on the light switch, Eric looked
at the Samurai sitting motionless, still uncovered

03:07:56.020 --> 03:07:57.880
after their last practice game.

03:07:57.880 --> 03:08:03.240
He’d sold off the chess pieces to a collector
in New York, now it was just an empty oddity.

03:08:03.240 --> 03:08:07.360
Eric placed a king on the board, one of the
ones that he and his dad had used when they

03:08:07.360 --> 03:08:08.630
played each other.

03:08:08.630 --> 03:08:13.970
With a gentle flick of his finger, he toppled
it, resigning to the strange automaton and

03:08:13.970 --> 03:08:15.880
leaving it there in the laboratory.

03:08:15.880 --> 03:08:20.080
He wasn’t sure how, but he had some kind
of gut feeling that this chess machine had

03:08:20.080 --> 03:08:22.970
somehow been responsible for his father’s
fate.

03:08:22.970 --> 03:08:29.899
Of course, Eric had no idea what the device
actually was, and what… or who lay beneath

03:08:29.899 --> 03:08:32.399
its whirring metal parts.

03:08:32.399 --> 03:08:38.529
SCP-1875 is the designation given to this
machine now, and has been ever since the Foundation

03:08:38.529 --> 03:08:41.950
recovered it not long after Eric sold his
father’s house.

03:08:41.950 --> 03:08:46.100
They were able to learn much more about it
than young Mr. Matthews, or even the late

03:08:46.100 --> 03:08:48.390
Professor Matthews, ever had.

03:08:48.390 --> 03:08:53.780
Not just how to make it work or what its function
was, but who had built it… and who had been

03:08:53.780 --> 03:08:55.380
used to build it.

03:08:55.380 --> 03:08:59.800
Although details of his real name eluded the
Foundation’s top researchers, they were

03:08:59.800 --> 03:09:04.551
able to uncover newspaper articles about the
device from all over Russia, America, and

03:09:04.551 --> 03:09:07.819
England, dating back to the early 1900s.

03:09:07.819 --> 03:09:12.561
The Russian chess prodigy who’d invented
it and toured the chess-playing machine around,

03:09:12.561 --> 03:09:16.070
had used some interesting components to make
his automaton.

03:09:16.070 --> 03:09:20.130
Maybe they were where it derived its skill
at the game, and its apparent temperament

03:09:20.130 --> 03:09:24.020
when the device was made to play at maximum
speed for too long.

03:09:24.020 --> 03:09:28.140
He had used his daughters to make the machine.

03:09:28.140 --> 03:09:32.710
Deep within the heart of this early form of
computer, the two girls brain tissue had been

03:09:32.710 --> 03:09:34.649
hooked up to the electromagnets.

03:09:34.649 --> 03:09:39.859
The machine’s moves were theirs; each pawn
or rook shifting across the black and white

03:09:39.859 --> 03:09:45.360
squares of the board, every rule or strategy
their prodigy father had taught, it all determined

03:09:45.360 --> 03:09:47.200
how the device played.

03:09:47.200 --> 03:09:51.920
Their minds, taken from their skulls, were
now the analytical engine of their father’s

03:09:51.920 --> 03:09:52.920
creation.

03:09:52.920 --> 03:09:57.130
The bones of their fingers, he'd carved into
chess pieces.

03:09:57.130 --> 03:10:03.880
Over the coming weeks, the Foundation naturally
ran their usual slew of tests on SCP-1875,

03:10:03.880 --> 03:10:05.970
playing multiple games of chess against it.

03:10:05.970 --> 03:10:11.390
Every time, they increased the machine’s
five levels of speed, until it started behaving

03:10:11.390 --> 03:10:14.189
erratically while on the highest setting.

03:10:14.189 --> 03:10:20.050
Shortly after, every member of personnel working
on SCP-1875 received a bizarre email.

03:10:20.050 --> 03:10:24.279
It contained a file, named with the Russian
word for chess…

03:10:24.279 --> 03:10:30.340
And a photograph; of the stretched, distorted,
smiling, and screaming faces of two young

03:10:30.340 --> 03:10:31.810
girls.

03:10:31.810 --> 03:10:34.760
Faces that you might be seeing very soon…

03:10:34.760 --> 03:10:38.279
‘You all know exactly who I am.’

03:10:38.279 --> 03:10:42.470
The bald chemistry teacher looked around at
the men surrounding him in the desert.

03:10:42.470 --> 03:10:45.710
Cadillacs, leather jackets, blacked-out shades.

03:10:45.710 --> 03:10:51.020
The gang members shifted nervously from foot
to foot as the man bragged openly about what

03:10:51.020 --> 03:10:54.030
he’s done to build his criminal empire.

03:10:54.030 --> 03:10:58.560
Walter White’s attention turned back to
the man in front of him, a slight sneer played

03:10:58.560 --> 03:11:00.210
around his lips.

03:11:00.210 --> 03:11:02.580
‘Now, say my name.’

03:11:02.580 --> 03:11:06.290
The man took a moment to reply as the desert
wind blew behind him.

03:11:06.290 --> 03:11:11.170
He seemed to be weighing his options, realizing
quickly that he had lost all control over

03:11:11.170 --> 03:11:12.290
the situation.

03:11:12.290 --> 03:11:13.290
‘Heisenberg.’

03:11:13.290 --> 03:11:15.029
‘You’re g-’

03:11:15.029 --> 03:11:16.029
BANG!

03:11:16.029 --> 03:11:18.120
Walter White fell to the ground dead.

03:11:18.120 --> 03:11:22.730
The characters all around yelped in surprise,
drew their guns, and started wildly pointing

03:11:22.730 --> 03:11:27.510
them around the circle, trying to figure out
who killed the great Heisenberg.

03:11:27.510 --> 03:11:31.729
But his killer just stared in awe from his
couch in the front room as the Netflix show

03:11:31.729 --> 03:11:33.920
kept playing on the TV.

03:11:33.920 --> 03:11:37.220
Had the toy gun in his hand really done that?

03:11:37.220 --> 03:11:38.220
*

03:11:38.220 --> 03:11:41.370
Josh had admitted to himself that he had a
problem a long time ago.

03:11:41.370 --> 03:11:47.270
But at least being a yard-sale addict was
cheaper than just a regular old retail addict.

03:11:47.270 --> 03:11:51.819
Actually in truth, he was also an estate-sale
addict, a garage-sale addict and a thrift

03:11:51.819 --> 03:11:53.390
store addict.

03:11:53.390 --> 03:11:58.370
Anywhere you could buy people’s old junk
for rock-bottom prices, he’d sniff it out.

03:11:58.370 --> 03:12:03.060
The Saturday that his life changed forever
was just like any other Saturday, really.

03:12:03.060 --> 03:12:08.420
In the sweaty heat of the Atlantic City suburbs,
he hadn’t meant to stop by any sales today,

03:12:08.420 --> 03:12:11.229
but one just so happened to catch his eye.

03:12:11.229 --> 03:12:16.220
There was a box marked ‘Nerd Stuff’ and
he took that as a personal challenge.

03:12:16.220 --> 03:12:20.351
Was this some old lady’s version of nerd
stuff or a veteran Dungeon Master’s treasure

03:12:20.351 --> 03:12:21.351
trove?

03:12:21.351 --> 03:12:23.960
The reality was somewhere in-between the two.

03:12:23.960 --> 03:12:29.160
There were a couple of seasons of Buffy on
VHS, a copy of the original Deus Ex, and some

03:12:29.160 --> 03:12:31.029
Legend of Zelda plushies.

03:12:31.029 --> 03:12:35.521
It hadn’t really been worth stopping his
car for, except for a little plastic splash

03:12:35.521 --> 03:12:37.710
of color that caught his eye.

03:12:37.710 --> 03:12:42.070
Josh reached way down to the bottom of the
box and rooted around for it.

03:12:42.070 --> 03:12:46.460
There was no way, it couldn’t possibly be
the toy he’d spent all his childhood asking

03:12:46.460 --> 03:12:47.859
for and never getting.

03:12:47.859 --> 03:12:50.160
But it was!

03:12:50.160 --> 03:12:53.740
A 1985 Nintendo Entertainment System Zapper!

03:12:53.740 --> 03:12:59.649
With an orange grip and barrel, NES gray bodywork,
and the beautiful font printed along the side,

03:12:59.649 --> 03:13:00.870
it was unmistakable.

03:13:00.870 --> 03:13:06.529
He threw a wad of cash at the old lady behind
the table and rushed back into his car, quickly

03:13:06.529 --> 03:13:07.569
as he could.

03:13:07.569 --> 03:13:12.670
It was time to cancel his day plans, he had
a childhood dream to fulfill!

03:13:12.670 --> 03:13:18.040
But no sooner than he had got home and flipped
on his TV, the dream came crashing down around

03:13:18.040 --> 03:13:19.040
him.

03:13:19.040 --> 03:13:24.060
His old NES, the one he’d had since he was
a little kid, had sadly keeled over.

03:13:24.060 --> 03:13:27.360
It refused to power on, no matter what he
did.

03:13:27.360 --> 03:13:30.130
So that made his Zapper pretty much useless.

03:13:30.130 --> 03:13:35.510
The best he could do with it now would be
to point it at the TV and make pew pew noises

03:13:35.510 --> 03:13:36.800
with his mouth.

03:13:36.800 --> 03:13:41.200
So Josh opened up Netflix and put on an episode
of Breaking Bad.

03:13:41.200 --> 03:13:46.600
Little did he know, as he raised the Zapper
to the screen, that he was actually in possession

03:13:46.600 --> 03:13:49.520
of SCP-674.

03:13:49.520 --> 03:13:53.630
Nobody would know looking at it on the surface,
even the most hardcore gamers wouldn’t have

03:13:53.630 --> 03:13:58.160
a clue, because you can fully disassemble,
clean, and reassemble the Zapper and get no

03:13:58.160 --> 03:14:01.920
indication that it’s anything other than
what it appears to be.

03:14:01.920 --> 03:14:06.710
However, as soon as the gun is pointed at
a television screen or a computer monitor

03:14:06.710 --> 03:14:10.930
of any kind, it starts to pose a very real
threat.

03:14:10.930 --> 03:14:14.189
Or, at least, a very real fictional threat.

03:14:14.189 --> 03:14:18.750
If you shoot at the screen, whatever media
is playing on it at the time will react and

03:14:18.750 --> 03:14:21.470
adapt to what has just happened.

03:14:21.470 --> 03:14:25.170
That means characters on the screens will
react in accordance with the rules of their

03:14:25.170 --> 03:14:26.950
respective universes.

03:14:26.950 --> 03:14:31.860
In a grounded, realistic drama, the gunshot
will behave like any other, killing or wounding

03:14:31.860 --> 03:14:36.770
the victim with perhaps enough time for them
to give a dying monologue, but in an episode

03:14:36.770 --> 03:14:42.370
of Rick and Morty, the shot could manifest
in any number of bizarre creative ways.

03:14:42.370 --> 03:14:46.250
News crews that witness the user shooting
a person in their live report will continue

03:14:46.250 --> 03:14:50.120
to cover the incident as if it is part of
the ongoing story.

03:14:50.120 --> 03:14:54.290
If you shoot Walter White, he falls to the
ground dead.

03:14:54.290 --> 03:14:56.521
Josh stared at the screen in utter amazement.

03:14:56.521 --> 03:15:00.160
He didn’t expect the writers to kill off
their main character just like that.

03:15:00.160 --> 03:15:03.020
Wasn’t there supposed to be another 9 episodes?

03:15:03.020 --> 03:15:05.260
What would they do to fill all that time?

03:15:05.260 --> 03:15:09.810
As the episode played out, it seemed to mostly
just follow Jesse, trying desperately to escape

03:15:09.810 --> 03:15:11.710
with his money before it was too late.

03:15:11.710 --> 03:15:17.120
He kept glancing towards the camera for some
reason, with a slight look of fear in his

03:15:17.120 --> 03:15:18.120
eyes.

03:15:18.120 --> 03:15:19.780
Very strange creative decision there.

03:15:19.780 --> 03:15:23.510
Josh backed out of the show to scroll through
his “continue watching.”

03:15:23.510 --> 03:15:27.540
There was something a little creepy about
how he’d pointed that Zapper at the TV,

03:15:27.540 --> 03:15:32.480
pulled the trigger, and perfectly, at that
moment, a character had been shot.

03:15:32.480 --> 03:15:36.750
Time to pick out a show without any guns and
murder for a bit.

03:15:36.750 --> 03:15:37.770
Something safe.

03:15:37.770 --> 03:15:42.120
The familiar melodica tune filled the lounge
as various shots of Dunder Mifflin employees

03:15:42.120 --> 03:15:46.000
busied themselves, shredding credit cards
and answering phone calls.

03:15:46.000 --> 03:15:50.820
It never ceased to make his worries go away
seeing the words ‘The Office’ appear on

03:15:50.820 --> 03:15:51.870
the screen.

03:15:51.870 --> 03:15:55.650
He settled into the couch, watching as the
characters all gathered in the conference

03:15:55.650 --> 03:15:57.770
room for some first-aid training.

03:15:57.770 --> 03:16:02.310
The CPR dummy lay on the floor with Michael
Scott earnestly explaining to the woman that

03:16:02.310 --> 03:16:04.400
the victim appeared to have lost their limbs.

03:16:04.400 --> 03:16:09.640
He laughed along as the scene descended into
chaos, as none of the employees took the training

03:16:09.640 --> 03:16:11.271
seriously at all.

03:16:11.271 --> 03:16:15.279
Even though he’d seen this episode countless
times, it never got old.

03:16:15.279 --> 03:16:19.510
Absent-mindedly, he pointed the gun at the
TV and pulled the trigger.

03:16:19.510 --> 03:16:20.510
BANG!

03:16:20.510 --> 03:16:22.170
Pam collapsed on the ground.

03:16:22.170 --> 03:16:26.930
All of the characters dived out of the way,
trying to find cover behind their chairs,

03:16:26.930 --> 03:16:29.700
not daring to peek out at the camera.

03:16:29.700 --> 03:16:33.800
Only the first aid lady stayed on the scene,
trying her best to resuscitate Pam.

03:16:33.800 --> 03:16:37.200
It cut to a shot of Dwight talking to the
camera sadly.

03:16:37.200 --> 03:16:41.550
He said something that was almost funny, but
there was a real heaviness in the moment.

03:16:41.550 --> 03:16:45.180
His eyes were red, like he’d been crying
off-camera.

03:16:45.180 --> 03:16:49.260
Josh flipped the TV off and stared at himself
in the dark reflection.

03:16:49.260 --> 03:16:50.260
Was he dreaming?

03:16:50.260 --> 03:16:53.279
He hadn’t just done that… had he?

03:16:53.279 --> 03:16:58.560
Fortunately for Josh and for the rest of humanity,
he had not killed Jenna Fisher or Bryan Cranston.

03:16:58.560 --> 03:17:02.780
He hadn’t even killed their characters in
the shows, at least not for anyone other than

03:17:02.780 --> 03:17:04.140
himself.

03:17:04.140 --> 03:17:09.670
Research into SCP-674 shows that the appearance
of characters being killed off on screen only

03:17:09.670 --> 03:17:12.220
exists in the mind of the shooter.

03:17:12.220 --> 03:17:16.360
For everyone else, The Office remains the
same as it always was.

03:17:16.360 --> 03:17:20.430
Unfortunately for Josh, however, Pam is now
dead in that universe and will always be whenever

03:17:20.430 --> 03:17:22.090
he watches an episode.

03:17:22.090 --> 03:17:26.739
It leaves a real shadow over the later seasons,
but the characters apparently manage to do

03:17:26.739 --> 03:17:30.140
a good job pivoting into its new darker tone.

03:17:30.140 --> 03:17:34.920
Other shows that have been tested in research
conditions have yielded interesting results.

03:17:34.920 --> 03:17:39.280
Like in Star Trek, it is impossible to actually
kill Captain Kirk.

03:17:39.280 --> 03:17:43.420
Instead, various red shirts always just so
happen to dive in front of him or fall into

03:17:43.420 --> 03:17:45.530
frame at the pivotal moment.

03:17:45.530 --> 03:17:49.390
With a small dead zone on the right-hand side
of the screen, characters who witness enough

03:17:49.390 --> 03:17:53.850
shootings have the sense to stick to that
side of the frame whenever the shot is on

03:17:53.850 --> 03:17:58.890
them, sometimes going so far as to plead directly
with the viewer, breaking the fourth wall

03:17:58.890 --> 03:18:01.130
in their desperation to survive.

03:18:01.130 --> 03:18:05.989
When Josh came back from a long walk outside,
he’d convinced himself that what he had

03:18:05.989 --> 03:18:08.979
seen earlier was all just some vivid dream.

03:18:08.979 --> 03:18:14.140
A dream he didn’t remember waking up from
but definitely wasn’t happening anymore.

03:18:14.140 --> 03:18:17.330
He’d play some video games, that’s what
he’d do.

03:18:17.330 --> 03:18:22.080
He had decided he would just play some Super
Smash Bros and unwind.

03:18:22.080 --> 03:18:25.970
Anyone who has ever said that sentence to
themselves will know firsthand how foolish

03:18:25.970 --> 03:18:27.000
that is.

03:18:27.000 --> 03:18:32.200
Not ten minutes later, Josh had definitely
forgotten all about his dream from before.

03:18:32.200 --> 03:18:35.210
He was too busy raging and cursing at the
screen.

03:18:35.210 --> 03:18:40.520
He couldn’t stand playing against King Dedede,
that stupid hammer move always got him.

03:18:40.520 --> 03:18:44.500
Without thinking, Josh snatched the Zapper
from the couch next to him and blasted Dedede

03:18:44.500 --> 03:18:46.000
right in the face.

03:18:46.000 --> 03:18:50.979
In classic Smash Bros style, the giant penguin
king was launched backwards, flying off the

03:18:50.979 --> 03:18:53.670
screen in a blast of colorful smoke.

03:18:53.670 --> 03:18:55.370
But he didn’t respawn.

03:18:55.370 --> 03:18:58.700
For a second, his character just stood on
the screen awkwardly before it kicked him

03:18:58.700 --> 03:19:01.320
back out to the character select screen.

03:19:01.320 --> 03:19:04.700
This time though, King Dedede’s picture
was missing.

03:19:04.700 --> 03:19:06.420
Josh couldn’t help himself.

03:19:06.420 --> 03:19:10.620
He set up a free for all match straight away,
picking all of the characters he hated to

03:19:10.620 --> 03:19:11.750
fight against.

03:19:11.750 --> 03:19:17.070
Sonic, Mario, Pikachu, Bowser, Link, Nes,
even Wii Fit Trainer.

03:19:17.070 --> 03:19:18.729
One after the other.

03:19:18.729 --> 03:19:20.689
Bang bang bang!

03:19:20.689 --> 03:19:24.350
He felt a strange sense of satisfaction once
he’d done his work.

03:19:24.350 --> 03:19:28.660
Great, now the character list only had the
fair, balanced ones left.

03:19:28.660 --> 03:19:32.149
They just so happened to all be his favorite
characters too.

03:19:32.149 --> 03:19:33.739
But why stop there?

03:19:33.739 --> 03:19:38.279
Josh jumped across to Elden Ring and marched
his knight up through the gate to Stormveil

03:19:38.279 --> 03:19:39.279
Castle.

03:19:39.279 --> 03:19:43.239
Before Margit had even gotten through his
opening cutscene, Josh had shot the boss right

03:19:43.239 --> 03:19:44.410
in the face.

03:19:44.410 --> 03:19:49.460
This boss battle that he’d been stuck on
for hours was over before it even started.

03:19:49.460 --> 03:19:50.460
What else could he do now?

03:19:50.460 --> 03:19:55.260
He put on an episode of Stranger Things and
shot the Demogorgon, then Moff Gideon in the

03:19:55.260 --> 03:19:58.479
Mandalorian, and some goblins in Hogwarts
Legacy.

03:19:58.479 --> 03:20:03.029
The clickers from the Last of Us took a couple
of extra shots but went down easy enough.

03:20:03.029 --> 03:20:07.600
He went over to his PC and killed the Ender
Dragon in Minecraft without blowing up any

03:20:07.600 --> 03:20:08.600
beds.

03:20:08.600 --> 03:20:13.290
He won a game of Rocket League 53-0 after
he shot both cars on the opposing team.

03:20:13.290 --> 03:20:16.470
He played through DOORS in Roblox without
a single jumpscare.

03:20:16.470 --> 03:20:20.290
He’d just shoot any monster that filled
his screen.

03:20:20.290 --> 03:20:21.290
What could be better?

03:20:21.290 --> 03:20:23.870
He reckoned it was time for more movies.

03:20:23.870 --> 03:20:28.399
Walking back across the lounge, spinning the
gun around his finger like a Western, he wondered

03:20:28.399 --> 03:20:33.779
if now would be a good chance to hunt down
all the legendary animals in Red Dead 2.

03:20:33.779 --> 03:20:38.240
It would certainly make the job a lot easier
if he could kill them all in just one shot.

03:20:38.240 --> 03:20:42.040
He’d just need to be careful not to shoot
Arthur by accident.

03:20:42.040 --> 03:20:44.120
But then Josh sat on the remote.

03:20:44.120 --> 03:20:49.230
The TV flicked over to an old rerun of Bob
Ross just as he’d been squaring up to shoot.

03:20:49.230 --> 03:20:51.540
He pulled the trigger before he could react.

03:20:51.540 --> 03:20:53.800
The canvas was spattered red.

03:20:53.800 --> 03:20:58.989
From somewhere off-camera he heard the artist’s
wheezing voice try to comfort him.

03:20:58.989 --> 03:21:03.580
‘There are no mistakes, just happy…’

03:21:03.580 --> 03:21:06.050
But he died before he could say another word.

03:21:06.050 --> 03:21:07.050
*

03:21:07.050 --> 03:21:10.210
The Exposition Gun first came onto the SCP
Foundation’s radar when a strange police

03:21:10.210 --> 03:21:14.630
report was doing the rounds on an email chain
amongst local authorities.

03:21:14.630 --> 03:21:19.790
They were all finding a great deal of value
from a man called Josh, who had turned himself

03:21:19.790 --> 03:21:24.359
in at his local station in Atlantic City for
‘killing like everyone’.

03:21:24.359 --> 03:21:29.949
Naturally, the police took his report very
seriously, for all of about 10 minutes.

03:21:29.949 --> 03:21:34.210
As soon as they realized that he was talking
about video games, TV, and movie characters,

03:21:34.210 --> 03:21:36.740
the whole case turned into one big joke.

03:21:36.740 --> 03:21:41.330
They sat Josh down in front of a TV and played
him episodes of the shows he was talking about,

03:21:41.330 --> 03:21:46.609
pointing out characters still alive in scenes
after he’d supposedly shot them to death,

03:21:46.609 --> 03:21:51.569
but he seemed to just grow more distressed
as he realized he was experiencing this phenomenon

03:21:51.569 --> 03:21:53.120
alone.

03:21:53.120 --> 03:21:57.210
By the time, Foundation agents tracked Josh
down and took him in for questioning, the

03:21:57.210 --> 03:21:58.460
gun had moved on.

03:21:58.460 --> 03:22:02.200
He’d marched it straight back to the old
lady who’d sold it to him, who had then

03:22:02.200 --> 03:22:03.750
sold it on again.

03:22:03.750 --> 03:22:08.819
Josh’s full account of events and description
of the Zapper were taken before he was administered

03:22:08.819 --> 03:22:14.380
Class C amnestics and allowed to continue
with his yard-sale addiction uninhibited.

03:22:14.380 --> 03:22:20.590
SCP-674 was found some time later in the possession
of a 33-year-old Caucasian male simply referred

03:22:20.590 --> 03:22:27.580
to as Mr S. A reclusive man in a neighboring
city, Mr S first gained the SCP Foundation's

03:22:27.580 --> 03:22:32.710
attention through his extremist political
blog, in which he claimed to have assassinated

03:22:32.710 --> 03:22:36.851
prominent political figures, news anchors,
and talk show hosts.

03:22:36.851 --> 03:22:42.090
While the truth of those claims was demonstrably
false, it was clear from the way he described

03:22:42.090 --> 03:22:45.500
events that he really did believe the delusion.

03:22:45.500 --> 03:22:49.000
After being questioned, it was discovered
that he had never left his home.

03:22:49.000 --> 03:22:53.720
Instead, he stayed on his couch, watching
news reports and firing at any prominent figures

03:22:53.720 --> 03:22:58.430
that appeared, believing that he was indeed
killing them in the real world.

03:22:58.430 --> 03:23:03.410
Despite claiming that he was using SCP-674
to prevent a military police state, using

03:23:03.410 --> 03:23:08.290
the device to "assassinate" so many high-profile
figures had the opposite effect on his video

03:23:08.290 --> 03:23:09.410
reality.

03:23:09.410 --> 03:23:15.500
In his blog posts, he described an increasingly
unstable global order, martial law, and catastrophic

03:23:15.500 --> 03:23:19.720
events consistent with the hypothetical release
of two deadly SCPs.

03:23:19.720 --> 03:23:24.740
His house was barricaded, with security cameras
set up in every direction.

03:23:24.740 --> 03:23:29.960
The more he had been using the gun, the more
the characters and journalists on TV had threatened

03:23:29.960 --> 03:23:34.620
him, claiming that the SCP Foundation were
on their way to apprehend him and that he

03:23:34.620 --> 03:23:37.989
would face retribution for what he had been
doing.

03:23:37.989 --> 03:23:41.320
Mr S’s intended ambush of Foundation agents
failed.

03:23:41.320 --> 03:23:46.040
While they appeared to be dying on the security
feeds he had set up, the agents experienced

03:23:46.040 --> 03:23:51.670
no physical harm in reality as they apprehended
the man and seized SCP-674.

03:23:51.670 --> 03:23:57.979
While the majority of researchers have deemed
that SCP-674 is harmless to all but the mental

03:23:57.979 --> 03:24:03.800
well-being of the shooter, there was a notably
sinister result to Mr S’s time in Foundation

03:24:03.800 --> 03:24:05.220
detention.

03:24:05.220 --> 03:24:09.979
Alone in his cell, Mr S soon started to engage
in heated arguments with the security camera

03:24:09.979 --> 03:24:11.760
set up to monitor him.

03:24:11.760 --> 03:24:16.380
There is no record of any voices in the room
other than his own, yet he seemed to be locked

03:24:16.380 --> 03:24:18.210
in a fierce argument.

03:24:18.210 --> 03:24:23.420
All of a sudden, Mr S dropped to the ground
dead with three .38 caliber handgun bullets

03:24:23.420 --> 03:24:25.310
lodged in his chest.

03:24:25.310 --> 03:24:30.720
The entirety of his holding cell was searched
for evidence of a shooter, but none was found.

03:24:30.720 --> 03:24:35.370
The Exposition Gun remains in a locked plastic
case in Storage Site 23.

03:24:35.370 --> 03:24:39.820
Aside from regular cleaning and maintenance,
it is used under controlled circumstances

03:24:39.820 --> 03:24:44.080
to creatively gather intelligence about further
SCP activity.

03:24:44.080 --> 03:24:48.600
In the event of the site being compromised,
personnel are allowed to destroy the Exposition

03:24:48.600 --> 03:24:53.240
Gun at their own discretion, should it fall
into the wrong hands.

03:24:53.240 --> 03:24:54.319
Rock.

03:24:54.319 --> 03:24:55.399
Paper.

03:24:55.399 --> 03:24:56.479
Scissors.

03:24:56.479 --> 03:25:01.060
A simple hand game dating back to the Han
Dynasty of Imperial China.

03:25:01.060 --> 03:25:07.260
It is a decisive game between two players,
with the only outcomes being win, lose, or

03:25:07.260 --> 03:25:08.260
draw.

03:25:08.260 --> 03:25:12.229
We’re quite familiar with the Rock-Paper-Scissors
here at the SCP Foundation,

03:25:12.229 --> 03:25:15.800
as the guards are often seen playing intense
rounds of the game to determine

03:25:15.800 --> 03:25:21.380
who will be the unlucky soul chosen to secure
one of various keter-class SCPs during a given

03:25:21.380 --> 03:25:22.380
week.

03:25:22.380 --> 03:25:27.390
In most cases, this use of Rock-Paper-Scissors
is accepted as a largely harmless and fair

03:25:27.390 --> 03:25:31.060
way for defensive personnel to arrange their
shifts.

03:25:31.060 --> 03:25:35.790
The reason for this is that the results of
Rock-Paper-Scissors are generally random,

03:25:35.790 --> 03:25:40.590
as there are very few ways for an individual’s
skill to influence the game.

03:25:40.590 --> 03:25:42.810
Or so one might think.

03:25:42.810 --> 03:25:46.500
In reality, Rock-Paper-Scissors is a deeply
psychological game,

03:25:46.500 --> 03:25:52.270
and this is mainly because of its origin as
a contest between fellow human beings.

03:25:52.270 --> 03:25:57.020
The average human is vulnerable to making
errors and falling into patterns,

03:25:57.020 --> 03:25:59.680
and with only three options to keep track
of,

03:25:59.680 --> 03:26:04.510
it is entirely possible for a seasoned veteran
of rock-paper-scissors to predict their opponent’s

03:26:04.510 --> 03:26:06.920
choice before any hands are thrown.

03:26:06.920 --> 03:26:11.540
Of course, that opponent may also commit an
error of their own, by misreading the other

03:26:11.540 --> 03:26:15.590
player’s actions,
and thus there is always a chance for an impulsive

03:26:15.590 --> 03:26:19.880
decision to swing the results of the game
in either direction.

03:26:19.880 --> 03:26:23.760
Human limitation is the only skill-based mechanic
in the game of Rock-Paper-Scissors,

03:26:23.760 --> 03:26:29.130
and that same limitation is what - in the
eyes of some - holds the game back from reaching

03:26:29.130 --> 03:26:31.021
its true potential.

03:26:31.021 --> 03:26:35.140
That was the case, until someone out there
discovered the rules to an anomalous version

03:26:35.140 --> 03:26:38.710
of Rock-Paper-Scissors being spread through
a mysterious email.

03:26:38.710 --> 03:26:43.170
While all attempts to trace the email back
to its original sender have left the Foundation

03:26:43.170 --> 03:26:47.040
with more questions than answers,
we have been able to learn more about the

03:26:47.040 --> 03:26:52.560
differences between the rules of standard
Rock-Paper-Scissors and the anomalous version.

03:26:52.560 --> 03:27:00.140
We have given this anomalous version of Rock-Paper-Scissors
the designation SCP-4633, and unlike the classic

03:27:00.140 --> 03:27:02.930
edition
- under no circumstances are any Foundation

03:27:02.930 --> 03:27:08.090
personnel allowed to play a game incorporating
any of the alternative rules.

03:27:08.090 --> 03:27:12.540
While the ceiling for skill and variety is
theoretically much higher in a Rock-Paper-Scissors

03:27:12.540 --> 03:27:18.020
match featuring SCP-4633,
the risks to the players far outweigh any

03:27:18.020 --> 03:27:19.710
added novelty.

03:27:19.710 --> 03:27:24.510
Here is how SCP-4633 functions during an average
game of Rock-Paper-Scissors…

03:27:24.510 --> 03:27:29.180
well, average prior to the anomalous properties
taking effect:

03:27:29.180 --> 03:27:33.540
rather than being limited to using merely
rock, paper, or scissors - as the name of

03:27:33.540 --> 03:27:37.500
the game implies
- the players gain access to a series of additional

03:27:37.500 --> 03:27:40.819
hand gestures
which upon the act of being thrown become

03:27:40.819 --> 03:27:45.510
seamlessly integrated into both players’
shared understanding of the rules.

03:27:45.510 --> 03:27:51.859
This alone would be bizarre enough, but the
true harm caused by SCP-4633 is in the nature

03:27:51.859 --> 03:27:54.220
of the gestures themselves.

03:27:54.220 --> 03:27:59.130
When one of the non-standard gestures allowed
by SCP-4633 is used,

03:27:59.130 --> 03:28:03.890
the players’ hands will change shape in
ways that, under normal circumstances, would

03:28:03.890 --> 03:28:06.729
be anatomically impossible.

03:28:06.729 --> 03:28:10.109
Each of these non-standard gestures is distinct
from the next,

03:28:10.109 --> 03:28:14.700
with the only commonality being an unusual
tendency for the final gesture to resemble

03:28:14.700 --> 03:28:18.590
biological structures often seen in sea life.

03:28:18.590 --> 03:28:23.529
One particular gesture might cause a player
to rapidly grow a ring of additional fingers

03:28:23.529 --> 03:28:26.040
surrounding a gaping anemone-like mouth in
the palm,

03:28:26.040 --> 03:28:31.980
while another could result in the player’s
entire arm flattening into a fin-like appendage.

03:28:31.980 --> 03:28:35.840
Regardless of how severe a departure from
a typical human limb the final gesture would

03:28:35.840 --> 03:28:38.970
be,
the limbs – and on rare occasions, the entire

03:28:38.970 --> 03:28:45.439
body – of the player using the gesture will
quickly and irreversibly mutate into the shape

03:28:45.439 --> 03:28:47.500
required to successfully perform it.

03:28:47.500 --> 03:28:52.110
While these gestures overwhelmingly result
in forms that would be distressing to most

03:28:52.110 --> 03:28:54.960
people,
the same cognitive effect that causes the

03:28:54.960 --> 03:29:00.040
participants to accept changes to the rules
also appears to apply to the changes affecting

03:29:00.040 --> 03:29:01.580
their own bodies.

03:29:01.580 --> 03:29:07.750
To the players of an SCP-4633-augmented match
of Rock-Paper-Scissors, the nonstandard gestures

03:29:07.750 --> 03:29:10.670
seem as mundane as the original three.

03:29:10.670 --> 03:29:16.460
Unfortunately, the morphic properties of SCP-4633
are practically irreversible,

03:29:16.460 --> 03:29:21.240
with extreme reconstructive surgery being
required in even the best of cases.

03:29:21.240 --> 03:29:27.840
In all instances of SCP-4633, surgical intervention
is necessary to prevent the spread of details

03:29:27.840 --> 03:29:31.720
regarding the non-standard gestures and their
usage.

03:29:31.720 --> 03:29:35.840
Because of the immediate shift in cognitive
awareness among participants, all that is

03:29:35.840 --> 03:29:42.700
needed for a new instance of SCP-4633 to occur
is the faintest hint of knowledge of the alternative

03:29:42.700 --> 03:29:43.700
rules.

03:29:43.700 --> 03:29:46.930
It’s even been noted that between different
groups of players,

03:29:46.930 --> 03:29:52.050
the non-standard gestures can vary heavily
or seemingly be created on the fly as the

03:29:52.050 --> 03:29:55.250
desire to win at all costs takes hold.

03:29:55.250 --> 03:29:59.670
Here are a few examples of anomalous gestures
which have been observed during instances

03:29:59.670 --> 03:30:00.790
SCP-4633.

03:30:00.790 --> 03:30:08.851
The list is by no means comprehensive,
but it will provide an insight into how SCP-4633

03:30:08.851 --> 03:30:11.729
drastically alters the existing mechanics
of the game

03:30:11.729 --> 03:30:14.640
as well as the physique of the players.

03:30:14.640 --> 03:30:18.780
The “Thu-ry-leh” gesture transforms the
user’s fingers into barbed tentacles.

03:30:18.780 --> 03:30:21.819
It beats paper and scissors, but loses to
rock.

03:30:21.819 --> 03:30:26.500
“Cha-voo-aga” folds the fingers of the
hand into the palm and causes them to emerge

03:30:26.500 --> 03:30:28.540
through the back of the palm.

03:30:28.540 --> 03:30:33.370
It allows the user to throw a second gesture
after they’ve seen their opponent’s choice.

03:30:33.370 --> 03:30:37.699
“Aj-kel-haz” splits the hand into a pair
of poisonous stingers which also produce a

03:30:37.699 --> 03:30:40.200
potent electrical current between them.

03:30:40.200 --> 03:30:44.130
It has been seen to lose to paper, but seemingly
of their own will,

03:30:44.130 --> 03:30:48.130
the stingers lashed at the opposing player
and caused them to fall unconscious three

03:30:48.130 --> 03:30:51.189
rounds later due to the effects of the poison.

03:30:51.189 --> 03:30:56.609
“Sha-ooh-sa” beats paper and two other
anomalous gestures; loses to scissors, rock

03:30:56.609 --> 03:31:00.020
and a third gesture
and morphs the user’s arm into a dactyl

03:31:00.020 --> 03:31:03.620
club similar to the front appendages of a
mantis shrimp.

03:31:03.620 --> 03:31:08.239
“Iz-urg-av” simply causes the player to
grow three additional thumbs on one hand.

03:31:08.239 --> 03:31:10.900
The final gesture resembles a triple thumbs
up.

03:31:10.900 --> 03:31:16.189
Iz-urg-av has not been seen to beat any gestures,
and the player who uses it always seems to

03:31:16.189 --> 03:31:18.470
go on to lose the match.

03:31:18.470 --> 03:31:22.149
“Pag-ak-mar” causes the middle and ringer
to recede into the hand,

03:31:22.149 --> 03:31:27.640
while the pointer and pinky fingers extend
to resemble the eye stocks of a snail or slug.

03:31:27.640 --> 03:31:31.940
The rest of the hand also becomes coated in
a thin layer of slime which continues to secrete

03:31:31.940 --> 03:31:33.020
from within.

03:31:33.020 --> 03:31:37.840
Pag-ak-mar beats scissors and paper, but loses
to rock and another anomalous gesture known

03:31:37.840 --> 03:31:39.149
as “vah-yan-jek.”

03:31:39.149 --> 03:31:44.370
Incidentally, vah-yan-jek causes the arm of
the player using it to elongate into a worm-like

03:31:44.370 --> 03:31:48.970
tube that periodically spews a gout of salt
water on the opponent.

03:31:48.970 --> 03:31:53.050
When vah-yan-jek was seen beating pag-ak-mar,
the salt water appeared to have some adverse

03:31:53.050 --> 03:31:57.020
effect on the latter gesture,
causing the eyestalks to droop and the hand

03:31:57.020 --> 03:32:00.449
to shrivel until it was half its original
size.

03:32:00.449 --> 03:32:05.290
Another anomalous gesture is “gra-zath-rog”
recognized by the skin, muscle, and veins

03:32:05.290 --> 03:32:10.120
of the hand turning translucent,
revealing a pulsing red organ in the interior

03:32:10.120 --> 03:32:11.410
of the palm.

03:32:11.410 --> 03:32:16.410
Once gra-zath-rag has been thrown, the player
who used it may call out the name of one other

03:32:16.410 --> 03:32:19.010
gesture,
which can no longer be used in the current

03:32:19.010 --> 03:32:20.080
match.

03:32:20.080 --> 03:32:24.870
The “uka-yag” gesture is a bit deceptive,
as it resembles rock when first thrown, but

03:32:24.870 --> 03:32:29.160
gradually causes the hand to condense into
a lump of inert material

03:32:29.160 --> 03:32:31.630
not dissimilar to actual igneous rock.

03:32:31.630 --> 03:32:36.840
The knuckles as well begin to exude a superheated
mineral substance similar to molten lava.

03:32:36.840 --> 03:32:41.920
Uka-yag exclusively beats paper and appears
to lose to everything else.

03:32:41.920 --> 03:32:44.399
Many of the gestures on record have no known
name,

03:32:44.399 --> 03:32:50.620
but their function within SCP-4633’s altered
rules is clear from the context in which they

03:32:50.620 --> 03:32:51.870
were used.

03:32:51.870 --> 03:32:56.050
Such is the case with one anomalous gesture,
which caused most of the player’s hand to

03:32:56.050 --> 03:32:59.439
withdraw into a siphon-like opening at their
wrist

03:32:59.439 --> 03:33:04.170
before the very same orifice shot a stream
of ink into the opposing player’s eyes.

03:33:04.170 --> 03:33:08.040
While there was no lasting harm done to the
opponent by this gesture,

03:33:08.040 --> 03:33:12.439
the ink did cause temporary blindness,
which persisted until they were defeated by

03:33:12.439 --> 03:33:15.410
way of their rock losing to paper.

03:33:15.410 --> 03:33:20.210
Another unknown gesture has been seen beating
both iz-urg-av and uka-yag.

03:33:20.210 --> 03:33:24.590
This gesture makes the player’s hand resemble
the crest and gas-filled body of a pacific

03:33:24.590 --> 03:33:25.930
man-o-war.

03:33:25.930 --> 03:33:30.229
These examples are only scraping the surface
of the seemingly endless possibilities that

03:33:30.229 --> 03:33:33.210
SCP-4633 has to offer.

03:33:33.210 --> 03:33:37.970
As you can see, the anomalous hand gestures
can be just as dangerous within the game itself

03:33:37.970 --> 03:33:40.890
as the permanent changes they invoke in the
user.

03:33:40.890 --> 03:33:45.060
While the Foundation has done its best to
contain all information surrounding SCP-4633,

03:33:45.060 --> 03:33:52.380
there have been clear efforts by several unknown
groups to push the game into continued usage.

03:33:52.380 --> 03:33:58.149
Over the past three decades, instances of
SCP-4633 have seen increasing popularity in

03:33:58.149 --> 03:34:00.520
the world of high-stakes gambling.

03:34:00.520 --> 03:34:04.449
Perhaps it is because of the inherent thrill
of watching two opponents trying to strategically

03:34:04.449 --> 03:34:08.200
outwit each other
with a countless number of non-standard gestures,

03:34:08.200 --> 03:34:14.490
but more likely, it is part of the anomalous
effects of SCP-4633 that the game would appeal

03:34:14.490 --> 03:34:19.410
to those desperate to risk everything
on a slim chance of victory.

03:34:19.410 --> 03:34:24.949
This neatly brings us to the SCP-4633-related
incident which occurred aboard the private

03:34:24.949 --> 03:34:28.670
ocean liner known as the SS Fateful Emma.

03:34:28.670 --> 03:34:33.260
Before the incident, the Fateful Emma would
sail into international waters twice a year.

03:34:33.260 --> 03:34:36.540
Each time it would bring along a new group
of passengers,

03:34:36.540 --> 03:34:40.670
seemingly selected from the underprivileged
and downcast sector of society.

03:34:40.670 --> 03:34:45.840
A great many of these passengers were convicts
with repeat violent offenses chosen from supermax

03:34:45.840 --> 03:34:47.780
facilities the world over.

03:34:47.780 --> 03:34:52.710
The process by which these inmates were chosen
for a voyage aboard the ship was not dissimilar

03:34:52.710 --> 03:34:56.960
from the methods the Foundation uses to acquire
new Class-D Personnel.

03:34:56.960 --> 03:35:01.290
Naturally, this was how the research team
was able to be tipped off about the Fateful

03:35:01.290 --> 03:35:02.290
Emma.

03:35:02.290 --> 03:35:06.790
It became apparent soon after looking into
the ship’s career that individuals altered

03:35:06.790 --> 03:35:13.090
by the effects of SCP-4633 had been seen departing
from the ship on multiple occasions in remote

03:35:13.090 --> 03:35:14.810
island harbors.

03:35:14.810 --> 03:35:18.500
Most of the time, the individuals would also
be - to the best of their ability with their

03:35:18.500 --> 03:35:22.340
metamorphosed forms
- carrying briefcases filled with hundreds

03:35:22.340 --> 03:35:24.620
of thousands of dollars in cash.

03:35:24.620 --> 03:35:28.680
While witnesses tended to write off the strange
appearances of those affected as being the

03:35:28.680 --> 03:35:33.449
result of deformity or injury
– or in more pronounced instances dismissing

03:35:33.449 --> 03:35:36.489
the encounters as sightings of creatures from
the local mythology

03:35:36.489 --> 03:35:42.500
– it was clear to anyone familiar with SCP-4633
what was truly going on.

03:35:42.500 --> 03:35:49.600
These passengers were being made to play pitched
games of Rock-Paper-Scissors featuring SCP-4633

03:35:49.600 --> 03:35:54.890
for massive sums of wealth, and what was worse:
the grand prize winners were seemingly being

03:35:54.890 --> 03:35:59.569
released back into civilization without the
knowledge of law enforcement.

03:35:59.569 --> 03:36:04.060
Of course, what the Foundation was most concerned
about was the potential for a large-scale

03:36:04.060 --> 03:36:08.920
leak of detailed information involving SCP-4633.

03:36:08.920 --> 03:36:13.449
It seemed that the Foundation’s latest faceless
enemy had revealed itself.

03:36:13.449 --> 03:36:17.199
Whatever wealthy group of individuals was
pulling the strings behind the games aboard

03:36:17.199 --> 03:36:20.840
the SS Fateful Emma,
it was very likely that the ship was also

03:36:20.840 --> 03:36:24.400
well-protected by a private army of trained
mercenaries.

03:36:24.400 --> 03:36:29.189
In a display of swift but necessary initiative,
The Foundation assembled a brand new Mobile

03:36:29.189 --> 03:36:32.660
Task Force to be dispatched onto the ocean
liner:

03:36:32.660 --> 03:36:37.890
Mobile Task Rho-52 with the fitting codename
“Rochambeau.”

03:36:37.890 --> 03:36:40.729
The mobile task force would board the vessel
secretly,

03:36:40.729 --> 03:36:45.689
and use whatever means necessary to prevent
the spread of SCP-4633

03:36:45.689 --> 03:36:50.029
and any evidence of the associated non-standard
hand gestures.

03:36:50.029 --> 03:36:54.210
Before the Fateful Emma’s next voyage, the
Foundation isolated the port where it would

03:36:54.210 --> 03:36:58.420
depart from and made sure that the MTF agents
were in position.

03:36:58.420 --> 03:37:02.310
The operation was co-captained by Agent Dubois
and Agent Zheng,

03:37:02.310 --> 03:37:06.330
both of whom prided themselves on having extraordinary
good luck.

03:37:06.330 --> 03:37:10.680
Agent Dubois would stealthily move the majority
of the agents under his command into the cargo

03:37:10.680 --> 03:37:14.029
hold below deck,
and await Agent Zheng’ signal.

03:37:14.029 --> 03:37:18.890
Agent Zheng himself would assume the name
and identity of one of the criminal passengers,

03:37:18.890 --> 03:37:22.270
who was intercepted before boarding by the
mobile task force.

03:37:22.270 --> 03:37:26.250
The would-be participant was subsequently
detained and brought into the Foundation as

03:37:26.250 --> 03:37:28.250
a Class-D personnel.

03:37:28.250 --> 03:37:31.260
As the SS Fateful Emma began to leave the
harbor,

03:37:31.260 --> 03:37:35.410
Agent Zheng joined the other passengers who
had been gathered together in an enormous

03:37:35.410 --> 03:37:36.410
function hall.

03:37:36.410 --> 03:37:40.319
There was little indication of when the games
would begin, and Zheng made an effort to keep

03:37:40.319 --> 03:37:43.960
a low profile until more information was available.

03:37:43.960 --> 03:37:47.820
Shortly after the ship had officially arrived
in international waters,

03:37:47.820 --> 03:37:52.800
an eccentric man entered the room on a walkway
that overlooked the rest of the passengers.

03:37:52.800 --> 03:37:55.489
He was flanked by a pair of armed bodyguards.

03:37:55.489 --> 03:37:59.800
It was plain from the man’s style of dress
and demeanor that he was both absurdly wealthy

03:37:59.800 --> 03:38:02.050
and completely out of his mind.

03:38:02.050 --> 03:38:06.840
He wore a pure white tuxedo and a scarf that
seemed to have been made from the fur of a

03:38:06.840 --> 03:38:08.500
Siberian tiger.

03:38:08.500 --> 03:38:12.370
A pair of dark shades with sequined rims obscured
his eyes from view,

03:38:12.370 --> 03:38:17.060
but by far the strangest aspect of the rich
man’s was the fact that emerging from the

03:38:17.060 --> 03:38:21.000
wrist of his right arm
was the eyeless head of a moray eel where

03:38:21.000 --> 03:38:22.229
his hand should be.

03:38:22.229 --> 03:38:25.310
The man spoke, addressing the entire room:

03:38:25.310 --> 03:38:29.570
“Welcome, passengers new and old, to the
SS Fateful Emma!

03:38:29.570 --> 03:38:33.229
While you are aboard this ship, your fate
is in your own hands.

03:38:33.229 --> 03:38:37.870
Over the next twenty-four hours, you will
have the rare opportunity to play the greatest

03:38:37.870 --> 03:38:42.170
game of chance that humanity has ever known!

03:38:42.170 --> 03:38:46.609
Generations of people from every continent
and every walk of life have given their all

03:38:46.609 --> 03:38:51.779
to the mastery of rock-paper-scissors-ack-rah-nos-vaf-lyl-tog-char-sth...”

03:38:51.779 --> 03:38:56.029
The man proceeded to speak unusual syllables
for the next minute straight without even

03:38:56.029 --> 03:38:57.670
a pause for breath.

03:38:57.670 --> 03:39:02.870
In his report, Agent Zheng described the man’s
voice as gaining an increasingly loud hum

03:39:02.870 --> 03:39:07.029
as his speech continued and that some of the
syllables didn’t seem to be the product

03:39:07.029 --> 03:39:08.930
of human vocal cords.

03:39:08.930 --> 03:39:13.359
He soon concluded his string of gibberish
and resumed speaking intelligible language:

03:39:13.359 --> 03:39:17.720
“As is customary, we will be utilizing the
ill-gesh-toe-eee rules.

03:39:17.720 --> 03:39:23.830
You will begin by selecting a single opponent
and challenging them to a best two of three

03:39:23.830 --> 03:39:24.830
match.

03:39:24.830 --> 03:39:29.720
This is a single elimination tournament, and
any competitors who cannot otherwise continue

03:39:29.720 --> 03:39:32.470
to engage in matches will also be eliminated.

03:39:32.470 --> 03:39:36.970
By the end of your time here, the luckiest
among you will be named the undisputed champion

03:39:36.970 --> 03:39:43.220
of these games and will be granted unlimited
freedom along with the cash prize!

03:39:43.220 --> 03:39:47.739
The rest of you will have to determine your
own fates, for you all know what awaits you

03:39:47.739 --> 03:39:48.920
below.

03:39:48.920 --> 03:39:55.350
May your hands be ready, your minds be sharp,
and remember… winning is everything!”

03:39:55.350 --> 03:39:59.359
With a flourish of his moray eel hand, the
wealthy man finished his introductions and

03:39:59.359 --> 03:40:01.569
promptly exited the room.

03:40:01.569 --> 03:40:05.220
Agent Zheng looked at the passengers surrounding
him and saw that they were already beginning

03:40:05.220 --> 03:40:07.780
to play rock-paper-scissors with each other.

03:40:07.780 --> 03:40:12.580
A few had already begun using the anomalous
gestures, warping their limbs into hideous

03:40:12.580 --> 03:40:14.750
sub-nautical shapes.

03:40:14.750 --> 03:40:18.529
Agent Zheng gave the signal to Agent Dubois
and the rest of the mobile task force to begin

03:40:18.529 --> 03:40:19.939
the operation.

03:40:19.939 --> 03:40:23.010
Their target was primarily the moray-handed
man,

03:40:23.010 --> 03:40:26.510
as well as any other close associates that
he had on board.

03:40:26.510 --> 03:40:31.710
They would capture him alive and interrogate
him into revealing the mystery behind this

03:40:31.710 --> 03:40:33.609
illegal gambling ring.

03:40:33.609 --> 03:40:37.620
Once the mercenaries were dealt with, Agent
Dubois’ team would commandeer the ocean

03:40:37.620 --> 03:40:43.840
liner and navigate it to SCP Foundation Research
Site 45 to await further orders.

03:40:43.840 --> 03:40:46.899
As for Agent Zheng, he knew what he had to
do.

03:40:46.899 --> 03:40:51.640
To buy time, he would participate in the ensuing
rock-paper-scissors tournament and eliminate

03:40:51.640 --> 03:40:53.750
as many of the other players as possible.

03:40:53.750 --> 03:40:58.020
This, he believed, was the best way to minimize
the number of anomalous gestures that would

03:40:58.020 --> 03:40:59.660
be used aboard the ship.

03:40:59.660 --> 03:41:04.029
Of course, he himself wouldn’t be using
any of the anomalous gestures either - opting

03:41:04.029 --> 03:41:06.840
to limit himself to the standard three.

03:41:06.840 --> 03:41:11.279
This could put him at a severe disadvantage
over the course of the competition, but Agent

03:41:11.279 --> 03:41:13.649
Zheng knew better than to tempt fate.

03:41:13.649 --> 03:41:17.960
Still, being eliminated seemed like something
he may want to avoid.

03:41:17.960 --> 03:41:22.239
Even though the consequences for elimination
were left vague in the strange rich man’s

03:41:22.239 --> 03:41:24.680
speech,
he still didn’t feel that it would be wise

03:41:24.680 --> 03:41:29.449
to put himself in a compromised position while
aboard the Fateful Emma.

03:41:29.449 --> 03:41:34.229
Bracing himself for the worst, Agent Zheng
accepted the challenge of a nearby passenger.

03:41:34.229 --> 03:41:38.989
He was off to a promising start when he threw
a scissors hand against his opponent’s paper.

03:41:38.989 --> 03:41:44.010
Because the matches were best two out of three,
all he needed was to win one of the next two

03:41:44.010 --> 03:41:46.819
matches, and he’d have survived this round.

03:41:46.819 --> 03:41:51.500
Then he saw the devious look on his opponent’s
face, and he knew that this match was about

03:41:51.500 --> 03:41:52.890
to get weird.

03:41:52.890 --> 03:41:57.210
Rather than admit defeat, Agent Zheng prepared
to throw scissors again.

03:41:57.210 --> 03:42:03.050
The two challenged their hands, and just as
Zheng had feared, his opponent threw an anomalous

03:42:03.050 --> 03:42:04.050
gesture.

03:42:04.050 --> 03:42:07.790
Almost instantly, the opponent’s hand took
on a multi-mouthed piscine form

03:42:07.790 --> 03:42:11.470
which began to spit sharp teeth in Agent Zheng’s
direction.

03:42:11.470 --> 03:42:15.489
Fortunately, the agent’s bulletproof vest
was able to withstand the impact of the teeth.

03:42:15.489 --> 03:42:20.409
What was even more fortunate was the fact
that the opponent seemed to be a good sport.

03:42:20.409 --> 03:42:21.810
“Scissors beats Az-rah-vok.

03:42:21.810 --> 03:42:26.409
You win,” said the other passenger before
walking away in search of their next match.

03:42:26.409 --> 03:42:30.540
Curiously, the passenger’s transformed arm
continued to fire teeth across the room at

03:42:30.540 --> 03:42:34.120
random intervals,
occasionally hitting and causing injury to

03:42:34.120 --> 03:42:36.320
one of the other competitors.

03:42:36.320 --> 03:42:40.130
Agent Zheng was grateful that the body armor
he was wearing had been tested to withstand

03:42:40.130 --> 03:42:43.250
rapid fire from SCP-127.

03:42:43.250 --> 03:42:47.279
Compared to the raw power of the living gun,
the teeth launched from the “Az-rah-vok”

03:42:47.279 --> 03:42:49.740
gesture were practically BB rounds.

03:42:49.740 --> 03:42:55.300
He had succeeded in not being eliminated,
both from the game and generally.

03:42:55.300 --> 03:42:59.630
Excited about his win, Zheng sought out another
opponent to test his luck.

03:42:59.630 --> 03:43:03.260
Below deck, Agent Dubois and the rest of the
mobile task force had just finished facing

03:43:03.260 --> 03:43:07.250
off against some of the hired mercenaries
when they entered a room presumed to be the

03:43:07.250 --> 03:43:09.460
liner’s sick bay.

03:43:09.460 --> 03:43:14.550
Inside was a grisly sight: a second rock-paper-scissors
tournament playing out between players

03:43:14.550 --> 03:43:19.870
that had been so thoroughly transformed by
the anomalous gestures of SCP-4633

03:43:19.870 --> 03:43:22.090
that they barely appeared to still be human.

03:43:22.090 --> 03:43:27.210
It was like a scene from a deep sea documentary,
with strange and unknowable creatures vying

03:43:27.210 --> 03:43:30.870
for dominance
- not within a natural food chain or competition

03:43:30.870 --> 03:43:36.640
for resources but within a seemingly never-ending
struggle to win a game of chance.

03:43:36.640 --> 03:43:39.320
It was purgatory.

03:43:39.320 --> 03:43:41.470
Rock-paper-scissors-purgatory.

03:43:41.470 --> 03:43:45.439
Agent Dubois was appalled, but he knew better
than to attempt to stop these creatures from

03:43:45.439 --> 03:43:48.580
doing the one thing keeping them all distracted.

03:43:48.580 --> 03:43:54.290
The moray-handed man was still somewhere in
the ship, and capturing him was far more important.

03:43:54.290 --> 03:43:57.380
The research team would decide what to do
with these former humans once the entire ship

03:43:57.380 --> 03:44:00.120
was secure within the Foundation’s custody.

03:44:00.120 --> 03:44:04.600
However, what Agent Dubois didn’t realize
is that some of these creatures were advancing

03:44:04.600 --> 03:44:05.909
on his team.

03:44:05.909 --> 03:44:09.750
In nightmarish and unearthly voices, they
chanted:

03:44:09.750 --> 03:44:11.800
“Rock… rock… paper…

03:44:11.800 --> 03:44:12.800
scissors…”

03:44:12.800 --> 03:44:17.030
Suddenly, one of the abominations - who resembled
nothing more closely than an enormous mass

03:44:17.030 --> 03:44:20.520
of coral
- produced four arms from within its body

03:44:20.520 --> 03:44:24.040
and began to throw anomalous hand signs toward
the agents.

03:44:24.040 --> 03:44:29.100
One of the arms threw “uka-yag”, which
coated an unsuspecting agent in hot lava,

03:44:29.100 --> 03:44:31.239
causing him to drop to the floor in pain.

03:44:31.239 --> 03:44:35.630
Panicked, Dubois ordered the task force to
eliminate every living thing in the sick bay

03:44:35.630 --> 03:44:39.420
- although not in the sense of their tournament
standing, of course.

03:44:39.420 --> 03:44:42.610
Meanwhile, Agent Zheng was on a win streak
in the function hall.

03:44:42.610 --> 03:44:47.920
Through sheer luck and determination had managed
to avoid elimination while eliminating several

03:44:47.920 --> 03:44:49.630
other passengers himself.

03:44:49.630 --> 03:44:54.590
He began to notice that those who were eliminated
were quietly escorted away by the bouncers,

03:44:54.590 --> 03:44:57.920
seemingly to have their anomalous changes
treated.

03:44:57.920 --> 03:45:01.340
Now it was down to only twelve remaining passengers.

03:45:01.340 --> 03:45:05.210
Agent Zheng knew that it was only a matter
of time before Dubois and his team took control

03:45:05.210 --> 03:45:08.210
of the ship
but feeling reckless, he challenged one of

03:45:08.210 --> 03:45:10.120
the remaining passengers.

03:45:10.120 --> 03:45:14.660
In that moment, something came over him, as
if his determination to see the mission through

03:45:14.660 --> 03:45:17.779
was also compelling him to win at any cost.

03:45:17.779 --> 03:45:23.000
His opponent threw pag-ak-mar,
and out of instinct Zheng threw vah-yan-jek.

03:45:23.000 --> 03:45:27.790
The agent went on to win the entire tournament,
but sadly, he was unable to collect the prize

03:45:27.790 --> 03:45:30.460
money,
as during the skirmish below deck, the man

03:45:30.460 --> 03:45:34.680
with the moray hand had escaped with the small
fortune in a high-speed submersible.

03:45:34.680 --> 03:45:39.840
His ultimate goals and the scope of the shadowy
group he represented would remain unanswered

03:45:39.840 --> 03:45:40.890
for the time being.

03:45:40.890 --> 03:45:44.620
When the SS Fateful Emma finally arrived at
Site 45,

03:45:44.620 --> 03:45:50.540
Agent Zheng came to his senses and realized
that he had underestimated the cognitive side-effects

03:45:50.540 --> 03:45:53.300
of SCP-4633.

03:45:53.300 --> 03:45:57.930
Due to the immediately recognizable anomalous
state of both of his arms, Agent Zheng was

03:45:57.930 --> 03:46:00.610
later contained on a site with minimal security.

03:46:00.610 --> 03:46:05.489
It is believed that researchers are still
searching for a method to reverse the effects.

03:46:05.489 --> 03:46:09.140
It just goes to show that winning isn’t
everything.

03:46:09.140 --> 03:46:16.899
The thin, pale humanoid anomaly had just found
itself… in Minecraft, of all places.

03:46:16.899 --> 03:46:21.490
The all too familiar wail of emergency alarms
rang out through the corridors of the Foundation

03:46:21.490 --> 03:46:22.490
facility.

03:46:22.490 --> 03:46:25.330
It had happened again, another containment
breach.

03:46:25.330 --> 03:46:29.760
Of course, the on-site personnel were more
than familiar with the protocols for a situation

03:46:29.760 --> 03:46:35.159
like this, so well-versed in what to do during
an emergency that it had become second nature.

03:46:35.159 --> 03:46:39.700
Whether they were research staff evacuating
for their own safety, or security teams moving

03:46:39.700 --> 03:46:44.390
in to recapture the escaped anomaly and get
the situation under control, everyone was

03:46:44.390 --> 03:46:48.399
moving like they had been programmed, muscle
memory taking over.

03:46:48.399 --> 03:46:54.630
The loose anomaly was none other than SCP-096,
all Foundation personnel were alerted and

03:46:54.630 --> 03:46:57.590
reminded not to look directly at the creature.

03:46:57.590 --> 03:47:02.150
There was just one problem: they couldn’t
look even if they’d ignored their order

03:47:02.150 --> 03:47:03.600
to try.

03:47:03.600 --> 03:47:06.940
Nobody could… because the Shy Guy was nowhere
to be found.

03:47:06.940 --> 03:47:09.950
Well, nowhere in the real world, at least.

03:47:09.950 --> 03:47:14.021
This whole situation – bizarre even by the
Foundation’s standards – had all started

03:47:14.021 --> 03:47:18.300
with an experiment involving a member of the
disposable D Class Personnel.

03:47:18.300 --> 03:47:23.490
Yet another dangerous convict severing one
of several life sentences, now being used

03:47:23.490 --> 03:47:29.040
as an expendable human lab rat for the Foundation’s
often sinister purposes.

03:47:29.040 --> 03:47:33.430
The experiment in question was an attempt
to further test the limitations of SCP-096’s

03:47:33.430 --> 03:47:34.430
abilities.

03:47:34.430 --> 03:47:40.370
By now, the Foundation had done extensive
research into the creature known as the Shy

03:47:40.370 --> 03:47:41.370
Guy.

03:47:41.370 --> 03:47:45.590
They knew its particular brand of anomalous
qualities were triggered when anyone looked

03:47:45.590 --> 03:47:47.230
directly at it.

03:47:47.230 --> 03:47:53.790
Being observed would instantaneously send
SCP-096 into an uncontrollable rage state,

03:47:53.790 --> 03:47:58.040
causing it to pursue its observer until they
were no longer standing.

03:47:58.040 --> 03:48:03.489
SCP-096 was known to be able to travel at
speeds so relentless it was pretty much possible

03:48:03.489 --> 03:48:04.680
to stop.

03:48:04.680 --> 03:48:09.390
One moment it was sobbing and screeching in
its cell having just been observed, and minutes

03:48:09.390 --> 03:48:15.170
later, the wide-jawed, long-limbed monster
was standing behind whoever had seen it, making

03:48:15.170 --> 03:48:19.400
its screaming face the image its victim took
to the grave with them.

03:48:19.400 --> 03:48:24.300
Foundation researchers knew that SCP-096’s
rage state could be triggered by as little

03:48:24.300 --> 03:48:27.790
as someone seeing the slightest hint of a
creature.

03:48:27.790 --> 03:48:32.040
Even if the Shy Guy had been captured as a
tiny speck in the background of a photograph,

03:48:32.040 --> 03:48:36.630
it would come after anyone whose eyes so much
as glanced over that speck.

03:48:36.630 --> 03:48:43.530
It also didn’t matter how far away the observer
was – SCP-096 couldn’t be outrun, it could

03:48:43.530 --> 03:48:45.960
track down anyone wherever they were in the
world.

03:48:45.960 --> 03:48:51.180
But what if a person who had observed the
Shy Guy was no longer in the world…

03:48:51.180 --> 03:48:55.760
The experiment had taken a lot of trial and
error – after all, digitizing a human being

03:48:55.760 --> 03:48:59.770
and transforming their entire consciousness
into code was no easy task.

03:48:59.770 --> 03:49:04.659
But the Foundation wasn’t above breaking
more than a few eggs to make an omelette,

03:49:04.659 --> 03:49:08.270
especially when those eggs were the brains
of a few D Class Personnel.

03:49:08.270 --> 03:49:13.920
Actually, the first test subjects turned out
more scrambled or fried, certainly overdone,

03:49:13.920 --> 03:49:16.460
given the amount of smoke coming from their
ears.

03:49:16.460 --> 03:49:21.250
But eventually, the research team had perfected
the device they had dubbed the ‘Neural Harness’,

03:49:21.250 --> 03:49:26.029
a headset that could translate a person’s
mind into pure digital code.

03:49:26.029 --> 03:49:30.180
Their consciousness would be extracted from
their body, leaving their physical form an

03:49:30.180 --> 03:49:35.040
empty shell while a digital copy of them was
able to traverse the local on-site computer

03:49:35.040 --> 03:49:36.040
network.

03:49:36.040 --> 03:49:40.560
Of course, the Foundation wasn’t about to
let a digitized D Class run amok with their

03:49:40.560 --> 03:49:45.521
systems, so their coding experts wrote a number
of specialized parameters into their successful

03:49:45.521 --> 03:49:46.521
test subject.

03:49:46.521 --> 03:49:51.460
Now, the digital D Class could be programmed
to follow the Foundation’s directive.

03:49:51.460 --> 03:49:56.210
From his perspective, it was like he was floating
through cyberspace, towards the system the

03:49:56.210 --> 03:50:02.790
Foundation had directed him too: a surveillance
camera inside SCP-096’s cell that usually

03:50:02.790 --> 03:50:04.470
remained inactive.

03:50:04.470 --> 03:50:05.710
But not today.

03:50:05.710 --> 03:50:10.140
The camera blinked to life as soon as the
D Class’s code-based consciousness reached

03:50:10.140 --> 03:50:16.109
it, and through it, he was able to observe
SCP-096, sitting against the far wall of its

03:50:16.109 --> 03:50:20.120
containment chamber, gently whimpering and
rocking back and forth.

03:50:20.120 --> 03:50:25.580
That is, until the creature felt that familiar
sensation of being watched.

03:50:25.580 --> 03:50:31.220
It stood up, enraged, wailing uncontrollably
as the D Class watched from within the camera.

03:50:31.220 --> 03:50:36.700
Then, a split second later, SCP-096 appeared
to blink out of existence.

03:50:36.700 --> 03:50:41.930
It hadn’t worked, the experiment had failed,
and the digital D Class began to panic.

03:50:41.930 --> 03:50:45.660
Before the Foundation’s coders could stop
him, he frantically scoured the local network

03:50:45.660 --> 03:50:49.880
for anything, some kind of escape route back
into the real world.

03:50:49.880 --> 03:50:53.859
Maybe, he thought, if he could get back into
his body, it wouldn’t count?

03:50:53.859 --> 03:50:58.290
Technically, his digital self was separate
from his physical form, so he might get out

03:50:58.290 --> 03:51:02.010
of SCP-096’s clutches on a technicality.

03:51:02.010 --> 03:51:06.540
But it was impossible, his body was already
burned out by the Neural Harness, there was

03:51:06.540 --> 03:51:09.550
no way for the D Class to transfer himself
back.

03:51:09.550 --> 03:51:12.130
There was only one option left: hide.

03:51:12.130 --> 03:51:17.180
And, as it turned out, someone had forgotten
to uninstall a certain building game from

03:51:17.180 --> 03:51:20.460
a computer in one of the Foundation doctor’s
offices.

03:51:20.460 --> 03:51:25.420
The D Class’s digitized mind zapped him
into Minecraft; from his point of view, it

03:51:25.420 --> 03:51:27.979
was just like he existed within the game itself.

03:51:27.979 --> 03:51:32.510
He did a computerized equivalent of breathing
a sigh of relief, only to take back all those

03:51:32.510 --> 03:51:38.109
ones and zeros in a horrified gasp as he noticed
a tall, wiry-framed figure before him.

03:51:38.109 --> 03:51:44.380
It hadn’t worked, SCP-096 had still managed
to follow him into Minecraft.

03:51:44.380 --> 03:51:48.600
Even in a world of computer code, the Shy
Guy was able to dispatch its digital observer

03:51:48.600 --> 03:51:54.149
quite easily, and what had once been a living
human mind in the form of binary code was

03:51:54.149 --> 03:51:55.239
quickly deleted.

03:51:55.239 --> 03:51:59.830
But that’s when SCP-096 started to take
notice of its surroundings.

03:51:59.830 --> 03:52:04.250
With the most recent person to see it gone,
it had a chance to actually take in where

03:52:04.250 --> 03:52:05.350
it was.

03:52:05.350 --> 03:52:10.120
The bright rays of warm light coming down
from a cube-shaped sun reveal a welcoming

03:52:10.120 --> 03:52:14.770
landscape all around, constructed entirely
from cubes.

03:52:14.770 --> 03:52:20.920
It fascinated SCP-096, instilling a kind of
childlike wonder in the Shy Guy the likes

03:52:20.920 --> 03:52:23.170
of which it hadn’t experienced since…

03:52:23.170 --> 03:52:25.640
well, since it couldn’t remember when.

03:52:25.640 --> 03:52:31.540
Rather than leaving, instantly reappearing
in its containment chamber, SCP-096 made the

03:52:31.540 --> 03:52:33.979
conscious decision to start exploring.

03:52:33.979 --> 03:52:39.140
There was a mountainous biome surrounding
SCP-096, stony spires reaching up towards

03:52:39.140 --> 03:52:43.760
a clear sky, with a waterfall cascading its
way down the nearest of them.

03:52:43.760 --> 03:52:47.680
At the foot of one of the mountains was the
opening to a cave, which the Shy Guy found

03:52:47.680 --> 03:52:49.390
itself wandering towards.

03:52:49.390 --> 03:52:53.490
The light from the sun, while a pleasant and
welcome change to the surroundings it was

03:52:53.490 --> 03:52:57.960
used to, was a little harsh on SCP-096’s
sunken eyes.

03:52:57.960 --> 03:53:03.199
So, instead it began to gravitate towards
the shadowy confines of the caves, not unlike

03:53:03.199 --> 03:53:07.650
the usual darkness of its containment chamber
back at the Foundation.

03:53:07.650 --> 03:53:13.550
Traversing through long, winding corridors
of stone, SCP-096 found itself having to bend

03:53:13.550 --> 03:53:18.399
down, to avoid hitting its head on the cave’s
low ceiling – obviously, this formation

03:53:18.399 --> 03:53:22.390
of rocky blocks hadn’t formed with someone
of the Shy Guy’s height in mind.

03:53:22.390 --> 03:53:26.680
There was a low, warm glow coming from the
farthest side of the tunnel, and the thin

03:53:26.680 --> 03:53:32.140
creature made its way closer and closer, despite
the stone walls around it feeling like a tighter

03:53:32.140 --> 03:53:34.540
confinement than its cell.

03:53:34.540 --> 03:53:39.489
Approaching the source of the light, SCP-096
was relieved that the cave opened out into

03:53:39.489 --> 03:53:44.510
a more spacious area, the cramped tunnel giving
way to an expansive chamber that lay within

03:53:44.510 --> 03:53:46.420
the heart of the mountain.

03:53:46.420 --> 03:53:51.739
The warm, orangey glow had been coming from
a flowing stream of boiling hot lava, that

03:53:51.739 --> 03:53:56.189
seemed to trickle down from somewhere above,
creating a deadly, fiery pool directly in

03:53:56.189 --> 03:53:57.409
the Shy Guy’s path.

03:53:57.409 --> 03:54:03.220
But just before it could turn back and leave
the cave through the way he came, SCP-096

03:54:03.220 --> 03:54:07.410
sensed that familiar feeling of something
observing him.

03:54:07.410 --> 03:54:11.281
Across the lava pool, on the other side, was
a creature comprised of a green cube for a

03:54:11.281 --> 03:54:16.239
head, with a long, armless cuboid body, and
four cubed feet.

03:54:16.239 --> 03:54:20.220
If it had been present in the real world,
it might have been made out of leaves, like

03:54:20.220 --> 03:54:21.489
a living shrub.

03:54:21.489 --> 03:54:26.020
It was staring directly at the Shy Guy from
across the lava, only for this strange newcomer

03:54:26.020 --> 03:54:28.040
to disappear a moment later.

03:54:28.040 --> 03:54:33.090
The Creeper hissed, before it realized that
SCP-096 had leaped up behind it to land a

03:54:33.090 --> 03:54:34.130
fatal blow.

03:54:34.130 --> 03:54:40.060
But little did the infamously anxious anomaly
realize the sentient shrub wasn’t done.

03:54:40.060 --> 03:54:46.149
There was a colossal boom that caused SCP-096
to stagger backwards, whimpering at the sudden

03:54:46.149 --> 03:54:49.600
loud explosion and the pain that had accompanied
it.

03:54:49.600 --> 03:54:55.050
The Creeper had blown itself to pieces, but
in doing so, had left a lasting injury, that

03:54:55.050 --> 03:55:00.830
caused SCP-096 considerable discomfort as
it began limping towards the cave that led

03:55:00.830 --> 03:55:03.609
out the opposite side of the mountain.

03:55:03.609 --> 03:55:07.870
Despite its injury, the Shy Guy was still
able to move with relative ease, and the further

03:55:07.870 --> 03:55:11.120
it went, the more it seemed to gradually heal.

03:55:11.120 --> 03:55:15.670
However, it was as it stepped out once more
into the sunlight that the creature realized

03:55:15.670 --> 03:55:17.770
just how hungry it was.

03:55:17.770 --> 03:55:22.040
Back in the real world, it hadn’t eaten
for several hours, and surprisingly – even

03:55:22.040 --> 03:55:26.370
while accidentally stuck in digitized form
– the hunger mechanics of Minecraft were

03:55:26.370 --> 03:55:30.020
having an effect on SCP-096.

03:55:30.020 --> 03:55:34.300
The Shy Guy started searching around for anything
that could prove to satiate its hunger.

03:55:34.300 --> 03:55:40.120
There was a grove of trees not far, so SCP-096
lumbered closer to take a closer look.

03:55:40.120 --> 03:55:44.090
It couldn’t be certain, but it felt there
was at least a slim chance that there were

03:55:44.090 --> 03:55:46.560
apples growing among the leaves.

03:55:46.560 --> 03:55:51.319
But even at the anomaly’s own elongated
height, it couldn’t simply reach up to pick

03:55:51.319 --> 03:55:52.319
them.

03:55:52.319 --> 03:55:55.899
However, it was while swiping its arms up
at the tree that the Shy Guy swung its fist

03:55:55.899 --> 03:55:57.620
and knocked the tree trunk.

03:55:57.620 --> 03:56:02.500
To the creature’s surprise, it was able
to chip away at the wood with its hands, punching

03:56:02.500 --> 03:56:06.500
at the tree until the trunk had been reduced
to a collection of wooden blocks that the

03:56:06.500 --> 03:56:08.810
Shy Guy was able to collect.

03:56:08.810 --> 03:56:13.600
And sure enough, some red apples dropped from
the leaves above, enough to quell the hunger

03:56:13.600 --> 03:56:17.820
that SCP-096 was feeling, at least for the
time being.

03:56:17.820 --> 03:56:20.069
Nearby, something was moving through the trees.

03:56:20.069 --> 03:56:26.220
It was yet to lock eyes on SCP-096, but the
second it did, the mob would surely have sealed

03:56:26.220 --> 03:56:27.310
its own fate.

03:56:27.310 --> 03:56:31.390
As it moved around, minding its own business,
the tall figure overheard the sounds of the

03:56:31.390 --> 03:56:36.290
Shy Guy eating, and its attention was drawn
to where the creature was standing, enjoying

03:56:36.290 --> 03:56:37.520
an apple.

03:56:37.520 --> 03:56:41.920
Sensing it had been seen, SCP-096 turned and
locked eyes with the entity.

03:56:41.920 --> 03:56:47.340
It was perhaps the only other being in Minecraft
that was a similar height and stature to the

03:56:47.340 --> 03:56:51.370
anomaly itself – in fact, the pair were
strikingly similar.

03:56:51.370 --> 03:56:56.680
Before SCP-096 could carry out its usual method
of attack, the Enderman suddenly teleported

03:56:56.680 --> 03:56:57.729
out of view.

03:56:57.729 --> 03:57:02.739
But it was too late now, both it and the Shy
Guy had looked at the other’s face; both

03:57:02.739 --> 03:57:07.859
had been provoked, and were compelled to react
in exactly the same way.

03:57:07.859 --> 03:57:13.290
Just as SCP-096 entered its rage state, the
Enderman too opened its mouth and began to

03:57:13.290 --> 03:57:18.720
shake, making loud, lengthy sounds not dissimilar
to the Shy Guy’s own angered screams.

03:57:18.720 --> 03:57:22.140
But something profoundly strange happened
next.

03:57:22.140 --> 03:57:27.380
SCP-096 and the Enderman kept attempting to
pursue one another, appearing in a new location

03:57:27.380 --> 03:57:31.630
within the Minecraft world, neither one getting
close enough to the other.

03:57:31.630 --> 03:57:35.830
They’d teleport, only for the other to teleport
away the moment they saw each other, each

03:57:35.830 --> 03:57:39.810
trying to catch one another but always staying
one step ahead or behind.

03:57:39.810 --> 03:57:44.480
This rapid chase almost became a strange,
ongoing sort of teleporting dance between

03:57:44.480 --> 03:57:46.840
SCP-096 and the Enderman.

03:57:46.840 --> 03:57:51.569
At first, the Shy Guy had been compelled to
attack the Enderman, like it was with any

03:57:51.569 --> 03:57:53.180
who looked directly at it.

03:57:53.180 --> 03:57:57.250
But as they appeared and disappeared from
place to place, the creature started to feel

03:57:57.250 --> 03:58:00.060
an odd kinship with this shadowy fellow.

03:58:00.060 --> 03:58:05.300
It was so used to being frightened and alone,
to its overwhelming feelings of insecurity

03:58:05.300 --> 03:58:10.359
and self-loathing, that the simplicity of
just traversing the open world – with a

03:58:10.359 --> 03:58:15.970
creature very alike itself in many ways – it
had all come as a welcome change.

03:58:15.970 --> 03:58:21.620
SCP-096 found it therapeutic even, and before
long, was following the Enderman not out of

03:58:21.620 --> 03:58:27.790
any intention to do it harm, but just to feel,
for a while, like it had a friend.

03:58:27.790 --> 03:58:34.069
Just a little bit longer, SCP-096 thought,
welcoming the enjoyment it felt playing this

03:58:34.069 --> 03:58:43.249
game of Minecraft.

